{
  "raw": {
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Assistant-Manager--Sound-Office_JR109214-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Manager, Sound Office",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Parsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks an Assistant Manager of the Sound Office for the Making Center. The Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines, and support experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The Assistant Manager should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. This is a fast-paced, dynamic environment that runs year-round. The Assistant Manager will interface with students, faculty, and staff to support multiple processes and equipment resources for all users in a range of making practices including sound recording studios, equipment resources, and media production studios. This is an exempt administrative position that reports to the Assistant Director of Sound & Filmmaking Operations .   CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Maintain a clean and safe working environment and uphold a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Oversee daily workings of the Sound Office, a major resource open to New School students supporting sound, video, and related equipment available for on- and off-campus check-out.  Management of full-time technicians and student workers, including interviewing, hiring, training, scheduling, tracking accountability, task assignment, scheduling/attending team and one-on-one meetings, and coordination and/or providing emergency coverage for unexpected absences.  Manage a fixed budget, including tracking, planning, and prioritization of expenses related to inventories, schedules, projects, and repairs. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Facility improvement projects, shows and installations, and special initiatives as needed. Hardware/software purchase recommendations as they pertain to the curriculum. Maintaining policies and systems for checkout, satellite store, scheduling, record keeping, and inventories, in addition to generating macro and micro levels of usage data and statistics - evaluating and updating these as needed. Organize calendars and communications for facilities, including creation of website updates, shop signage, safety orientations, technical/instructional documentation for labs/equipment, workshops, class demos, deliveries, special events, and modified holiday hours. Work across systems in the Making Center, connecting separate areas in order to streamline efficiency and communication. Facilitate equipment repair and maintenance, including in-house repairs and coordination with external vendors to manage repair processes.  Adapt to changes in the work environment; adjust approach and prioritize competing demands as necessary. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment with productive and positive workplace relationships.  Stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by supervisor. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor’s Degree and 3-5 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that may include nights and weekends, in addition to scheduling shifts with semester changes. Completion of university required staff trainings and necessary COF's for all Making Center Facilities within the first 6 months of employment. Advanced knowledge of a variety of sound practices; be familiar with equipment and software related to current sound design practices - including: studio-based and experimental processes, digital input/output, and industry-standard software applications.  Experience maintaining, repairing, and troubleshooting equipment/tools. Full technical knowledge of sound-based analog and digital technologies, including but not limited to monitors, microphones, control panels, preamps, mixers, media players, Mac mini(s), tablets, and other smart devices. Understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment, with a working knowledge of large-scale hardware and software deployment, network systems, and color calibration/profiling. Familiarity with Adobe Creative Cloud, Pro Tools, Avid, and Google Docs. Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Proven track record of working well with people from diverse cultural backgrounds, with a strong ability to support students working in an interdisciplinary manner from concept to fabrication. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Knowledge of Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems a plus.  Knowledge of large-scale hardware and software deployments; Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems a plus.  WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee.#LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $64,350 - $69,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness  Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/DJ-Skills---Styles---Student-Assistant_JR109473-1": {
      "title": "DJ Skills & Styles - Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nTo be an assistant for DJ Skills & Styles student workers will need to assist with the setup and return of all dj coffins and speakers at the start of the first section of class and end of the last section of class. The attributes of a successful candidate are someone who is on time to work and somewhat knowledgeable about the ins and outs of the dj equipment. A successful candidate will impact the university in a positive way by showing professionalism to their fellow classmates in the form of efficiency and conduct. RESPONSIBILITIES A Student assistant in DJ Skills & Styles will be required to do the following ● Remove dj coffins and speakers from their lockers and set them up on the classroom tables ● Plug the power from the classroom tables to the wall outlets ● Check the gear to ensure it is ready for class ● Report irregularities and damage of the dj gear to the professor for repair and/or replacement ● Return dj coffins and speakers to their lockers ● Unplug the power cables for the classroom tables and stow them out of the way of foot traffic MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS To be a student assistant an employee would need the following qualifications ● Currently enrolled as a student at The New School ● Ability to lift dj coffins from lockers to classroom tables and back to lockers ● Understand basic signal routing to provide power to the dj stations and speakers PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Knowledge of the dj gear and software (Serato) is encouraged though not a strict requirement WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17.00-$19.00 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The DJ Skills & Styles Student Assistant supports the setup and breakdown of DJ coffins, speakers, and related equipment before and after class sessions. Responsibilities include connecting power, checking equipment, reporting damage or issues to the professor, and safely returning and storing all equipment. Candidates must be currently enrolled students who can safely lift the equipment and understand basic signal routing; familiarity with DJ equipment and Serato is preferred. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/G---80-Fifth-Avenue/Wellness-and-Health-Promotion-Peer-Health-Advocate--OCSE-_JR109383": {
      "title": "Wellness and Health Promotion Peer Health Advocate (OCSE)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nResponsibilities Peer Health Advocates (PHAs) are student health ambassadors who plan and implement health promotion and education for their peers and the general university community.  Specific duties and responsibilities include: Interface with University Housing and Residential Education, supporting Resident Advisors (RAs) with health promotion & education programming.  Help create and curate messaging around healthier living, harm reduction, mental health awareness, and sex education (among other topics).  Conduct and promote events throughout the university, which will include workshops and training, tabling and flyering across the university. Some of the events may be in the evening and weekends. Provide content for and help promote our social media. Train as a Peer Health Advocate (PHA). There is a mandatory PHA training retreat during the Spring 2027 semester. Commit to at least 2 hours/week of administrative office duties and perform other assignments as needed that support Student Health Services.  Minimum Qualifications You should have an interest in health and well-being.  We seek candidates with a strong work ethic, the ability to take initiative, work independently, communicate clearly and meet deadlines. You must be organized and able to interact with students, staff, and faculty of diverse backgrounds.  Familiarity with Google docs and social media platforms.  Ability to adhere to University COVID-19 Policy. Work on site at Student Health Services, which is a requirement of this position, requires use of a surgical grade mask at all times. Preferred  Qualifications We prefer students with experience in health promotion, mental health Initiatives, and previous experience in coordinating and presenting workshops.   Work Mode Hybrid/Split -This position requires a regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus and also works some of the time remotely. This will be coordinated with the supervisor. #LI-DNI   This position requires both work on site at Student Health Services or at other locations on the university campus and independent work that can be done remotely. The final schedule will be determined with the supervisor and could be variable week by week, depending on the programs and services being offered. Hourly Rate: $21/hr Special Instructions Interested applicants should email a cover letter, résumé and available days & times to wellness@newschool.edu. For more information, please contact Tamara Oyola-Santiago, Director, Public Health Services, 646.909.1252. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Student Health Services’ Wellness and Health Promotion (SHS/WHP) is seeking a Peer Health Advocate , who will support health and wellness projects and initiatives for students, including becoming health ambassadors who plan and implement health promotion and education. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/G---80-Fifth-Avenue/Student-Assistant-Specialist--Mindfulness-Practitioner--OCSE_JR109382": {
      "title": "Student Assistant Specialist (Mindfulness Practitioner) OCSE",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nRESPONSIBILITIES Meet individually with clients to teach mindfulness skills. Participate in monthly community outreach within the New School Attend weekly team supervision with a clinical therapist and the Mindfulness team directors on Wednesdays, 12pm-1pm.  Participate in both individual and group-based mindfulness 2 hour training from 1:15pm-3:15pm on the following Wednesdays – September 9th, 16th, 23rd and September 30th – prior to facilitating mindfulness sessions independently.    MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Incoming first-year Psychology MA student at The New School Interest in mindfulness Interest in community outreach Strong interpersonal skills Personal mindfulness or meditation practice preferred Interest in Clinical Psychology PhD at the New School preferred    WORK MODE Work-mode can be 100% remote, hybrid, or 100% in person #LI-DNI   Hourly Rate: $20.00-21.00 per hour SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS *  If you would like to apply, please email your CV and cover letter to RobinT@newschool.edu , bautk225@newschool.edu , and segup890@newschool.edu by Monday, August 10, 2026 at 11:59PM EST.  We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The New School Counseling Services is seeking applications from students for an exciting opportunity to be part of the Mindfulness Initiative with a five hours per week, pa id position. The Student Health Services Mindfulness Team works to assist Counseling Services and Wellness and Health Promotion to offer support and mindfulness education to New School students. The Mindfulness Team provides 30-minute one-on-one mindfulness sessions an d participation in monthly community outreach within the New School. As part of this team, you will receive training and ongoing support in teaching mindfulness techniques and facilitating mindfulness practice and will gain experience working with clients in a clinical and community setting. Mindfulness Counselors report to PhD Psychology student co-supervisors and Senior Associate Provost for Student Health Services. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist---Academic-Science-Labs_JR109477": {
      "title": "Student Assistant Specialist - Academic Science Labs",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nThe University Academic Science Labs were designed to serve all students and thus we not only offer lab based courses with no prerequisites we offer employment to students interested in gaining more skills and supporting their peers in their intellectual and creative growth related to STEM and STEAMD learning. This is in line with the Student Affairs mission of promoting empowered change makers. We are seeking academically strong students who have. completed the Biodesign Lab course spring of 2026 and/or have interest in staying current in this field and gaining more skills to support their community and professional practice. Skills in communication design and with a strong secondary STEM training makes for a strong candidate, as well as someone who can take directive, and has exemplary follow through. RESPONSIBILITIES ● Train for the role early in the semester – 10 hours in September ● Organize lab supplies and assist in the curation of the teaching collection of microbialspecimens as needed 4-6 times per semester ● Clean and maintain the lab environment weekly ● Sterilize tools, materials and lab benches weekly ● Create and distribute promotional material related to activities for residents in Kerrey Hall related to sustainability, science fiction, Bioart, Biodesign ● Maintaining the growth and conditions of living organisms during long-term lab activities ● Prepare materials and set up and break down of lab activities before and after class ● Assist in teaching lab activity ● Update and create visuals for lab protocols and curricular resources in the first third of the semester (remotely)  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS ● STEM background specifically basic math and biology ● Strong communication skills, organized work flow, and exemplary follow through PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS ● Completion of a prior lab course WORK MODE ● Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $21 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Lab Assistant Specialist will support student learning by organizing and tracking materials needed for the lab to operate, and developing visual materials and content for lab protocols specific to those with little knowledge in STEM. The Specialist will also edit and improve existing materials, support the growth of living organisms, prepare and break down for lab activities and gain skills in growth media preparation and sterile environments. These activities will result in the delivery of stellar lab learning experiences to students registered in classes and for those that are one -off workshops serving the community at large and residents in Kerrey Hall under the supervision of the University Academic Science Labs Director and Faculty in Residence Katayoun Chamany. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Research-Assistant---Book-Research---Development--and-Promotional-Assistant_JR109401": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - Book Research & Development, and Promotional Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nThe Research Assistant will work with Jessica Walker to support ongoing promotion of her recently released book as well as develop research and production for a new graphic novel project and support with event facilitation around the theme of children’s book publishing.   Duties and Responsibilities: Meet bi-weekly with faculty to develop research plan Identify primary and secondary resources that can inform the project’s written and visual content Assemble research findings into a shared repository which may include Google Drive and Miro Document all works cited for written and visual resources Support with promotion and launch events for project being released in November 2025 Support with social media strategic plan Qualifications – Education, Experience, and Skills Experience conducting research for visual and/or narrative projects Ability to work independently Familiarity with novels and graphic novels for young adults Experience with creating social media content for creative projects WORK MODE: Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $31.37/hour This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce Responsibilities: The Research Assistant will work with Jessica Walker to support ongoing promotion of her recently released book as well as develop research and production for a new graphic novel project and support with event facilitation around the theme of children’s book publishing. Some tasks include developing a research plan, identifying sources, organizing findings and digital databases, among others. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/N---66-Fifth-Avenue/Social-Media---Digital-Content-Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109451": {
      "title": "Social Media & Digital Content Student Assistant Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nWe are seeking an enthusiastic and creative student with extensive knowledge of social media, digital content creation, photo & video production, and graphic design. The Social Media & Digital Content Student Assistant Specialist will work in collaboration with the Brand, Marketing, and Communication team in the newly formed college integrating art, design, and the performing arts (encompassing Parsons School of Design, Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama portfolios) to capture, write, edit, and produce photographic and video content for our college-wide communications platforms. Responsibilities Aid in the drafting of weekly newsletters with events and notable items for the college community through the intake and researching of content suggestions and ideas Attend university events to capture photo and video content for social media and University platforms  Utilize a strong, creative, and media-savvy skill set to aid in the production of social media content across college-wide social platforms, including Instagram, Twitter/X, Facebook, and LinkedIn.  Shoot and edit photo and video content for use on social media, web, and program marketing platforms.  Aid in the tracking and management of photo & video release forms Conduct interviews with students, alumni, and faculty for video content. Design graphic assets for social media, print flyers, websites, and newsletters. Write compelling, concise copy that follows marketing best practices and branding guidelines. Monitor social accounts, including tags, mentions, and direct messages, interacting with both the college community as well as broader external audiences (e.g. prospective students and their families, alumni, etc). Proactively research news items, events, and other content ideas for social media to promote and celebrate the Parsons, Mannes, Jazz, & Drama community. Abide by university guidelines outlining the responsible use of AI tools. Qualifications Strong interest and fluency in social media, storytelling, and community building Photography and/or videography experience  Graphic design experience (Photoshop, Illustrator, InDesign, etc.) Knowledge of latest digital trends Copywriting and editing skills Available during office hours, with flexibility to occasionally attend evening events Strong communicator and team player; responsive to feedback Enthusiastic, proactive, and ability to work independently Skilled at executing multiple projects simultaneously with efficient time management and without sacrificing exceptional attention to detail Appreciation for arts, design, student life, and higher education Knowledge of Parsons and Performing Arts programs is a plus Comfortable on camera a plus Background in digital media, marketing, journalism, or communications a plus High level of professional discretion Work Mode : Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17-$21 / hour *Please indicate in your application if you have previously held any student worker positions at The New School.* We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Social Media & Digital Content Student Assistant Specialist will work in collaboration with the Brand, Marketing, and Communication team in the newly formed college integrating art, design, and the performing arts (encompassing Parsons School of Design, Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama portfolios) to capture, write, edit, and produce photographic and video content for our college-wide communications platforms. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Student-Marketing-Model---Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109086": {
      "title": "Student Marketing Model - Student Assistant Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. We are seeking Student Models to participate in Marketing and Communications (M&C) photoshoots and videoshoots which will be used in advertising for recruitment for the Fall 2026 semester. The selected students will collaborate with The New School marketing staff and other selected students to gather useful photography and films to convey marketing stories. The photoshoots are expected to take approximately 5 hours (although some shoots may take longer), typically over the course of 1-2 days. CORE CAPABILITIES AND PRIMARY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Pose for marketing photography / videography Advertise TNS products and services in marketing films. Participate in a photoshoot set up and provide input when requested. QUALIFICATIONS Current New School students in good standing and enrolled for the Spring 2026 semester Responsible and punctual, and able to meet deadlines and take direction WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement.   #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $20 per hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: We are seeking Student Models to participate in Marketing and Communications (M&C) photoshoots and videoshoots which will be used in advertising for recruitment for the Fall 2026 semester. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Wardrobe-Supervisor_JR109456": {
      "title": "Temporary Overhire Wardrobe Supervisor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking a highly qualified Wardrobe Supervisor to join its Performance Operations – Costume Department Staff. The Costume Department is responsible for the creation and delivery of garments as designated and discussed by the Costume Designer, Design Team, and Production Staff. The Wardrobe Supervisor coordinates with designers, shop management, and the production team to guarantee their assigned show meets the overarching theatrical vision while ensuring the safety and support of the students. The Wardrobe Supervisor is a well-organized, caring, and focused individual with experience in managing groups of people with varying experience in theater and opera production. The Wardrobe Supervisor reports to the Costume Shop Manager and works closely with Production Management and Stage Management for show-related tasks. This is an overhire position, with opportunities for work to be scheduled as needed by the Costume Department. The position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. RESPONSIBILITIES Collaborate with designers, creative teams, and production crew Delegate responsibilities to all overhire wardrobe crew members, and supervise overhire workers during shifts in the absence of the Costume Shop Manager Attend Load In and Strike, as well as all technical rehearsals and performances Launder rehearsal and production garments, and manage dry cleaning Cultivate safety and comfort in dressing rooms, quick-change areas, and fitting rooms Uphold professional demeanor in challenging situations, especially where students are present Follow all notes documents, paying close attention to shop management’s instructions  Additional responsibilities as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS 1 year previous Wardrobe Supervision experience 2 years of Theatrical Wardrobe experience Exceptional verbal and written communication skills appropriate in a professional, educational environment. Basic knowledge of stitching by hand, machine, and serger Team-oriented problem solver with strong collaborative skills. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience in an academic environment/working with students or young professionals. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours. #LI-Onsite Nights and weekends will be required. Costume Shop hours are 10:00am-5:00pm Monday through Friday. Schedule adjustments may be made due to external venue time constraints when offsite Hourly Rate: $30.00/hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Assistant-Stage-Manager_JR109457": {
      "title": "Temporary Overhire Assistant Stage Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Concerts & Events Production Department at The New School’s College of Performing Arts is seeking highly qualified Overhire Assistant Stage Managers to join its team. Reporting to the Event Production Manager , the Overhire Assistant Stage Manager is directly responsible for supporting the production of concerts and special events presented by Performing Arts at the New School. The Assistant Stage Manager will support Concerts & Events by facilitating stage moves, backstage operations, and artist entrances during events. They will also coordinate logistics and paperwork with the Stage Manager or Production Manager and assist with soundchecks and technical rehearsals as needed, to remain on schedule and ensure that all events are run smoothly and efficiently.  Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more.   This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Production department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding.   CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Create, edit, manage and distribute show paperwork in collaboration with the Stage Manager/Production Manager Manage onstage and backstage activities including stage moves and artist wrangling Communicate with creative team, cast and crew Be familiar with all stage management responsibilities on a production Supervise stage crew including student workers (as needed) Manage backstage spaces including backstage wings, green room(s) and artist holding Spike furniture and other items Other duties related to the successful execution of the event as assigned Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS One year previous experience in assistant stage management or three years experience as a production assistant Strong analytical skills and ability to develop creative approaches to complex issues Excellent oral and written communication skills Interpersonal skills that include management, teamwork and collaboration, and conflict resolution Time and task management skills, including re-prioritization and an ability to work graciously under strict time constraints Must be able to remain standing for long periods, bend down and/or lift and move up to 50 pounds repeatedly Experienced or able to learn and develop fluency in The New School’s suite of applications including Google Suite, Microsoft Office, WorkDay, Asimut and 25Live. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS   Three years previous experience as assistant stage manager, experience as stage manager Familiarity working on concerts and/or special events Ability to step in and call a show Experience reading stage plots Ability to read music WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite SALARY RANGE $30.00/hour  WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/FWS---Costume-Shop-Assistant--Student-Assistant_JR109461": {
      "title": "FWS - Costume Shop Assistant, Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Performance Operations – Costume Department at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as a Student Costume Shop Assistant . The Student Costume Shop Assistant assists the Costume Shop Manager with stock pulls, maintains the costume stock, and runs the stock pulls for faculty and classroom requests. We’re looking for a well-organized, caring, focused individual with experience in group leadership, costume history, costume production, and general knowledge of the New York City Garment District, to support initiatives within the Costume Department . The Student Costume Shop Assistant reports to the Costume Shop Manager and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in the fields of costume design and technology.  This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards.   RESPONSIBILITIES Provide additional support during productions Attend all shifts in person as scheduled by shop management Assist fellow student workers as needed and overhires Act as a shopper for shop management Assist shop management with measurements Take notes in fittings Act on behalf of shop management in urgent or emergency situations Additional responsibilities as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Recipient of Federal Work Study award Basic stitching skills (hand and/or machine) Familiarity with fabrics and garment silhouettes Familiarity with costume production and costume design PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Current student in a College of Performing Arts or Parsons degree program Experience working on costumes for personal or production means Experience working as an Assistant Costume Designer or Production Assistant WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. Hourly Rate: $ 18/hour Hours per Week: up to 20 hours per week We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, as well as a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. Responsibilities: Provide in-person production support by taking notes during fittings, assisting shop management with measurements, and acting as a shopper for shop needs. Support fellow student workers and overhires, step in on behalf of shop management during urgent situations, and handle additional responsibilities as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Research-Assistant---Indigenous-Practices---Land-Based-Technologies-in-Art---Design-Education_JR109402": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - Indigenous Practices & Land-Based Technologies in Art & Design Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPOSITION PURPOSE: This research assistant position will support the development of curricular-based educational opportunities that prioritize Indigenous material practices, relational making methods, and land-based technologies within art and design education at Parsons School of Design. The role includes assisting with research, curriculum development, archival investigation, material experimentation, and community engagement initiatives that center Indigenous making-based knowledge systems and pedagogies.    This position will provide the student with valuable experience in collaborative research, pedagogical development, and community-centered design practices within a higher education setting. Through direct engagement with Indigenous methodologies, material practices, and interdisciplinary creative research, the student will strengthen their critical thinking, organizational, and professional skills while contributing to more inclusive and socially engaged approaches to art and design education Duties and Responsibilities Research Indigenous material practices, craft traditions, and land-based technologies. Assist with pedagogical development for workshops, educational programming and learning resources. Engage in archival and visual research related to Indigenous fashion, art, and design methodologies. Compile annotated bibliographies, teaching resources, and reference materials. Assist with material experimentation and documentation processes. Coordinate communication and scheduling with faculty, students, artists, and community collaborators. Support event planning, workshop preparation, and public programming initiatives. Maintain organized records of research findings, project timelines, and educational resources.   Qualifications – Education, Experience, and Skills Current graduate or undergraduate student at Parsons School of Design. Interest in Indigenous studies, art and design education, material culture, fashion, or socially engaged practices. Strong research, writing, and organizational skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively. Experience with archival research, curriculum development, or community-based practices is an asset.  WORK MODE: Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $31.37/hour This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: This research assistant position will support the development of curricular-based educational opportunities that prioritize Indigenous material practices, relational making methods, and land-based technologies within art and design education at Parsons School of Design. The role includes assisting with research, curriculum development, archival investigation, material experimentation, and community engagement initiatives that center Indigenous making-based knowledge systems and pedagogies. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Research-Assistant----Traces-of-Motherhood--Initiative-Support-Role_JR109404": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - 'Traces of Motherhood' Initiative Support Role",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Research Assistant will support Assistant Professor Anna Lerner-Zwick in multiple projects related to her research initiative, “Traces of Motherhood.” The assistant will play a key role in designing and working on a new exciting project in the field between design, medicine and function. This position is designed to be collaborative and immersive. The Research Assistant will be considered an integral part of the project, contributing meaningfully to its outcomes. Assistant Professor Lerner-Zwick is committed to providing a rich learning experience, ensuring the assistant gains substantial knowledge, skills, and insight while actively participating in the research process. Outcomes and Goals for this year: One major project for 2026/2027 focuses on a very exciting collaboration between a maternal fetal doctor in NYC and Anna Lerner-Zwick, redesigning and sampling garments for women in maternity care. In addition to that the work will also be to support the making and development of a collection to get ready for a future art exhibition around the theme of motherhood as well as supporting the next conference preparations. Here more information about the research topic : The research explores “traces of motherhood”- the intersection of textile, birth, design, and motherhood. It investigates lived experiences of motherhood and artist-life, examining how art portrays women and birth, and how women reflect on motherhood in their practice. Building on scholarship around design systems that center the female body, birthing people, and women’s rights, this work contributes to the underrepresented field of female experience in art and design, particularly suppressed voices tied to birth and care. Through craft, making, and experimental garments, the project uses textiles and material processes to reflect themes of motherhood, identity, and care.   Duties & Responsibilities: Conduct independently patternmaking and sewing required for the project Conduct in-depth library and archival research, including collecting, organizing, and synthesizing visual and textual materials relevant to the project’s themes. Assist in craft-based practice, including knitwear and other selected mediums, contributing to the development of creative outcomes. Support the curation, preparation, and documentation of physical creative outcomes, including exhibition preparation and installation. Provide organizational and logistical support for workshops, panel discussions, and other project-related events Contribute to the planning and development of publications (e.g., essays, zines, or books) emerging from the project. Document research processes, project activities, and outcomes for dissemination in both academic and creative contexts. Qualifications – Education, Experience, and Skills: The ideal candidate needs to be a Fashion Design Student and have good sewing and patternmaking skills: needs to be comfortable to sew on an industrial machine and be able to comfortably adjust and draft patterns and drape on a form. Needs to be committed to reliably meet at once a week for check ins, and to discuss ongoing research Should have a general interest in craft, and themes at the intersection of art, design and motherhood Strong organizational and time-management skills to support research and project development Proficiency in Adobe Creative Suite Experience in designing, drafting, and evaluating surveys to support qualitative and quantitative research Ability to conduct in-depth research, including gathering and curating relevant visuals, texts, and archival materials around a defined topic WORK MODE: Hybrid/Remote - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $31.37/hour This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Research Assistant will support Assistant Professor Anna Lerner-Zwick in multiple projects related to her research initiative, “Traces of Motherhood.” The assistant will play a key role in designing and working on a new exciting project in the field between design, medicine and function. Some tasks include patternamking and sewing, library and archival research, organizational support for workshops and program events, among others. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Associate-Director--Audience-Engagement-and-Paid-Media_JR109393-1": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Audience Engagement and Paid Media",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School   At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately  9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW This person will work closely with Enrollment and college leadership to oversee and execute media planning, content distribution, and social media publishing that builds the brand and reputation for The New School and its individual colleges. The role is highly strategic– the ideal candidate can develop long-tail campaigns that work to support enrollment over time.  This person should have a background working with both paid and organic content, and the ability to direct agency partners and set benchmarks. This person will work closely with producers, Creative staff, and college communications leads to generate and publish content that represents the diverse, polyphonic life of The New School. They will also act as a key point of contact for the communications and public affairs teams to manage rapid response and crisis scenarios.  While the majority of this person’s time will be dedicated to supporting the Parsons and Performing Arts college, some time will also be committed to supporting Lang, NSSR, centers and institutes, and university-wide initiatives. This position reports to the Assistant Vice President, Audience Development and Media Activation.  CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Planning & Campaign Execution Establish and execute strategic plan for paid and organic media, working collaboratively with leadership in BMC and the Parsons/PA college to set priorities, develop campaigns, and grow our audience of right-fit prospects. Coordinate campaigns with website, SEO, and AIO strategy so efforts cross-pollinate. Content Creation & Platform Management Collaborate with Content, Creative, and Parsons/PA teams to produce social media content that drives recruitment and supports retention, affiliation, and alumni engagement for our current community. Oversee distribution of paid and organic content to social media, YouTube, Google, and other platforms via direct publishing and agency partners. Cross-Functional & Audience Integration Integrate campaigns and organic content distribution with Enrollment, Recruitment, and Student Retention teams. Manage user communities and concerns, collaborating with Media Relations to ensure leadership messages are shared as appropriate on social media and colleges communications teams are prepared to respond. Analytics, Performance & Governance Set KPI benchmarks by platform and interpret and report analytics to inform strategy and tactics. Set policy, train, and advise colleagues on social media best practices. Present trend reports, insights, and research to senior leadership as needed. Keep abreast of current events as well as trends and advances in digital media. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Three+ years in marketing or advertising. Understanding of the marketing funnel and how it applies to student recruitment and retention.  Strong grasp of analytics, marketing KPIS, benchmarks, and a strategic mindset to turn data into actionable insights.  Strategic mindset and the ability to iterate based on data and insights. Familiarity with social media publishing software(s) such as Sprout, ContentStudio, etc. Experience managing paid media campaigns across platforms and modalities.  Experience creating and executing strategic brand campaigns.  Skilled team leader comfortable with dotted-line reporting structures. Working knowledge of AI and implications for search and inbound queries. High integrity and adherence to brand guidelines. Ability to engage a unique and diverse community and its concerns. Experience in community management and crisis communications. Excellent interpersonal skills; able to take feedback and work collaboratively.  Sensitive to the speed required for social content, with a healthy respect for deadlines. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Higher education experience a plus. Experience managing high-profile brands. Experience establishing a brand presence on emerging platforms. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $83,500 - $90,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness   Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.   We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/SAS-Making-Center-Sound-Office-Tech-Assistant_JR109453": {
      "title": "SAS Making Center Sound Office Tech Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Media Studies Film Office within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.  The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor  Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them  Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously  Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests  Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:    Student Sound Office Techs: Support the Sound Office with the maintenance, organization, and day-to-day operation of sound equipment and resources. Assist faculty and students with the setup, use, and proper care of sound equipment, including microphones, recorders, and accessories. Maintain and support Sound Suits and recording booths, ensuring spaces and equipment are clean, organized, and ready for use. Assist faculty and students with the proper use and basic troubleshooting of Sound Suits and recording booths. Assist with equipment check-ins, check-outs, setup, and inventory. Support the use of additional production equipment, including lighting and camera equipment for in-class use. Use online equipment management and scheduling platforms to support Sound Office operations. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Must be an active continuing matriculated student Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE On-Campus Position  - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate:   $17.00hr/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provided customer service and front-desk support in a diverse and inclusive learning environment, assisting students, faculty, and staff with equipment reservations, resource requests, and departmental services. Maintained shop, classroom, and equipment spaces through organization, upkeep, troubleshooting, and equipment management, while supporting daily operations, training sessions, and special projects. Assisted faculty and students with classroom and Film Office equipment, including check-in/check-out procedures, technical support, and maintaining equipment resources. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/Graduate-Archives-Assistant--Student-Assistant-Specialist-_JR109325": {
      "title": "Graduate Archives Assistant (Student Assistant Specialist)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School Archives and Special Collections is hiring Archives Assistants to help with routine administrative tasks. Archives Assistants are graduate students who work under the supervision of the archivists to prepare for research appointments, assist with data entry, and complete basic tasks related to the processing and care of archival materials. Open to matriculated graduate students only, this is an on-campus position starting in Fall 2026, with possible extension through the 2026-2027 academic year. Students, with the approval of the supervisor, will establish a schedule of approximately 10-15 hours a week, between Monday and Friday, 9 am to 5 pm. Once established, the student must maintain a consistent schedule and commit to the position for the entire summer, preferably with the intention of remaining in the position throughout the 2026-2027 academic year. Preference will be given to students in the early stages of their academic program. RESPONSIBILITIES Setting up for and assisting researchers during visits to the Archives Research Room. Locating, retrieving, and re-shelving materials. Entering file-level inventories into archival management systems and computer applications such as Microsoft Word or Excel. Conducting basic research to assist archivists in identifying and describing archival materials. Writing summaries of research findings and abstracts of biographical and historical information. Answering phone calls.  Organizing and replenishing supplies to support daily operations.  Other tasks to assist the archivists, as assigned.  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Matriculated graduate student, with ability to maintain consistent schedule for the summer. Prior office experience. Demonstrated professionalism and strong work ethic. Superior English written and oral communication skills. Aptitude for following precise written and oral instructions and to complete detail-oriented work with precision. Research skills and ability to summarize findings in writing with precision. Ability to work independently, with minimal supervision. Ability to evaluate and correct one’s own work, when needed. Provide welcoming, friendly customer service to wide range of visitors internal and external to the university.  Physical ability and willingness to lift and carry boxes weighing up to 50 lbs.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Customer service experience. Early career graduate student. Interest in 20th century American history. Interest in the history of The New School and its departments/programs. Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) and Microsoft Office. HOURLY RATE: $20/hour WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Special Instruction: Applicants must attach a cover letter, resume, and 2 writing samples to their submission. Samples should demonstrate writing for a range of audiences and can be academic or professional. Incomplete applications will not be considered.  Applications for the fall semester are due by September 30. The department will review applications on a rolling basis. Responsibilities: The Student Archives Assistant will assist with routine administrative tasks, under the supervision of the New School Archives and Special Collections staff.  Student is expected to communicate clearly and precisely in written and oral communications, prepare materials for transfer or use in archives, welcome visitors and serve as a clerk during visits, and complete basic tasks to assist with care of archival materials. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Research-Assistant---Scholarly-Fashion-Studies-Book-Proposal-Assistant_JR109399": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - Scholarly Fashion Studies Book Proposal Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Research Assistant will support the research development and preparation of a scholarly fashion studies book proposal focused on swimwear. Building on an established theoretical framework examining swimwear through the intersections of diversity, social justice, and environmental impact, the position will assist the Lead Faculty Researcher in advancing the project from existing research and annotated bibliographies toward thematic synthesis, structural development, and manuscript planning. The Research Assistant will contribute to organizing and synthesizing existing scholarship, developing the project’s thematic architecture, researching appropriate publishing formats and requirements, and supporting preparation of materials for submission to prospective academic publishers. Duties & Responsibilities Review existing annotated bibliographies and research notes to identify, organize, and synthesize key themes relevant to the book project. Support refinement of the book’s focus and thematic structure through literature mapping and comparative analysis of existing scholarship. Assist with evaluating potential publication formats, including a single-author monograph and edited volume, and support development of corresponding chapter structures. Support preparation of the book proposal by organizing theoretical frameworks, research findings, chapter concepts, and related project materials. Research publisher requirements and proposal guidelines, including those of Intellect Publishing and other appropriate academic presses. Format and organize proposal materials in accordance with publisher submission requirements. Depending on the final publication strategy, assist with research and organizational support for a potential special issue or edited-volume pathway. Identify and catalogue potential multidisciplinary scholars and contributors whose work addresses relevant gaps in swimwear scholarship. Maintain bibliographies, research files, project documentation, and milestone tracking to support the timely development of the project. Qualifications – Education, Experience, and Skills Currently enrolled in a graduate program (Master’s or Ph.D.) in Fashion Studies, Art History, Cultural Studies, Sociology, the Humanities, or a closely related field. Strong written communication, organizational, analytical, and research skills. Familiarity with literature reviews, annotated bibliographies, and scholarly research practices. Ability to work independently, manage multiple tasks, and meet agreed-upon project timelines. Strong attention to detail and ability to organize research materials and project documentation. Preferred Qualifications Research interest or prior experience in critical fashion studies, sustainability, body politics, social justice, or related interdisciplinary areas. Familiarity with academic publishing processes, book proposals, or editorial workflows. WORK MODE: Hybrid/Remote - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $31.37/hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: The Research Assistant will support the research development and preparation of a scholarly fashion studies book proposal focused on swimwear. Building on an established theoretical framework examining swimwear through the intersections of diversity, social justice, and environmental impact, the position will assist the Lead Faculty Researcher in advancing the project from existing research and annotated bibliographies toward thematic synthesis, structural development, and manuscript planning. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/Research-Assistant----TBD-Design-Development---Research-Assistant_JR109400": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - [TBD]Design Development & Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPosition Purpose: This position supports the development of [TBD] Design, an emerging paradigm that treats structural conditions - legal, regulatory, institutional, economic, historical, and cultural - as active design material rather than fixed constraints. The Research Assistant will support both the theoretical development of this framework (contributing to an academic book chapter) and the design of practical tools that allow business strategists and innovators to apply it. This is an opportunity for a graduate student with interdisciplinary interests in design, policy, and business strategy to contribute directly to original scholarship and co-develop a new practitioner-facing framework from the ground up.  Duties & Responsibilities Conduct literature review and source verification across design thinking, institutional theory, regulatory studies, and related fields  Research and help document real-world case studies illustrating the interaction between policy/regulatory conditions and innovation outcomes Assist in designing a practical framework or tool (e.g., a \"Conditions Audit\" or \"Conditions Map\") that operationalizes Conditions Design principles for business practitioners Support prototyping and iteration of the tool (e.g., in Figma, Miro, or similar), including structuring content, visual layout, and usability considerations Compile and organize research materials, citations, and supporting documentation Participate in regular check-ins to discuss findings, direction, and revisions Qualifications – Education, Experience, and Skills Coursework or project experience in institutional analysis, regulatory studies, administrative law, or public policy Experience designing frameworks, canvases, workshop tools, or other structured decision-support artifacts Familiarity with design thinking methodology and tools (e.g., customer journey mapping, service design, systems thinking) Proficiency with visual/prototyping tools such as Figma or Miro Interest or background in sustainability, ESG, circular economy, or social innovation WORK MODE: Hybrid/Remote - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions . #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $31.37/hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: This position supports the development of [TBD] Design, an emerging paradigm that treats structural conditions - legal, regulatory, institutional, economic, historical, and cultural - as active design material rather than fixed constraints. The Research Assistant will support both the theoretical development of this framework (contributing to an academic book chapter) and the design of practical tools that allow business strategists and innovators to apply it. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Parsons-Scholars---Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109355-1": {
      "title": "Parsons Scholars - Student Assistant Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons Scholars was founded in 1997 and provides NYC, financially eligible high school students with a 2.5 year art and design scholarship. We are seeking student workers to support Parsons Scholars’ programming, focusing on mentorship for high school participants, as well as administrative and design support. Programming primarily runs on Saturdays from 8:40 AM - 5:40 PM, as well as offering weekday support for scholars. The fall semester will run from August 22, 2026 to December 12, 2026. The spring semester will run from January 16, 2026 to May 29, 2026. Students applying for this role should also apply to the Parsons Scholars Course Assistant role for a total estimated 10 hours per week.  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Matriculated undergraduate student Federal Work Study eligible Organizational and communication skills Design skills (InDesign, Illustrator, Photoshop) Interest in art and design education Interest in equity and access in art and design PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with high school students Bilingual in English and either Spanish or Mandarin WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $21.00 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Parsons Scholars Mentors will support Youth Advocates for Saturday cohort sessions with material organization, preparation, and facilitation, provide one-on-one mentorship with high school students, document programming through photographs and written blog posts. They will also be expected to help organize and maintain inventory room, including restocking supplies as well as designing digital and print material for recruitment, public events, and the annual lookbook. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Social-Media-Student-Assistant_JR109366": {
      "title": "Social Media Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe School of Fashion seeks Social Media Student Workers for Fall ‘26/Spring '27 to help shape and implement a content strategy aimed at reaching external audiences through social media such as Instagram. This candidate will help shape engagement through building a strong internal community that supports students, alums & Program activities. This candidate may work across programs for full school-wide coverage. As such, they will work with the Program Administrators. Hiring Programs: AAS: FD - Fashion Design and FM - Fashion Marketing & Communications MFA: Fashion Design MFA: FDS - Fashion Design & Society and TEX - Textiles MPS: FBM - Fashion Business Management Responsibilities will include Understanding and contributing to the departmental content strategy Demonstrate skill in graphic design, photography and video content creation Designing social media strategies following the academic calendar Managing, creating and publishing original, high quality content on social platforms Schedule posts pre-approved by the Program Directors and Program Administrators Gather content  Administering programs social media accounts to ensure up-to-date content Establish social media guidelines and policies Planning, producing, and publishing around 3 posts per week for the @parsonsaasfashion social media account Documents (via photo + writing / tagging) and promotes events, curricular activities, and student work. Responding to replies and direct messages on Instagram, et al. Collecting and analyzing data generated by these efforts, and suggesting changes in strategy based on that data A weekly check-in meeting Hourly Rate : $18 per hour Number of hours per week: 5-10 hours per week WORK MODE On-Campus Position - expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Off-Campus Position - expected to work off-campus (fashion show or event site) during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Name of supervisor: Catherine Cacho-Leary, Associate Director of Operations, School of Fashion Contact information for supervisor: acholec@newschool.edu Responsibilities: This position supports the department’s social media and content strategy by creating and managing high-quality digital content. Responsibilities include developing social media plans aligned with the academic calendar, producing approximately three posts per week, and maintaining program social media accounts. The role also documents and promotes events, student work, and curricular activities through photography, video, writing, and other visual content. Student workers will engage with followers, monitor social media activity, and analyze performance data to recommend improvements to content strategy. The position also includes regular collaboration with Program Directors and Administrators through pre-approved content and weekly check-in meetings. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Event-Support-Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109371": {
      "title": "Event Support Student Assistant Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately  9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. EVENT SUPPORT STUDENT WORKER, SOF The Event Support Student Worker will support the Program Administrator working with the School of Fashion and the program faculty as assigned with general event needs such as the planning, setup, and breakdown of events during the year. This may include the SOF fashion shows, open studios, program recognition ceremonies, and material donations. This role includes both regularly scheduled and variable hours, depending on event needs. Evening and occasional weekend availability is required. RESPONSIBILITIES Support the School of Fashion programs and the Program Administrators with event planning, preparation, and execution. Support Staff and Faculty with projects for SOF events, materials donations, fashion shows and other productions. Welcome guests at events, manage check-in, provide information, and help direct foot traffic. Pick up and deliver items for event set up; assist with breakdown of events, including cleanup, room reset, and return of supplies to SOF operations hub. Coordinate with external vendors (catering, rental services, etc) and internal operations teams (security, facilities, events, IT) as related to work tasks. Support faculty and staff with materials donations, get materials unpacked and stored properly. Communicate proactively with SOF staff regarding scheduling, availability, and any potential conflicts for event support shifts. Assist event photographer with setup and capturing the event. Provide DJing services, play music playlist, confirm clean song versions. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Ability to perform physical tasks consistent with a Medium exertion level, including regularly lifting and carrying up to 25 pounds and occasionally lifting up to 40 pounds; must be able to bend, reach, and lift fabric rolls to shoulder height. Strong communication skills with both internal and external stakeholders Attention to detail and ability to follow through on assigned tasks. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. Off-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work off-campus (fashion show or event site) during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $21.00 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The position supports the School of Fashion with event planning, preparation, and execution, including fashion shows, productions, and other programs. Responsibilities include welcoming guests, managing check-in, assisting with event setup and breakdown, and coordinating with vendors and internal teams. The role also provides support with materials donations, event photography, and organizing supplies. Student workers are expected to communicate with staff regarding scheduling and event support needs. Additionally, the position may provide DJ services and music support, including managing playlists and ensuring appropriate song versions are used. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Parsons-Scholars---Course-Assistant_JR109356": {
      "title": "Parsons Scholars - Course Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons Scholars was founded in 1997 and provides NYC, financially eligible high school students with a 2.5 year art and design scholarship. We are seeking course assistants to support Parsons Scholars’ Unfathomable Futures and Space and Materiality courses. Courses will take place on Saturdays, beginning August 29, 2026 to December 12, 2026. These course assistants will carry on to support Parsons Scholars’ Drawing and Imaging, Integrative Studio I, and Integrative Seminar I courses in spring 2026 from January 23, 2027 to May 8, 2027. Students applying for this role should also apply to the Parsons Scholars Student Assistant Specialist role for a total estimated 10 hours per week.  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Matriculated undergraduate student Federal Work Study eligible Organizational and communication skills Interest in art and design education Interest in equity and access in art and design PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with high school students Bilingual fluency in Spanish and/or Mandarin WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-Onsite Hourly Rate: $25.13 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.   This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Parsons Scholars Course Assistants support teaching and learning outcomes through non-instructional activities for Parsons Scholars FY courses: Space & Materiality, Unfathomable Futures, Integrative Studio I, Integrative Studio II, and Drawing & Imaging. Duties may include: attending class, making photocopies, demonstrating use of equipment, proctoring exams, collecting assignments, assisting with classroom set-up, uploading course content online, tracking students’ participation online, assisting students having difficulty with online environment, and grading of student work. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Composition-Area--Student-Assistant_JR109439": {
      "title": "Composition Area, Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Composition Area at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Composition Area, Student Assistant . We’re looking for a creative and responsible individual to support initiatives within the Composition Area . This position reports to the Program and Administration Manager, Mannes School of Music and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in the composition or classical music-related field. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist with rehearsals and scheduling of Composition Department activities. Liaise and coordinate with the College of Performing Arts Production Office and Library as needed Assist in the coordination and promotion of activities related to the Composers Collective. Assist with Departmental activities in coordination with the Chair and as assigned by the Program & Administration Manager. Additional duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current student in the Composition area (BM, MM, or PDPL in Composition). Previous administrative experience. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Current student in a College of Performing Arts degree program Recipient of Federal Work Study award WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-Onsite Hourly Rate: $17.00 Hours per Week: 2-3 hours per week on average (estimated 40 hours per semester) We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS  Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, as well as a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. Responsibilities: Assist with the scheduling, rehearsal coordination, and execution of Composition Department activities while liaising with the College of Performing Arts Production Office and Library. Support the coordination and promotion of Composers Collective events in collaboration with the Department Chair and Program & Administration Manager alongside additional duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Light-Board-Programmer-Op_JR109410": {
      "title": "Temporary Overhire Light Board Programmer/Op",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Light Board Programmer & Operator to join its Production department. Reporting to the Master Electrician, the Overhire Light Board Programmer & Operator is directly responsible for supporting the Production Department and College Leadership in all areas of lighting operations as assigned, including but not limited to Drama productions, operas, concerts, experimental productions, external performance partnerships, and musicals. The Overhire Light Board Programmer & Operator will oversee all lighting control systems and equipment for productions produced by the College of Performing Arts. Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Lighting department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Serve as the light board programmer and operator for College of Performing Arts productions, concerts, or events as directed by the Master Electrician. Collaborate with the Lighting Designer to execute the programming of cues, effects, and parameter tracking for productions. Oversee minor repairs or maintenance of lighting equipment and systems for assigned CoPA productions with support from the Master Electrician.  Provide assistance with class concerts or smaller productions as required. Offer consultation on projection/video/audio-related requests pertaining to the reception or triggering of MIDI or OSC communications. Ensure strict adherence to the enforcement, compliance, and permits for all Fire, Health, and Safety regulations within CoPA venues and productions, in coordination with University partners, particularly as they relate to lighting or effects. Maintain flexible working hours based on project assignments. QUALIFICATIONS A minimum of one to three years of experience as a light board programmer is required. Demonstrated expertise in ETC Ion Classic and XE light boards, ETC networking software, Paradigm controls, and LED lighting units. Experience working with multi-parameter lighting equipment, including but not limited to moving head units, theatrical LED units, and multi-cell theatrical lighting units. Proficiency in setting, adjusting, and maintaining DMX and RDM-controlled theatrical lighting systems. The ability to work productively, courteously, and with a professional demeanor under strict time constraints. A strong understanding of the principles of lighting, lighting technology, low-voltage LEDs, and electricity. Knowledge of safety procedures for theatrical lighting, rigging, and staff safety when working at elevated heights. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Demonstrated understanding of the interaction between projection, video, and audio playback systems and lighting control through MIDI, OSC, and UDP protocols Prior experience within an educational or instructional setting Bachelor of Arts or Bachelor of Fine Arts degree in Theatre Design and Technology WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite Hourly Rate: $30.00/hour  WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/Student-Lab-Assistant_JR109428": {
      "title": "Student Lab Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School’s Innovation Center is seeking enthusiastic, curious, and motivated undergraduate students to join our team as Student Lab Assistants .     The Innovation Center explores the intersection of artificial intelligence (AI), extended reality (XR), emerging media, creative technology, design, and future-facing practices . Through the Center’s AI, XR, and emerging technology initiatives, students and faculty engage with new tools and modes of creative practice through workshops, courses, research, experimentation, public programs, hackathons, and collaborative projects.   Student Lab Assistants work closely with the Senior Director, Innovation Center team, faculty, and fellow students to support the Center’s programs, spaces, technologies, and projects. The position offers an opportunity to develop hands-on experience with emerging technologies while contributing to a multidisciplinary community of students, faculty, researchers, artists, designers, and external partners.   We are particularly interested in students who are curious about AI, immersive and spatial media, interactive experiences, creative computing, digital storytelling, and emerging forms of human-computer interaction .   Candidates are not expected to have expertise in every technology or area listed below . We are looking for students who bring relevant skills, intellectual curiosity, strong communication abilities, and a willingness to learn new tools and approaches. Student roles and responsibilities will be shaped around each individual’s background, experience, skills, and interests.   Responsibilities:   AI, XR, and Emerging Technology Support Explore, test, evaluate, and document emerging technologies, platforms, applications, and creative tools used by the Innovation Center. Support experimentation and projects involving generative AI, immersive media, virtual and augmented reality, spatial computing, interactive media, creative coding, 3D environments, and other emerging technologies . Assist students and faculty in learning and using Innovation Center technologies and platforms. Facilitate access to equipment and resources during open lab hours, workshops, classroom visits, demonstrations, and special programs. Provide basic technical troubleshooting and user support. Assist with prototyping, experimentation, user testing, research, and documentation of Innovation Center projects. Research new tools, platforms, creative practices, and developments in AI, XR, emerging media, and related fields. Participate in testing and evaluating technologies for potential use in teaching, learning, creative practice, and public programming. Help maintain equipment, software, digital resources, and Innovation Center workspaces.   Workshops, Learning, and Community Support Assist with the preparation and facilitation of workshops related to AI, XR, immersive media, creative technology, and emerging tools. Support students during open lab hours and hands-on learning sessions. Help develop tutorials, demonstrations, resource guides, and other learning materials. Assist faculty and classes visiting the Innovation Center. Help create a welcoming and accessible environment for students with different levels of technical experience. Explain technical concepts and tools clearly to students, faculty, and visitors from a wide range of disciplines.   Projects and Events Support Innovation Center projects, research initiatives, exhibitions, demonstrations, hackathons, design jams, talks, conferences, and public events. Assist with event preparation, technology setup, participant support, documentation, and event logistics. Collaborate with Innovation Center staff, student teams, faculty, and external partners on interdisciplinary projects. Support project organization, scheduling, research, documentation, and other administrative needs. Assist with tracking project progress and maintaining shared project resources.   Media, Communications, and Documentation Help document Innovation Center projects, workshops, events, student work, and research through photography, video, and other media. Assist with the creation of visual and multimedia materials for the Innovation Center’s website, social media, presentations, events, and public communications. Produce or edit content including graphics, short-form video, photography, web content, and social media posts. Help organize and maintain digital archives of Innovation Center projects and activities. Contribute ideas for communicating emerging technology, student projects, research, and Innovation Center initiatives to broader audiences.   Minimum Qualifications: Currently enrolled as an undergraduate student at The New School. Demonstrated interest in one or more of the following areas: artificial intelligence, emerging technology, immersive media, XR/AR/VR, interaction and visual design, digital media, creative technology, creative coding, game design, storytelling, video, communications, or multimedia production . Strong written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to communicate effectively with students, faculty, staff, and visitors from different disciplines and levels of technical experience. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to work independently as well as collaboratively within a team. Willingness to learn new technologies, platforms, and workflows. Ability to work responsibly within Innovation Center policies, procedures, and equipment guidelines. Ability to manage multiple priorities in a dynamic and fast-paced environment.   Preferred Qualifications : Photography, video production, motion graphics, audio, or multimedia editing. Generative AI tools and creative AI workflows. Creative and development tools such as Unity, Unreal Engine, Blender, Adobe Creative Cloud, TouchDesigner, Figan or related platforms. Interactive media, creative coding, web development, game development, or physical computing. AI-assisted image, video, audio, design, research, or prototyping tools. XR technologies, including virtual reality, augmented reality, mixed reality, or spatial computing. VR headsets and immersive hardware such as Meta Quest or similar platforms. UX, interaction design, prototyping, or user testing. Web publishing and digital communication platforms. Workshop facilitation, peer tutoring, technical support, or student mentoring. Event production, exhibitions, hackathons, or public programming. Research into emerging technologies, creative practices, or technology trends.   A strong candidate will be curious, collaborative, proactive, creative, dependable, and comfortable learning through experimentation . Excellent candidates will also demonstrate patience and enthusiasm when helping others explore unfamiliar technologies.   WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $17–$19 per hour   We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The position involves exploring, testing, evaluating, and documenting emerging technologies such as AI, XR, immersive media, creative coding, and 3D environments. Student Lab Assistants provide technical support to students, faculty, and visitors by assisting with equipment, software, troubleshooting, and open lab activities. They support workshops, classes, tutorials, demonstrations, and hands-on learning while helping create an inclusive and welcoming environment for users with varying levels of technical experience. Assistants also contribute to Innovation Center projects, research, events, exhibitions, hackathons, and other programs through technology setup, project coordination, documentation, and participant support. Additionally, they help create and organize media and communications materials, including photography, video, graphics, web content, social media, and digital archives. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist---Red-Dog-Productions_JR109430": {
      "title": "Student Assistant Specialist - Red Dog Productions",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks student staff for the Red Dog Productions videography team. The assignments include University-wide live events, covering symposia and panel discussions hosted in the auditoriums as well as documenting conferences, exhibitions, social receptions, concerts and lectures. Red Dog Productions will train individuals and they will work alongside producers and faculty advisors to create professional quality, original content that can be added to career portfolio and resumes, while gaining professional experience. Red Dog Productions is currently seeking great candidates to perform the duties of the Production Crew. This is a non-academic worker student staff position that reports to the Manager of Red Dog Productions. RESPONSIBILITIES Shoot HD video and set up equipment including tripod and mics Using editing software, transfer and compress files; edit footage Audio mixing Lighting setup and design for interviews, streaming and still photography Web and social media for video and photography Training in different areas as needed MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current FT active student status Knowledge of film and video equipment Technical knowledge of film and video cameras, lenses, lighting, audio, and support equipment   Experience with film and video production procedures and protocols, set ups, studio and equipment maintenance Film editing and software experience Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Production experience  WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE : $17/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * For full consideration, please include a Resume and Cover Letter in your application. Responsibilities: Shoot HD video and set up equipment including tripod and mics. Using editing software, transfer and compress files and edit footage. Create audio mixing, lighting setup/design for interviews, streaming and still photography. Create web and social media for video and photography. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/FWS---Mannes-Dean-s-Office--Student-Assistant_JR109434": {
      "title": "FWS - Mannes Dean’s Office, Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately  9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Mannes School of Music Dean's Office at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as the Mannes Dean’s Office Student Assistant . We’re looking for a responsible and motivated individual to support initiatives within the Mannes Academics area . This position reports to the Program and Administration Manager, Mannes School of Music, and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in music performance or music education. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards. RESPONSIBILITIES Set up classroom technology, assisting faculty with logging into computers and accessing files. Coordinate with the Technical Services Office to ensure classroom technology needs are met, including retrieving, setting up, and returning loaned gear.  Provide the Mannes Dean’s Office with any general office assistance as needed, including delivering/receiving equipment and scores  Additional duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current student in Mannes (BM, MM, or PDPL). Previous administrative experience. Recipient of Federal Work Study award. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Current student in a College of Performing Arts degree program. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $17.00 Hours per Week : 1-3 hours per week on average SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume and cover letter outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, and their Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Set up classroom technology and assist faculty with computer login and file access while coordinating with the TSO office to retrieve, configure, and return loaned gear. Provide general administrative support to the Mannes Dean’s Office through the delivery and receipt of scores and equipment alongside additional duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Woodwind-Area--Student-Assistant_JR109440": {
      "title": "Woodwind Area, Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Woodwinds Area at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Woodwind Area, Student Assistant . We’re looking for a creative and responsible individual to support initiatives within the Woodwind Area . This position reports to the Program and Administration Manager, Mannes School of Music and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in the orchestral or classical music-related field. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist with rehearsals and scheduling of Woodwind area activities. Liaise and coordinate with the College of Performing Arts Production Office and Library as needed. Assist in Master Class coordination and promotion Assist with Departmental activities in coordination with Co-Chairs and as assigned by the Program & Administration Manager. Additional duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current student in the Woodwinds area (BM, MM, or PDPL in Orchestral Instruments, with a concentration in Flute, Oboe, Clarinet, or Bassoon). Previous administrative experience. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Current student in a College of Performing Arts degree program Recipient of Federal Work Study award WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE Hourly Rate: $17.00 Hours per Week: 1-2 hours per week on average (estimated 20 hours per semester) We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS  Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, as well as a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. Responsibilities: Assist with the scheduling, rehearsal coordination, and master class promotion for Woodwind area activities while liaising with the College of Performing Arts Production Office and Library. Support departmental initiatives in coordination with the Co-Chairs and Program & Administration Manager alongside additional duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
