    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/Drama-Bank-Street-Programs--Student-Assistant_JR109446": {
      "title": "Drama/Bank Street Programs, Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe School of Drama at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Drama/Bank Street Programs, Student Assistant. We’re looking for an organized and enthusiastic individual with a self-starter disposition interested in all aspects of drama production & operations to support the School of Drama’s programs at 151 Bank Street . This is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in non-profit, performing arts, or higher education administration.. RESPONSIBILITIES Support daily operations within the School of Drama and Production programs with scheduling, paperwork, database upkeep, and general organizational/operational tasks Station Bank Street’s Welcome Hub in Room 102 to answer student, faculty and visitor questions Assist with faculty copy/IT needs, mail distribution, stocking supplies, program printing, special events, and mainstage marketing support.  Generate information and update College of Performing Arts Calendar; Post monthly on Call Board Support needs for classes / small projects Manage supply orders and restocking (tape, first aid, etc.) Create and circulate materials for the Arts Letters Maintain photo archives, scripts and production books Maintain physical supplies in Bank Street as requested Additional responsibilities as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Ability to commit to a consistent weekly schedule. Excellent written and verbal communication, attention to detail, and group and independent problem solving skills Working knowledge of Google Suite: Drive, Calendar, etc General knowledge of theater practices and protocols Commitment to diversity and inclusion PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Current student in a College of Performing Arts degree program At least one semester at The New School/other university or equivalent professional experience. Interest in operations and production, administration, and/or creative technologies. Current student enrolled in a College of Performing Arts program Recipient of a Federal Work Study award. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: Hourly Rate: $17.00 Hours per Week: up to 10-20 on average SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS  Applicants must submit a cover letter, resume, and copy of their Federal Work Study award through their application. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.​ Responsibilities: Station at the Bank Street Welcome Hub to manage daily administrative operations, scheduling, database upkeep, and front-desk reception for the School of Drama and Production programs. Assist with faculty IT needs, supply inventory, calendar management, event marketing, production archiving, and the creation of Arts Letters materials. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/H---72-Fifth-Avenue/FWS-Student-Assistant---Admission-Operations_JR109431": {
      "title": "FWS Student Assistant - Admission Operations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Admission Operations office seeks motivated students to provide general office support. Responsibilities include but are not limited to the following: Open, scan, and process incoming mail Provide administrative support to the Operations team Consolidate applicant and prospect records Perform duplicate checks for applicant records in our CRM Respond to prospective applicant inquiries via email Maintain current knowledge of admission and student services areas Assist in admission-related mailings Participate in special projects as needed Additional duties may be assigned Minimum Qualifications: Professional demeanor and ability to maintain confidentiality Excellent written and verbal communication skills Superior organizational skills Energetic, personable and team-oriented Strong work ethic; Ability to take direction and follow through on jobs until completion Experience working independently with limited hands-on supervision Ability to take the initiative and remain flexible Working knowledge of Google Suite and Microsoft Office Suite (Excel, Word). Preferred Qualifications : Preference will be given to students with Federal Work-study awards Hourly Rate : $17  Hourly Rate 10-20 hours a week   Work Mode : Hybrid/Remote  – Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely, with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce Responsibilities: The Admission Operations Student Assistant will provide administrative support to the Operations team. They will be expected to perform duplicate checks for applicant records in our CRM, answer student emails in a professional manor and other duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Affairs-Assistant---Dean-of-Students-Office_JR109445": {
      "title": "Student Affairs Assistant – Dean of Students Office",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nReporting to the Assistant Director of Student Conduct and Community Standards, the Student Affairs Assistant will work as part of a high-impact student cohort supporting key leadership initiatives, event logistics, research projects, and administrative operations for the Dean of Students Office. The Student Affairs Assistant will have opportunities to collaborate with professional staff and fellow student employees, engage directly with the New School community, and contribute to programs and resources that promote a welcoming, inclusive, and connected campus experience. This role is ideal for a student interested in higher education, student affairs, and community engagement and is eager to develop professional skills while making a meaningful contribution to the student experience. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist in the planning, promotion, and execution of monthly Dean of Students Office Hours and divisional student engagement events. Provide logistical and administrative support for the Faculty in Residence (FIR) program and related initiatives. Assist with collecting data on various  student affairs topics. Collaborate within a four-person student cohort on short- and long-term special projects assigned by the Dean of Students Office. Provide occasional reception coverage, front-desk customer service, and visitor greeting during staff lunch breaks, illness, or high-volume periods. Staff events, team meetings, and office hours in person as required. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS This Position requires a Federal Work-Study Award Proficiency in Canva, Google Suite, and Microsoft Office applications. Can work with The New School’s branding and style guides to create a suite of coherent collateral across Student Success PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience in event coordination, student leadership, customer service, or administrative office support. Familiarity with research methods and basic project management toolsets. Demonstrated commitment to community building, inclusivity, and student engagement. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $18-$19/ hr Diversity Statement:   We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Questions can be emailed to baholdie@newschool.edu but applications must be submitted through the New School WorkDay Jobs Portal for an interview to be scheduled. Responsibilities: The Student Affairs Assistant will work as part of a high-impact student cohort supporting key leadership initiatives, event logistics, research projects, and administrative operations for the Dean of Students Office. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Success-Hub---Student-Assistant--FWS-_JR109405": {
      "title": "Student Success Hub - Student Assistant (FWS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nStudent Success is dedicated to providing students with a dynamic student life experience that complements their curricular experiences and cultivates their intellectual curiosity. We work collaboratively with parts of the university to equip students with resources and opportunities that allow them to thrive both academically and personally. Our goal is to foster a sense of belonging and help students succeed in an ever-changing world.  The Student Success hub is our collaborative work space that houses several units under the Student Success umbrella. Reporting to the Manager of Operations, the Student Office Assistant will be the first point of contact for visitors to the Student Success Hub, providing customer service to our internal and external clients. The Student Office Assistant will also be responsible for assisting in general office tasks and ad hoc projects.  RESPONSIBILITIES Greet visitors who enter the office space and assist them with the check in process Notify appropriate staff member of guest arrival  Provide general information to students and other visitors of Student Success areas Assist with picking up and dropping off mail and packages from mailroom Support with administrative tasks like printing, photocopying, and data entry Assist with inventory of office supplies Ad hoc projects as assigned by Director of Operations and Manager of Operations   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled as a New School Student who qualifies for Federal Work Study Ability to provide a high level of customer service to our diverse community Ability to work a minimum of 7 hours per week during the hours of 9-5, Monday through Friday PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Enjoys problem solving Familiarity with navigating The New School’s resources Ability to handle multiple tasks at once in busy environments Ability to demonstrate strong organizational skills   WORK MODE   ●  On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely.  #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17-19 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) Please include your hours of availability with your resume and cover letter .  Responsibilities: The Student Office Assistant will be the first point of contact for visitors to the Student Success Hub, providing customer service to our internal and external clients. The Student Office Assistant will also be responsible for assisting in general office tasks and ad hoc projects.   We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Mannes-Techniques-of-Music--Tutor-Generalist_JR109444": {
      "title": "Mannes Techniques of Music, Tutor Generalist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Mannes School of Music at the College of Performing Arts seeks highly motivated students to serve as Tutor Generalists. We’re looking for patient and responsible individuals to support instruction. This position reports to Part-time Associate Teaching Professor Christopher Park, and is an ideal position for students who are interested in careers in Music and education. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award. RESPONSIBILITIES Tutor students in approved subjects as assigned by the Tutoring Coordinator Respond to email inquiries regarding tutoring assignments in a timely manner Submit timesheets and tutoring session confirmation forms in a timely manner Schedule tutoring sessions with assigned tutees Additional responsibilities as assigned to assist other students in out-of-class by tutoring and answering questions about course content taught in class. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS For each class you wish to tutor, minimum qualifications are as follows: Completed or tested out of class Excellent content knowledge in subject matter Excellent verbal and written communication skills Ability to patiently and efficiently provide instructional support to other students Ability to self-schedule tutoring sessions, once assigned PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Recipient of a Federal Work Study Award Current student in a Mannes School of Music or Performer-Composer degree program WORK MODE On-Campus Position - E mployees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE Hourly Rate: $29.20 Hours per Week: Up to 10, as needed This is a SENS-UAW unit position as per the collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Tutor students in approved subjects as assigned by the Tutoring Coordinator, providing out-of-class support and answering questions regarding course content taught in class. Schedule tutoring sessions, respond promptly to email inquiries, and submit required timesheets and confirmation forms in a timely manner alongside additional responsibilities as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Mannes-Percussion--Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109436": {
      "title": "Mannes Percussion, Student Assistant Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Mannes School of Music at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Mannes Percussion, Student Assistant. We’re seeking an organized, driven, and responsible individual to support percussion-related activities within the Mannes School of Music & Production - Large Ensemble Area. This position reports to the Program and Administration Manager, Mannes School of Music and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in orchestral percussion, non-profit, higher education administration, or performing arts production. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award.   RESPONSIBILITIES Organize Mannes Percussion Equipment Assist with the management of Percussion equipment Assist with the management of Percussion Practice Space Basic Percussion Maintenance  Support the Program and Administration Manager, Mannes School of Music and Large Ensemble as needed Assist with percussion specific set up and break down for rehearsals Other additional duties as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current Percussion student in their 2nd, 3rd, or 4th year of UG study or MM / PDPL PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Recipient of Federal Work Study award General Knowledge of Percussion Equipment Strong Organizational Skills Strong Interpersonal Skills WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE Hourly Rate: $17/hour Hours per Week: 5-10 Hours a Week We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS  Applicants should submit a resume and cover letter outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, and a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. Responsibilities: Manage and organize Mannes percussion equipment and practice spaces while performing routine instrument maintenance. Execute percussion-specific setups and breakdowns for rehearsals while providing support to the Program and Administration Manager and handling additional duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant-for-Communications---Graphic-Design_JR109419": {
      "title": "Student Assistant for Communications & Graphic Design",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Student Assistant for Communications & Graphic Design will play an integral role in supporting both communications and creative design efforts in Student Success. This position combines communication strategy, content development, and student outreach with graphic design and multimedia content creation for digital and print platforms.  Applicants should anticipate working approximately 15–20 hours per week during the academic year from September 2026 through May 2027. Compensation is $17-$21/hour depending on experience. This position is primarily in-person, with some opportunities for remote work and virtual meetings. Some evening and event support hours may occasionally be required. RESPONSIBILITIES Communications & Outreach Copyedit and proofread Student Success communications Gather, organize, and develop content for the bi-weekly Student Newsletter and monthly Belonging Bulletin Board, including messaging related to student resources, university initiatives, cultural heritage months, and campus events Assist with maintaining the communication calendar, blog, and other organizational systems for divisional communications Respond to timely student inquiries in the studentquestions@newschool.edu inbox. Design & Social Media Create Canva graphics for Student Success events, programs, social media @NewSchoolStudentLife, newsletters, presentations, and campaigns. Create social media content and monitor social media trends and contribute ideas for engaging, student-centered digital content Support photography, videography, and multimedia content collection during programs and events MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Must be a current full-time New School student pursuing a degree. Ability to communicate clearly and effectively both in writing and verbally Strong editing and proofreading skills. Ability to manage multiple projects and meet deadlines. Ability to communicate effectively with diverse audiences. Proficiency in Google Workspace and Microsoft Office. Proficiency in Canva. Ability to follow The New School’s brand and style guidelines. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Full-time New School student pursuing a Graduate degree. Previous experience in communications, media, marketing, design, or a related field. Previous communications, marketing, social media, or design experience. Experience creating content for social media, newsletters, email, or other digital platforms. Ability to communicate effectively with diverse audiences. Experience with Canva or Adobe Creative Suite. Experience with email platforms such as Mailchimp or Constant Contact. Familiarity with The New School and its student community. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17-21/hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.​ Responsibilities: The Student Assistant for Communications & Graphic Design will play an integral role in supporting both communications and creative design efforts in Student Success. This position combines communication strategy, content development, and student outreach with graphic design and multimedia content creation for digital and print platform. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Jazz---Contemporary-Music--Tutor-Generalist_JR109429": {
      "title": "Jazz & Contemporary Music, Tutor Generalist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nINTRODUCTORY PARAGRAPH The School of Jazz & Contemporary Music at the College of Performing Arts seeks highly motivated students to serve as Tutor Generalists. We’re looking for patient and responsible individuals to support instruction. This position reports to Part-time Associate Teaching Professor Ed MacEachen, and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in Jazz & Contemporary Music and education. This is a SENS-UAW unit position as per the collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award. RESPONSIBILITIES Tutor students in approved subjects as assigned by the Tutoring Coordinator Respond to email inquiries regarding tutoring assignments in a timely manner Submit timesheets and tutoring session confirmation forms in a timely manner Schedule tutoring sessions with assigned tutees Additional responsibilities as assigned to assist other students in out-of-class tutoring, and answering questions about course content taught in class. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS For each class you wish to tutor, minimum qualifications are as follows: Completed or tested out of class  Obtain faculty recommendation Excellent content knowledge in subject matter Excellent verbal and written communication skills Ability to patiently and efficiently provide instructional support to other students Ability to self-schedule tutoring sessions, once assigned PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Recipient of a Federal Work Study Award Current student in a School of Jazz & Contemporary Music or Performer-Composer degree program WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $29.20 Hours per Week: Up to 10, or as needed We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume and cover letter outlining their skills and experience concerning this position; and their Federal Work Study award letter if applicable Submit SJCM Tutoring Application Form: https://forms.gle/jnyoJvc4rsejVQ6u8 For each subject you wish to tutor, obtain a faculty recommendation to be sent to Ed MacEachen at Maceache@newschool.edu Responsibilities: Tutor students in approved subjects as assigned by the Tutoring Coordinator, assisting with out-of-class coursework and answering questions regarding course content. Manage session scheduling, maintain prompt email communication, and submit required timesheets and confirmation forms in a timely manner alongside additional assigned duties. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/FWS---Theater---Opera-Production--Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109424": {
      "title": "FWS - Theater & Opera Production, Student Assistant Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately  9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Performance Operations area at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Theater & Opera Production Student Assistant Specialist. We’re looking for self-sufficient, adaptable, hard-working individuals to support initiatives within the Production area. This position reports to the Producer of Theater and Opera and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in Non-Profit Arts Management or Entrepreneurship or Technical/Theatrical Production. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist Production Management with multiple fall/spring projects, including but not limited to Mannes Opera and School of Drama BFA and MFA productions.  Maintain campus access records to confirm that temporary employees have building access for all work-related calls. Schedule design, production, or various other meetings for assigned projects - taking notes and distributing meeting minutes. Attend and support required load-ins, strikes, run-throughs, technical rehearsals, and dress rehearsals as assigned. Schedule rehearsal and performance space usage in coordination with production, academic & university teams. Independently lead small-scale projects. Additional responsibilities as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Exceptional written and verbal communication skills. Interpersonal skills that allow the individual to function in a variety of settings among widely different groups of people. A unique combination of creative and analytical talents. The ability to work productively and graciously under strict time constraints. Flexibility and a “team player” sensibility. Enthusiasm to learn the operations of carpentry/rigging, lighting, sound, carpentry/props, costumes, ground plans, and light plots. Recipient of Federal Work Study award. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS BFA Student in Dramatic Arts. MFA Student in Contemporary Theater Performance. MA Student in Arts Management and Entrepreneurship degree program. MM Student in Voice.   WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $18.00 per hour Hours per Week: up to 15-20, not to exceed 20 hours in a single workweek while classes are in session SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, as well as a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Assist Production Management across fall and spring Mannes Opera and School of Drama productions by coordinating venue scheduling, meeting logistics, and temporary employee access records. Attend and support key production milestones including load-ins, technical rehearsals, and strikes while independently leading small-scale projects and handling additional responsibilities as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/No-Campus-Location/InsideSchools-FWS-Student-Assistant_JR109411-1": {
      "title": "InsideSchools FWS Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately  9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. InsideSchools, a project of the Center for New York City Affairs, is seeking a skilled researcher and writer to support the education team. Serving the largest school district in the country, our education team is committed to helping improve the quality of New York City schools for all families, especially communities in poverty and of color. For two decades, our InsideSchools website has been a trusted resource for caregivers and students seeking information about NYC’s 1,800 public schools, with more than 1 million users visiting our site every year. The education team offers policy analysis, reports, and public commentary to inform system change and school improvement. InsideSchools also supports immigrant families to navigate the NYC public school system. We facilitate workshops and provide one-on-one support at shelters and in partnership with community-based organizations.  This work-study position is for 10 to 20 hours per week and will report to the Director of Education Policy and InsideSchools and the InsideSchools Program Manager. RESPONSIBILITIES The Student Assistant will contribute to building the capacity of InsideSchools to more effectively and efficiently serve NYC parents and students. The position will support the education team’s major projects. Responsibilities include: Write descriptions of over 1,800 NYC early childhood centers, elementary schools, middle schools, and high schools. Elevate the voices of middle and high school students through youth-centered services.  Support newly arrived migrant families to navigate the NYC public school system. Engage and empower parents. Collect input from school communities to inform content and programming. Develop comprehensive resources to support public school families throughout the K-12 experience. Collect and conduct data analyses of NYC education data, research individual schools, and draft narrative content for school profiles. Support the design, collection, and analysis of data, including surveys, analytics, interviews, and focus groups, etc. Answer questions from public school families, including working with the team to monitor comments and inquiries. Research educational topics and draft content for InsideSchools website and policy reports. Conduct and present literature reviews and data analyses related to NYC education that guide the education policy agenda and program implementation. Assist in planning and implementing workshops for migrant families, events, and focus groups. Attend education team meetings. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current full-time New School student with a Federal Work Study (FWS) award as part of their financial aid package for the 2026-27 academic year.  Strong interest in the public education system. An understanding of or interest in learning more about the most pressing issues in urban education, particularly the relationship between families and schools. Strong research and analytical skills.  Excellent writing and communication skills. Capacity to work independently and as part of a team. Spanish, French, or Chinese language skills are a plus (not required). PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS We are looking for individuals who have experience in one or more of the following areas: Data analysis Marketing and outreach Qualitative and quantitative research  Social media  Writing Project management Teaching, training, or curriculum development Youth development WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE  $17 - $19 per hour  We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Student Assistant will support the InsideSchools team through research, writing, data analysis, and community engagement to help improve educational access for NYC public school families. Responsibilities include researching and writing school profiles and educational resources, analyzing education data, assisting with workshops and outreach for newly arrived migrant families, collecting feedback from students and families, and contributing to policy research and content that informs programs and advocacy. This position offers hands-on experience at the intersection of education, research, and community engagement.  We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/No-Campus-Location/InsideSchools-FWS-Design-Student-Assistant_JR109412": {
      "title": "InsideSchools FWS Design Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately  9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. InsideSchools, a project of the Center for New York City Affairs, is seeking a skilled design assistant to support the education team. Serving the largest school district in the country, our education team is committed to helping improve the quality of New York City schools for all families, especially communities in poverty and of color. For two decades, our InsideSchools website has been a trusted resource for caregivers and students seeking information about NYC’s 1,800 public schools, with more than 1 million users visiting our site every year. The education team offers policy analysis, reports, and public commentary to inform system change and school improvement. Since last summer, InsideSchools has supported newly arrived migrant families living in shelters to navigate the NYC public school system. We have facilitated workshops and provided one-on-one support at shelters and in partnership with community-based organizations.  This work-study position is for 10 to 20 hours per week and will report to the Director of Education Policy and InsideSchools and the InsideSchools Program Manager. RESPONSIBILITIES The design assistant will support the education team’s major projects including: Providing descriptions of over 1,800 NYC early childhood centers, elementary schools, middle schools, and high schools. Elevating the voices of middle and high school students through youth-centered services.  Engaging and empowering parents. Collecting input from school communities to inform content and programming. Developing comprehensive resources to support public school families throughout the K-12 experience. Developing and supporting partnerships and training programs with schools and community-based organizations.  Take photos of individual schools, edit images, draft captions for images, manage necessary release forms, and draft narrative content for school profiles.  Update and maintain the InsideSchools website and customized search tools, provide design suggestions for engagement.  Produce and edit content for promotion, communication, and marketing of InsideSchools through email copy, social media content, graphics, and flyers. Support the design, collection, and analysis of data, including surveys, analytics, interviews and focus groups, etc. Answer questions from public school families, including working with the team to monitor comments and inquiries. Assist in planning and implementing events and focus groups. Attend education team meetings. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current full-time New School student with a Federal Work Study (FWS) award as part of their financial aid package for the 2026-27 academic year.  An understanding of or interest in learning more about the most pressing issues in urban education, particularly the relationship between families and schools. Design capabilities to create graphics for flyers, social media, and website. Experience using content management systems (CMS) (i.e. Contentful, Wordpress), productivity software (i.e. Trello, Google Workplace, Google Analytics), design software (Canva, Illustrator, Photoshop), and social media (Twitter, Facebook) a plus. Strong writing and communication skills. Capacity to work independently and as part of a team. Spanish, French and Chinese language skills are a plus (not required). PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS We are looking for individuals who have experience in one or more of the following areas: Graphic design Data visualization Marketing and outreach Photography Social media  Writing Web design WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $17 - $19/hour  We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Design Assistant will help build InsideSchools' capacity to better serve NYC public school families by creating engaging digital and print content, maintaining website resources, and supporting communications, outreach, and community engagement initiatives. Responsibilities include graphic design, photography, website updates, content creation, data visualization, and assisting with research, events, and school profiles to ensure families have access to clear, accessible, and high-quality educational information. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/No-Campus-Location/Equity-for-Children-FWS-Social-Media-and-Outreach-Assistant_JR109413": {
      "title": "Equity for Children FWS Social Media and Outreach Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. Position/Role Overview Equity for Children/Equidad para la Infancia (EFC), a center within the Studley Graduate Program in International Affairs (SGPIA), is a pioneer and leader in the movement for equity through direct action and support of initiatives and programs that advance the agenda of children’s rights and wellbeing. We work to advance equity for children by creating and disseminating research about policies affecting the most underserved populations and programs that raise innovative solutions. We engage with children and youth to help shape responsible future leaders who realize the importance of equity-focused policy for themselves and for society in general. Founded in 2006 by SGPIA Professor Alberto Minujin at The New School, the program provides a global knowledge acquisition tool for policymakers, academics, students, practitioners and all those working to better the lives of children in need. With a presence in New York, Buenos Aires and Mexico City, Equity for Children sponsors independent, critical thinking and debate, original research, teaching and events in person and through its websites in English, Spanish and Portuguese, encouraging shared conversation, vision and knowledge for all those involved in children’s rights. Reporting to EFC’s Associate Director, the Social Media and Outreach Assistant will focus on developing the social media and online presence of EFC. Additional tasks may be requested to assist with the general communications strategy of the organization. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Tasks may include the following, and specific deliverables will be further identified: I. Social Media & Websites Manage and post given articles and links on EFC Social Media (Facebook, Twitter and Linked In) at least once per week on each account. Track number of hits, users, likes, followers, friends, and other metrics on a regular basis (once a week or month). Propose strategies to improve social media presence and track metrics. Post EFC website content on our WordPress sites to drive followers. Help with specific dissemination strategies on social media (i.e., for new research or an event). Collect and compile monthly strategic website and mass mailing metrics. Provide metrics analysis to pull out recommendations and best practices to increase outreach. II. Assist with documenting events for the EFC website As needed: Live post via social media. Take photos and notes. Prepare write-ups for the website. Conduct short video interviews. QUALIFICATIONS Interest in children’s rights. Verbal and written fluency in English and Spanish, preferred. Technologically capable and experienced with WordPress and HTML; social media platforms expertise with Facebook, Twitter, Instagram and LinkedIn. Some knowledge of graphic design platforms (Photoshop, Canva, etc.) preferred. Some knowledge of video production preferred. The ability and commitment to work for the agreed hours and days, and to be responsive with communication, and responsible with their duties. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $18/hr WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Reporting to EFC’s Associate Director, the Social Media and Outreach Assistant will focus on developing the social media and online presence of EFC. Additional tasks may be requested to assist with the general communications strategy of the organization. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Residential-Education-Community-Assistant---FWS-_JR109395": {
      "title": "Residential Education Community Assistant ((FWS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately  9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. We are seeking competent Community Assistants to support the daily operation of the residence hall offices during the Fall 2026 - Spring 2027 academic year. Based on needs and talents, this position will assist with graphic design, social media influence, office coverage, answering questions from students and parents, and other duties as assigned. The ideal candidate will be a hard-working student worker able to undertake a variety of tasks, be detail-oriented and creative, use discretion, exude confidence in making decisions, and work well under pressure. The qualified candidate will be able to prioritize their work and will rise to the challenge of incorporating new and effective strategies to achieve the best results.  The Residential Education Community Assistant (CA) position at The New School is a position for the duration of the academic year beginning Monday, September 7, 2026 and ending Friday, May 14, 2027. Community Assistants may be asked to work over University Breaks, including but not limited to Winter Break and Spring Break. Community Assistants incoming for the academic year will need to take part in CA Training as scheduled by your supervisor. CAs are required to work a minimum of 10 hours and cannot exceed a maximum of 15 office hours per week. Hours may include weekends if coverage is needed of the office and a CA is available to work. Additionally, hours must be worked between 9 am-5 pm, Monday through Friday.    RESPONSIBILITIES Office Responsibilities  Staff the residential office during your scheduled shift and maintain a visually appealing, clean, and sanitized work area.  Administrative tasks including completing paperwork (log, reports, etc), responding to building emails and voicemails, assisting with filing, and other office coverage tasks as needed. Contact Hall Staff regarding emergencies as necessary.  Provide excellent customer service in all interactions with residence hall occupants.  Conduct tours of the residence hall during your designated shift.  Other duties as assigned.  Maintain confidentiality in all aspects of the position General ResHall duties as assigned including but not limited to: Lockouts. Keycard encoding. Assisting professional staff with dorm initiatives including creating marketing events, posting signs, etc.  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Ability to lift packages that weigh at least 20 pounds.  Excellent customer service skills.  Clear communication skills: listening, verbal & written skills etc.  Dexterity, efficiency, and self-motivation to sort large volumes of mail.  Good organizational skills, reliable, dependable, and attentive to detail.  Ability to complete administrative tasks neatly and legibly. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Graphic design experience or courses.  Ability to use computer software including Google and Microsoft Suite.  Experience with Google Suite.   WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $17.00/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Community Assistants will support the daily operation of the residence hall offices during the Fall 2026 - Spring 2027 academic year. Based on needs and talents, this position will assist with graphic design, social media influence, office coverage, answering of questions from students and parents, and other duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/No-Campus-Location/FWS-Student-Assistant-Specialist--Web-UX-and-Content-Creation_JR109384": {
      "title": "FWS Student Assistant Specialist, Web UX and Content Creation",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately  9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Higher Education Affordability Project at the Center for New York City Affairs is hiring a team of Federal Work Study students to assist our work in web design, social media development, and content creation. You will be part of a student team working for our award-winning website at Understandingfafsa.org . The website and email newsletter are focused on helping first-generation students and college access counselors understand and navigate our nation’s complicated and confusing financial aid process. We work closely with students and counselors to design, craft, and test materials with the goal of making college financial aid easy to understand. We are seeing FWS students with interest and experience in web and UX design, social media marketing and/or web content creation. This is an ideal position for a student with a passion for web design, publishing, and social media who is interested in helping families navigate the financial aid system and avoid too much college debt. Students will work as a team and report directly to the Director of CNYCA’s Higher Education Affordability Project. RESPONSIBILITIES Work with our professional design team on developing V6 of the website including a new section focused on our popular classroom materials for counselors and families.  Assist with the administration and publication of our weekly newsletter and updates on LinkedIn and Instagram.  Assist the team in developing content and curriculum for the website, including research and writing of short “how-to” blogs and tools for students, families, and educators. Work with these audiences to develop new publications or products to make financial aid easier to understand and more accessible in high school and college.  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Experience in graphic design, web design, social media or content production. Passion for learning new skills and working with a team to develop materials and games that help students to learn about college financing and financial planning. Attention to detail and excellent organizational skills with the ability to meet deadlines. Full-time New School student with a Federal Work-Study award (If you don’t see this on your award letter, ask the financial aid office if you have a work-study award. Many students are unaware of this financial aid benefit.)    WORK MODE Hybrid - This FWS position is for 10-15 hours per week with flexibility around the semester schedule. All work and meetings can be remote, though we hope to organize in-person team meetings occasionally. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $21/hr This is a Federal Work-Study position with the possibility of evolving into a New School Student Employment position if grant funding allows.  SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS Please include the following with your application: New School Financial Aid Award Letter with FWS Allocation  Cover Letter detailing your interest and what role(s) you would like to play  Resume Portfolio or work samples  We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Students will assist the project director and the editorial and design team in developing and promoting new content for our website at Understandingfafsa.org , assisting with a major buildout, promoting new content on social media and helping with administrative tasks. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Technician--Audio-Production---Media-Resources_JR109346": {
      "title": "Technician, Audio Production & Media Resources",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Parsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks a Full-time Technician in Audio Production and Media Resources for the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The Technician should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. This is a fast-paced, dynamic environment that runs year-round. The Technician will interface with students and faculty daily and work with Making Center staff to support multiple processes and resources for all users in a range of making spaces including sound production studios and equipment resources, traditional black and white darkroom, film processing room, experimental darkroom, Digital-C printing lab, film scanning and inkjet printing labs, photography and motion capture studios, and the Equipment Resource Center.  This is an administrative position, governed by a Local 1205 Teamsters Professional union contract, that reports to the Assistant Director, Sound and Filmmaking Operations.  CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Oversee daily workings of the Making Center facilities as assigned and maintain a clean and safe working environment.  Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Work with other Making Center staff on the troubleshooting, repair, maintenance, installation, and continuous operation of a variety of hardware/software technologies and stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Support faculty and students. Lead demo sessions about various pieces of equipment. Make hardware/software purchases and process recommendations. Create and update web page content. Create and conduct orientations, trainings, and tours. Participate in the onboarding process for new technician staff through peer-to-peer trainings and skills sharing. Maintain administrative systems for scheduling, record keeping, inventories, and metrics tracking. Provide support for store and tool checkout functions as related to assigned areas. Supervise student workers, including training, tracking accountability, and assigning tasks during shifts. Become proficient in Web Check Out or current equipment management system within the first 6 months of employment. Create clear, accurate, user-friendly technical documentation, signage, and instructions for labs and equipment. Assist with facility improvement projects, shows and installations, and special Making Center, program, school or Parsons-wide initiatives as needed. Adapt to changes in the work environment; meet deadlines and anticipate facility and checkout center needs, adjusting approach and prioritizing competing demands.  Ability to be proactive in addressing facility needs, including but not limited to maintaining organized workspaces, tracking inventory locations, and monitoring Technician communication networks.  Properly handle, mix, and dispose of photo-chemistry and safely use ladders and other hardware to support facility needs. Acquire working knowledge of Digital C printing and a variety of Motion Capture and Augmented/Virtual Reality practices (including but not limited to: full body tracking, facial tracking, projection mapping, mixed reality, Motive (or other equivalent Motion Capture software), relevant CG and SFX software and SDKs) within the first 6 months of employment.  Maintain audio equipment and materials. Maintain audio interface and software in labs. Perform audio inventory, prepare checkouts, and perform logistics and deliver to classrooms as needed. Completion of university-required staff trainings and necessary COF's for all Making Center Facilities within the first 6 months of employment. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by supervisor. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor’s Degree and 3 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that include nights and weekends, in addition to scheduling shifts with semester changes. Knowledge of a variety of analog and digital photographic and audio processes, be familiar with practices related to current design and art practice - including: analog/experimental/digital photography, digital input/output, studio and on-location lighting and audio recording equipment.  Experience and knowledge of working with traditional darkroom chemistry. Technical knowledge of analog and digital technologies, including but not limited to still cameras, video equipment, audio equipment, studio strobe lighting and grip equipment, continuous lighting, virtual reality, projectors, steadicams, film scanners, large format printers, media players, Mac mini(s), tablets and other smart devices. Full comprehensive experience in maintaining and troubleshooting equipment/tools. Understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment, with a working knowledge of large-scale hardware and software deployment, network systems, color calibration/profiling. Direct experience with Adobe Creative Cloud, Capture One, Flexcolor Scan Software, various printer server software, Opti-Track Motive, Google Docs, Audio production processes and equipment & software including Pro Tools.  Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Strong ability to assist students working in an interdisciplinary and/or experimental manner from concept to fabrication. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.  Knowledge of Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems.  Knowledge of Motion Capture and Augmented/Virtual Reality practices. Knowledge of Digital C Printing. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-ONSITE SALARY $28.52 per hour/$51,906.40 per annum  SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS *  For full consideration, please submit a resume and cover letter in your application. WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness   Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/UC-Sewing-Student-Assistant_JR109318": {
      "title": "UC Sewing Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in various shops within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open work spaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines, and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.  The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Duties for each space vary slightly, but entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment and/or monitoring making spaces and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  Responsibilities Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor.  Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available  to assist them.  Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls in a professional and courteous manner.  Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests.  Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance.  Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery.  Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness.  Complete special projects as assigned.  Participate in training sessions and staff meetings  Minimum Qualifications:  Excellent customer service skills  Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users  Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail  Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Physical ability to lift items  Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends Work mode: On-campus position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work that is not feasible, or not in the organization's best interest, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Salary: $17.00 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment and/or monitoring making spaces and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E4-Wood-Student-Assistant_JR109157-1": {
      "title": "E4 Wood Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the E4 Wood, Metal, Tool Checkout, and Light lab within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.  The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.    RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor  Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them  Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls / emails professionally and courteously  Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests  Help students checkout appropriate tools for their projects. Inspect tools upon return and address issues such as damage, missing parts, or cleanliness. Maintain tool organization within the shop Using ladders and assisting in the installation of lighting equipment (specific to Light Lab Student Employees) Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:  Opening and closing the shop as assigned (unlocking resources, logging into computers, etc.). Processing / recycling materials. Receiving and organizing deliveries. Tracking and moving inventory. Assist with store support, including: Procuring purchased items for students and faculty.  Processing and preparing materials (including but not limited to wood, metal, plaster, clay and alginate). Counting and tracking inventory. Moving and organizing materials (up to 25 lbs, occasionally up to 50 lbs). Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery  Replace tool parts in kits and sets. Restock consumables. Change blades and other machine components as needed. Daily maintenance and cleaning of machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, including: Dust control including vacuuming and mopping Restocking PPE Clearing surfaces and resetting machines  Complete special projects as assigned  Participate in training sessions and staff meetings  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users  Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail  Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs  Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends Work Mode : On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI   Hourly Rate : $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests , assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,  and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
