    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Schneider-Concerts-Usher--Student-Assistant_JR109363": {
      "title": "Schneider Concerts Usher, Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPOSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The New School Concerts Department at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Schneider Concerts Usher, student assistant. We’re looking for a(n) responsible, outgoing, music-loving individuals to provide both a welcoming and efficient presence at our chamber music concerts. This position reports to the Director, New School Concerts and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in the live performing arts and events fields. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Check concert ticket buyers in at event using Splashthat app (training provided); Interact conversationally with event patrons - providing patrons with directions to available seats, exits, box office, restrooms, and other information Answer onsite questions as needed Ensure entrances and exits are not blocked, security guard has good visibility, and there is no crowding in lobby area or aisles Direct those with disabilities in finding appropriate, available seats and other supports QUALIFICATIONS Excellent verbal communication and customer service skills Comfortability interacting with members of the public Timeliness PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Love and knowledge of chamber music a plus Federal Work Study Award letter WORK MODE This position is in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $17.50 Hours per Week: as needed on the following six Sundays: 10/4/2026, 11/15/2026, 12/6/2026, 1/31/2027, 3/14/2027, 4/25/2027. Not to exceed 20 hours in a single workweek while classes are in session. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit both a resume and cover letter outlining their interest, skills, and experience pertaining to this position; and a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. Responsibilities: Check in concert ticket buyers using the Splashthat app while interacting with patrons to answer questions and provide directions to seating, restrooms, and accessibility supports. Maintain lobby safety and smooth crowd flow by keeping entrances, exits, and aisles clear while ensuring security personnel have unobstructed visibility. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/H---72-Fifth-Avenue/OCSE-Student-Assistant---Admission-Call-Center-Representative_JR109352": {
      "title": "OCSE Student Assistant - Admission Call Center Representative",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Admission Call Center Representative will be responsible for fielding a series of admissions inquiries from prospective students, parents, and counselors on The New School Admissions phone line. The Admissions Call Center Representative will help triage communication across departments and convey information regarding application instructions, admission events registration, deadlines, campus updates, program information, and general inquiries. Providing exceptional customer service is a key aspect of the role.  The role of an Admission student call agent is to act as a partner between staff and students throughout the admission process. Student Representatives are expected to be proactive with a friendly and professional attitude and answer general Level 1 admission-application enrollment inquiries that focus on gathering and recalling information.  Student worker(s) will perform professional duties and have responsibilities that require knowledge of admission regarding the university and high levels of accuracy. The ideal candidate will be able to practice comprehensive decision-making skills, be able to prioritize tasks and work collaboratively in a remote setting. This position would work under the supervision of the Senior Office Assistant and carry out well-established procedures and existing policies.  RESPONSIBILITIES Answer incoming calls and triaging communication to appropriate staff/departments Assist callers in navigating The New School website, application processes, and event registration Manage inquiries with enthusiasm for The New School, a positive attitude, flexibility, and excellent customer service Effectively and strategically utilize New School remote communication technologies MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Candidate must be a New School Degree Student Minimum one-year office experience  Understanding of The New School programs and admission procedures.  Excellent communication skills, both written and verbal.  Working knowledge of Google Suite and Microsoft Office Suite.  Must be able to work a minimum of 6-10 hours per week This position requires reliable access to a computer/laptop and internet. WORK MODE Hybrid/Remote - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: Hourly Rate: $17 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Admissions Call Center Representative will help triage communication across departments and convey information regarding application instructions, admission events registration, deadlines, campus updates, program information, and general inquiries. Providing exceptional customer service is a key aspect of the role. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E4-Metal-Shop-Student-Assistant_JR109135": {
      "title": "E4 Metal Shop Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the E4 Wood, Metal, Tool Checkout, and Light lab within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.  The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor  Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them  Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls / emails professionally and courteously  Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests  Help students checkout appropriate tools for their projects. Inspect tools upon return and address issues such as damage, missing parts, or cleanliness. Maintain tool organization within the shop Using ladders and assisting in the installation of lighting equipment (specific to Light Lab Student Employees) Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:  Opening and closing the shop as assigned (unlocking resources, logging into computers, etc.). Processing / recycling materials. Receiving and organizing deliveries. Tracking and moving inventory. Assist with store support, including: Procuring purchased items for students and faculty.  Processing and preparing materials (including but not limited to wood, metal, plaster, clay and alginate). Counting and tracking inventory. Moving and organizing materials (up to 25 lbs, occasionally up to 50 lbs). Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery  Replace tool parts in kits and sets. Restock consumables. Change blades and other machine components as needed. Daily maintenance and cleaning of machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, including: Dust control including vacuuming and mopping Restocking PPE Clearing surfaces and resetting machines  Complete special projects as assigned  Participate in training sessions and staff meetings  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users  Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail  Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs  Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends Work Mode: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests , assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,  and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E4-Wood-Student-Assistant_JR109138": {
      "title": "E4 Wood Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the E4 Wood, Metal, Tool Checkout, and Light lab within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.  The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.    RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor  Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them  Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls / emails professionally and courteously  Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests  Help students checkout appropriate tools for their projects. Inspect tools upon return and address issues such as damage, missing parts, or cleanliness. Maintain tool organization within the shop Using ladders and assisting in the installation of lighting equipment (specific to Light Lab Student Employees) Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:  Opening and closing the shop as assigned (unlocking resources, logging into computers, etc.). Processing / recycling materials. Receiving and organizing deliveries. Tracking and moving inventory. Assist with store support, including: Procuring purchased items for students and faculty.  Processing and preparing materials (including but not limited to wood, metal, plaster, clay and alginate). Counting and tracking inventory. Moving and organizing materials (up to 25 lbs, occasionally up to 50 lbs). Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery  Replace tool parts in kits and sets. Restock consumables. Change blades and other machine components as needed. Daily maintenance and cleaning of machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, including: Dust control including vacuuming and mopping Restocking PPE Clearing surfaces and resetting machines  Complete special projects as assigned  Participate in training sessions and staff meetings  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users  Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail  Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs  Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends Work Mode : On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI   Hourly Rate : $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests , assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,  and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/D---79-Fifth-Avenue/Event-Planning-Support--Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109332": {
      "title": "Event Planning Support, Student Assistant Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-21\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe India China Institute (ICI) at The New School seeks a highly motivated student to provide part-time support for the Institute's public programs, events, communications, and day-to-day activities.   The ideal candidate is organized, detail-oriented, dependable, and able to work independently while collaborating effectively with faculty, staff, students, invited speakers, fellows, and other members of the university community. The position offers an opportunity to gain hands-on experience in event planning, communications, digital media, and program administration in an international academic environment.   RESPONSIBILITIES The student worker will assist with the planning and implementation of the Institute's public programs and activities. Responsibilities may include, but are not limited to: Assist in coordinating public programs, including creating event pages, marketing campaigns, booking spaces, arranging catering and honoraria Submit expense reports and navigate platforms for reservations, receipts and reconciliations (MyDay, Concur); Manage correspondence with fellows, invited guests, event audience, students and faculty, administrators and colleagues, newsletter subscribers, etc.; Design monthly newsletter using Mailchimp Support other Institute programs and administrative activities as needed. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS For Federal Work Study award letter Strong written and verbal communication skills. Excellent organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to manage multiple tasks and deadlines effectively. Ability to work independently while also collaborating with a diverse group of colleagues and stakeholders. Familiarity with Microsoft Office, Google Workspace, and other common digital tools. Comfort learning and using web-based applications and new software. Interest in event planning, communications, international affairs, higher education, or related areas.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with Mailchimp, WordPress, Adobe Creative Suite, or similar communications and design platforms. Experience with event planning, communications, social media, or digital content creation. Working knowledge of MyDay and/or Concur. Experience with maintaining databases or managing online content. Other web, digital media, or programming skills.  WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate:  $19 to $21 per hour. The rate for this position is commensurate with experience.   We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The student will provide part-time support for the Institute's public programs, events, communications, and day-to-day activities. The student will collaborate with staff, faculty and students across the university. The position is open to both graduate and undergraduate students.  Federal Work Students are encouraged to apply.  This position may have high and low weeks of work. At times, it may demand up to 20 hours per week.  At other times, it may demand fewer than 10 hours. The candidate must be available to deliver on demand.  We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Teaching-Assistant-26-27-AY_JR108509": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant 26-27 AY",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-20\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nTeaching Assistants support faculty with a range of instructional responsibilities. These may include leading discussion sections, delivering a lecture, assisting with classroom management, assigning homework, assessing student work, administering exams, and maintaining attendance and other records. TAs work under the close supervision of the instructor of record, who designs the syllabus and determines course content. Before applying, carefully read the information below and review the TA Opportunities document . On the online application, you can indicate your preference for up to five courses that require TAs. Please note that this information may change depending on enrollments and course cancellation. Additional opportunities may also become available. When this happens, applications will be distributed to the faculty teaching these courses for review. Please see the Provost Office’s Graduate Student Teaching Program webpage for information about the programs and departments that are hiring TAs, and other useful information.  While the Provost’s Office coordinates the application process for Teaching Fellows, the departments and programs that sponsor these courses are responsible for making all hiring decisions. Please note: Teaching Assistant positions are governed by the Policy on Maximum Student Work Hours .  Minimum Qualifications: Applicants must meet the following minimum eligibility qualifications in order to be considered for a TA position. These requirements vary depending on the school to which the applicant is applying.  Applicants’ home schools and departments determine initial eligibility (please note: eligibility does not guarantee final selection). The departments that sponsor the courses for which TAs will be selected then make all hiring decisions.   Applicants for most TA positions:   Must be matriculated (i.e., enrolled in courses or maintaining status) in the semester(s) in which they plan to be Teaching Assistants; Must be within the time limit for attaining their degree; Must not have more than 33 percent of attempted course credits marked as incomplete; Must have a cumulative GPA of 3.4 or above; and If in a Master’s degree program, must be on track to complete 50 percent of their Master’s degree program by the end of the spring 2026 semester. (MFA Photography, MArch Architecture, and MA International Affairs students are an exception, and are eligible to work as TAs in the second year of their studies.)     Applicants for TA positions offered by departments at the The New School for Social Research: Must be matriculated (i.e., enrolled in courses or maintaining status) in the semester(s) in which they plan to be Teaching Assistants; Must be within the time limit for attaining their degree; and Must not have more than 33 percent of attempted course credits marked as incomplete. Specific duties will be outlined in your appointment letter. Under no circumstances should a TA work more than the assigned number of hours, perform duties not assigned, work outside of the semester in which they are appointed, or pay for work-related expenses. Work Mode : In person #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $45.97 Additional questions should be sent to gradstudentteaching@newschool.edu. For answers to the most commonly asked questions, please consult our FAQ and also refer to the CBA for the Academic Student Worker’s union on our Labor Relations website. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Teaching Assistants support faculty with a range of instructional responsibilities. These may include leading discussion sections, delivering a lecture, assisting with classroom management, assigning homework, assessing student work, administering exams, and maintaining attendance and other records. TAs work under the close supervision of the instructor of record, who designs the syllabus and determines course content We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist-for-Communications-and-Analytics_JR109306": {
      "title": "Student Assistant Specialist for Communications and Analytics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-20\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nFounded in 1919, The New School is a progressive, historic university in New York City. It is celebrated for redefining the boundaries of premier design, performing arts, liberal arts, and social research education. Bringing together more than 8,300 students across distinct, world-renowned colleges—including Parsons School of Design, the College of Performing Arts, Eugene Lang College, and The New School for Social Research (NSSR)—the university is transitioning to a dynamic two-college model, designed to integrate all art, design and performing arts within one college and liberal arts and social research in a second. To support this model, the Brand, Marketing & Communication (BMC) department is creating a highly supportive, integrated, and collaborative administrative ecosystem which includes two complementary and interwoven types of operating structures: one designed to provide an enterprise-wide spine of service and support, and another with integrated BMC teams dedicated to each of the two colleges.  BMC seeks a creative and analytic Student Assistant Specialist to assist the Associate Director of Events and Audience Engagement in elevating The New School brand and supporting enrollment. This role requires a high level of organization and excellent written and verbal communication skills. The right candidate will have a solid understanding of data management and visualization, the ability to responsibly work with Personally Identifiable Information (PII), a sharp, copy-editor’s eye, and an interest in strategic project management.  The role will support work on the university’s monthly Public Programs Newsletter which reaches an audience of over 100K, and an internal Splash users newsletter. Regular projects include editing copy and HTML and managing subscriber lists, but there will also be the opportunity to offer input on content and help craft and edit event marketing descriptions. The student worker will be responsible for creating monthly Splash data reports, ad hoc reports as needed, and managing data tracking for the Public Programs newsletter and other marketing initiatives. They will assist with key strategic projects from planning to execution, and be available on-site for key events and activations. Other tasks that fall under the purview of Events and Audience Engagement may be assigned.   Please note, while use of generative AI is not prohibited for all aspects of this role, the right candidate will be able to accomplish all required tasks unassisted. Use of generative AI for tasks involving sensitive data will result in immediate termination.  RESPONSIBILITIES: To elevate the reputation of The New School and support recruitment as part of the Brand Marketing and Communications team, and to collaborate with the communications and events teams in Eugene Lang college and NSSR on key initiatives. Compile and visualize monthly reports of Splash data Edit pre-generated HTML to create a monthly events newsletter Copy-edit and review content for two monthly newsletters Manage large-scale monthly contact uploads in Listrak Draft communications materials including marketing and newsletter copy, event descriptions, reports, and summaries without the support of AI  Schedule meetings, take notes, and assist with project management for high-priority initiatives and Splash projects Support key event series and marketing initiatives as needed  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Proficiency with Excel  Proficiency with Google and Microsoft suites  Current matriculated student at The New School  Must have a graduation date of June 2027 or beyond Excellent written and verbal communications skills Copy-editing experience and an eye for detail Ability to work independently as well as collaboratively Highly organized, with excellent attention to detail Ability to manage multiple projects and prioritize work PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Experience with Splash event management software Experience with Listrak email marketing software Familiarity with basic HTML  Graduate student Previous communications and events experience HOURLY RATE : $18 per hour Hours per Week: 10 Position End Date: Friday, May 14, 2027 (or thereabouts) WORK MODE : Primarily on campus. The student assistant specialist is expected to work both on campus and on occasion, remotely (with pre-approval required from the worker’s supervisor). #LI-DNI ​ REQUIRED DOCUMENTS: Resume/CV Brief Cover Letter  Original Writing Sample. ~300 words, creative writing, marketing copy, or business writing are all acceptable. (Please do not submit work created or edited with AI.)  OPTIONAL DOCUMENT : Federal Work Study Award Letter WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with visionary leaders, and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. The New School is  an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief,  national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce, and particularly seeks applications from members of underrepresented groups, as well as candidates who share this commitment.    Responsibilities: The role supports The New School’s Brand, Marketing & Communications team by managing newsletter content, editing HTML and copy, maintaining subscriber data, and creating monthly data reports and visualizations. It also involves assisting with strategic projects and events, drafting marketing materials, coordinating meetings, and supporting event marketing initiatives while handling sensitive data responsibly. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/D---79-Fifth-Avenue/Individual-Giving-Student-Assistant_JR109315": {
      "title": "Individual Giving Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-20\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School’s Development and Alumni Engagement (DAE) Office seeks a dynamic and talented Student Assistant to work with the Individual Giving Team. Reporting to the Individual Giving Director – and in collaboration with the team – the Student Assistant will provide part-time support (15 – 20 hours per week) of the university’s individual giving efforts by helping to foster a culture of philanthropy among students and assisting in outreach to alumni and donors.  They will receive on-the-job training and gain valuable skills in the professional areas of advancement, including fundraising, donor cultivation and stewardship, advancement services, and communications and marketing.  Key responsibilities: Donor Outreach & Events  Assist with process of outreach for donor renewals via text, email, and mail Assist in creating and reviewing content for email, social media, and other donor-facing materials including capturing campus and student content via photos and video Provide support to the DAE’s donor events  Student Engagement & Philanthropy Promotion Support the planning and execution of the Student Gift  Represent department at on-campus events including tabling and engagement activities Collaborate with team to increase student presence and voice in Annual Giving communications Campaign Support Assist in the creation and implementation of the on-campus elements of fundraising initiatives, including Giving Tuesday and Giving Day Support logistics, incentive distribution, and volunteer solicitor coordination Lead or contribute to student-centered components Help generate creative ideas to build excitement and awareness across campus Administrative and Data Support Provide administrative support to the Vice President including calendar management and data entry  Provide backup support of the Annual Fund inbox Assist in reviewing donation details in Salesforce and in associating with specific campaigns Participate in data maintenance efforts in partnership with the Advancement Operations team Qualifications Applicant must be a matriculated undergraduate or graduate student at The New School Strong written and verbal communication skills Demonstrated organizational skills and attention to detail Flexible, motivated, and dependable individual that can work well independently and in a team-based setting Exercises good judgement and discretion with sensitive and confidential information.  Willingness to engage with peers and campus community members in person and online Strong technology skills, including proficiency in Google Workspace and Microsoft Office, as well as willingness to learn new systems Experience in content creation (photo, video, graphic design) is a plus WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $17 per hour, 15-20 hours per week We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Student Assistant will support the Individual Giving Team with donor outreach, event coordination, student engagement, and fundraising campaigns such as Giving Tuesday and Giving Day. They will also provide administrative and data support, including communications, content creation, Salesforce updates, inbox management, calendar coordination, and data maintenance. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/N---66-Fifth-Avenue/Student-Assistant--Equipment-Resource-Center---Photography-and-Media-Resources_JR109304": {
      "title": "Student Assistant, Equipment Resource Center - Photography and Media Resources",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-20\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Photography Labs and Equipment Resource Center within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and support experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff. Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor. Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them. Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures. Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously. Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests. Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:  Darkroom and Alternative Process Labs - Students are responsible for setting up and maintaining equipment, mixing and preparing photographic chemicals, and keeping the space clean and organized. Assist other students with darkroom procedures, manage supplies, enforce safety protocols, troubleshoot issues, and document processes. Additionally, you might train new users and help with routine maintenance and repairs to ensure everything runs efficiently. Print and Scanning Labs - In the scanning and print lab, student workers will be responsible for setting up and operating scanning and printing equipment, managing and organizing digital files, and ensuring high-quality output. They will assist users with file preparation, troubleshoot technical issues, and maintain and calibrate equipment. Additionally, the role includes managing inventory of paper, ink, and other supplies, keeping the workspace clean and organized, and providing support and guidance to fellow students on lab procedures and equipment use. Equipment Resource Center - As a student worker assisting technicians in the daily operations of an equipment resource center, you will support the setup, maintenance, and operation of various equipment and tools. Responsibilities include effectively using Webcheckout for managing checkouts and returns, helping with routine inspections, troubleshooting technical issues, and ensuring equipment is clean and in good working order. You’ll assist in managing inventory, including tracking and restocking supplies, and provide support to users by explaining equipment functionality and usage procedures. Additionally, you’ll contribute to organizing and maintaining a safe and efficient work environment, and collaborate with technicians to streamline daily operations and address any operational challenges. Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery. Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness. Complete special projects as assigned. Participate in training sessions and staff meetings. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from the supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users. Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes. Attention to detail. Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds. Ability to work without close supervision or alone. Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.). Ability to lift items up to 25lbs. Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI   HOURLY RATE: $17.00 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests, assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/G---80-Fifth-Avenue/Part-time-Student-Health-Counselor_JR109251-1": {
      "title": "Part-time Student Health Counselor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-20\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 8,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world.   The New School is seeking two part-time, 10 months per year, Student Health Counselors who are licensed clinical psychologists to provide clinical supervision of doctoral-level psychology students, crisis intervention, short-term therapy, and case management to both graduate and undergraduate degree seeking students. We are seeking candidates who are highly skilled clinicians who have experience working with individuals with diverse identities and have experience with clinical supervision. This position reports directly to the Director of Counseling. These positions are part of the Student Health Employees at the New School (SHENS-UAW) Union.  The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the New School and SHENS-UAW. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Provide crisis intervention, assessment,  short-term individual and group therapy, and case management to university students Work with students in crisis and those with trauma histories, including racialized and other trauma related to identity. Supervise PhD psychology externs and MSW interns Connect with collateral contacts including hospitals, community providers, faculty and staff, as appropriate Assist with wellness outreach to university students, staff, and faculty Collaborate closely with interdisciplinary team within Student Health Services including: psychiatry, medical, and public health Participate actively in staff meetings and in weekly supervision. Maintain timely, appropriate and accurate records and correspondence related to students served Attend workshops, training seminars and university meetings as appropriate   QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D or Psy.D in psychology Current licensure to practice in New York State Clinical supervision experience Some evening hours required    WORK MODE This position is 100% in-person on campus during fall and spring semesters #LI-Onsite 10-month per year position Winter break will afford opportunities for some remote work hours.  HOURLY RATE $55.44/hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact.   BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP)   Financial Wellness  Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan   Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable)   Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents   Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/H---72-Fifth-Avenue/Assistant-Director-of-Financial-Advocacy_JR109300-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Financial Advocacy",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-19\nPosted: Posted 17 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Assistant Director for Financial Advocacy (FA) reports to the Director of Student Support and Advocacy (SSA) and serves as a member of the department’s leadership team. The Assistant Director of FA shares in the comprehensive development, implementation, and evaluation of departmental priorities and daily office functions that meet the mission of Student Success and The New School. The Assistant Director will lead the design, execution, and assessment of proactive financial wellness strategies that support enrollment, reduce financial barriers, and strengthen student success outcomes. This position's primary responsibilities are focused on supporting student financial wellness, overseeing the university’s emergency fund, supporting case management efforts of the department, and serving on the university-wide behavioral intervention team (CARE Team). Additionally, the Assistant Director serves on-call for off-campus student emergencies for a campus population of 10,000 students.  CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Financial Support & Wellness Program Administration: Works collaboratively with campus partners to administer the New School Emergency Assistance Program and accompanying initiatives, and manages the budget for the Emergency Assistance Program. Student Advising: Provides individual financial support advising to students. Initiatives & Outreach: Leads financial wellness and scholarship initiatives and conducts outreach to eligible students. Training Coordination: Assists in the organization and coordination of university-wide training for faculty, staff and student leaders on financial wellness, financial literacy and student support services. Student Support Case Management and Crisis Intervention: Serves as case manager and supports, advises, and advocates for students in distress, while utilizing university and community resources to meet their needs. Serves on the university’s behavioral threat assessment team (CARE Team). Resource Development: Supports the development, implementation, and evaluation of campus-wide resources that support students navigating critical situations impacting their academic and personal experience. This includes college transition issues, housing and food insecurity, financial wellness, identity development, discrimination, mental health, and sexual violence and harassment. Referral Network: Serves as a referral resource for students, parents, faculty, and staff. Campus Events: Participates in university-wide events, including residential hall openings, Orientation, Welcome Week, Family Weekend, and Commencement.  On-call Responsibilities Participate in 24-hour on-call rotation with campus colleagues; university on-call responsibilities are expected 365 days of the year, and being on-call during holidays should be expected; high level of in-person response to mental health concerns; other student support situations should be expected. Consistent communication with senior leadership during crises or emergencies; maintain accurate incident reports using PAVE Software. Work with the Campus Safety, Student Health Services and Residential Education team to respond to critical incidents on campus; work with other departments to provide an appropriate response to crises. Maintain contact with parents and other constituencies as appropriate. Leadership, Supervision & Operations Strategic Leadership: Serves as a member of the Student Support and Advocacy leadership team and assists in the development, implementation, and assessment of the department’s mission and priorities. Staff Supervision: Hires, trains, and supervises student staff. Performs other duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree and 2-4 years of work experience related to the position. Strong administrative, customer service, and interpersonal skills. Demonstrated intercultural competence and a continuing commitment to advance equity, inclusion, and social justice practices in support of a diverse community. Previous experience with student crisis response and/or service in an on-call capacity. Impeccable integrity and ability to handle complex, sensitive, and confidential issues. Keen understanding of issues facing college students and their parents. Ability to manage multiple collaborative projects and systems in a fast-paced environment. Strong problem-solving abilities.  Creativity, flexibility, and collaborative skills.  Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills. Ability to navigate Banner, Stellic, Pave Suites, and other student information systems.   WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-ONSITE Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. SALARY $74,300 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness   Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/H---72-Fifth-Avenue/Assistant-Director--One-Stop-Student-Service-Center_JR109258-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, One Stop Student Service Center",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-19\nPosted: Posted 17 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The One Stop Student Service Center team provides high quality customer service to support students' academic and financial objectives. The team’s partners are Financial Aid, the Registrar’s Office, the university Retention Initiative, and Student Accounts. One Stop Counselors are cross-trained to assist with questions and requests regarding billing, payment, financial aid, enrollment verification, transcript ordering, and completion of forms. The team provides assistance in-person, by phone, and by email.    The Assistant Director reports to the Director in delivering exceptional service while driving operational improvement. This role balances student-facing support, leadership, knowledge management, and process innovation. The ideal candidate is comfortable assisting students directly, supervising Student Assistants, and streamlining workflows. Success in the role requires a student service orientation, kindness, accuracy, meticulous attention to detail, and enthusiasm for system improvement. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES   Student Service & Coverage Provide support to students via email, phone, and in-person meetings regarding billing, payment, financial aid, student records, and housing. Troubleshoot and resolve a wide variety of student issues to point of resolution. Develop and maintain a comprehensive understanding of the University's policies and procedures in relation to financial aid, billing, payment, registration, and student records, and demonstrates strong ability to explain and effectively counsel students on navigating these processes. Document history of each student service interaction in Banner or Stellic. Provide outstanding customer service to students and families, approaching every encounter professionally and with a welcoming and caring attitude. Serve as primary backup to the Director; assume Director responsibilities during Director’s absences, including decision-making on urgent escalations and team coordination.   Student Assistant Supervision & Development Recruit, hire, train, and manage the student assistant team; create schedules in collaboration with campus partners. Assign tasks, set performance expectations, provide ongoing feedback, and conduct regular one-to-one meetings.    Knowledge Base & AI Student Service Platform Management Assist Director in revising, and updating policies and procedures in Guru, our knowledge base, and also in our AI platform to ensure accuracy and completeness. Collaborate with Director, Counselors, and partner offices to maintain current, student-facing information. Monitor AI agent responses for accuracy and thoroughness; identify gaps or errors requiring correction. Ensure that information in knowledge base is accurate, relevant, and reflective of current institutional practices and regulations. Maintain documentation of changes and version control.   Counselor Training & Development in Coordination with Director: Assist Director in developing training materials, resource guides, and tip sheets for Counselor team. Assist Director with the coordination and management of counselor schedules to ensure appropriate coverage, operational efficiency, and effective student support.    Workflow Improvement & Process Automation Identify inefficiencies in current workflows and recommend improvements. Explore opportunities to automate routine tasks using Google Workspace tools (Forms, Sheets, Flow) and other automation tools to reduce manual work and improve accuracy. Document processes and create standard operating procedures. Assist Director with data analysis on service metrics (volume, response time, resolution rate, escalation rate, student satisfaction).   QUALIFICATIONS Education: Bachelor’s Degree required. Experience: At least four years in higher education, and supervisory experience in a fast-paced, data-driven frontline student support team with multiple service channels is a plus. Skills: Exceptional interpersonal and communication skills; attention to detail; strong proofreading skills. Customer service orientation: Genuine commitment to helping students solve problems with kindness and efficiency. Excellent problem-solving skills. Technology proficiency: Interest in learning new platforms and tools. Strong facility with Banner, Google Workspace, Microsoft Office, and experience with TouchNet, Slate, and call center systems is preferred.  Mentor and Leadership; Demonstrated ability to manage, develop, and provide feedback to Student Assistants. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $74,300 - $75,300 per annum   WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact.   BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include:   Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP)   Financial Wellness  Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan   Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents   Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.  We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/Technician--Sound---Film-Resources_JR109309": {
      "title": "Technician, Sound & Film Resources",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-19\nPosted: Posted 17 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Parsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks a Full-time Technician for Sound Film Resources for the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and support experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The Technician should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. This is a fast-paced, dynamic environment that runs year-round. The Technician will interface with students and faculty daily and work with Making Center staff to support multiple processes and resources for all users in a range of making spaces, including sound & video production studios and equipment resources, filmmaking equipment center, and production facilities and studios. This is an administrative position, governed by a Local 1205 Teamsters Professional union contract, that reports to the Assistant Director, Sound and Filmmaking Operations.    CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Oversee daily workings of the Making Center facilities as assigned and maintain a clean and safe working environment. Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff. Work with other Making Center staff on the troubleshooting, repair, maintenance, installation, and continuous operation of a variety of hardware/software technologies and stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Support faculty and students Lead demo sessions about various pieces of equipment Make hardware/software purchase and process recommendations Create and update web page content Create and conduct orientations, trainings, and tours Participate in the onboarding process for new technician staff through peer-to-peer trainings and skills sharing Maintain administrative systems for scheduling, record keeping, inventories, and metrics tracking. Provide support for store and tool checkout functions as related to assigned areas Supervise student workers, including training, tracking accountability, and assigning tasks during shifts Become proficient in Web Check Out or current equipment management system within the first 6 months of employment Create clear, accurate, user-friendly technical documentation, signage, and instructions for labs and equipment Assist with facility improvement projects, shows and installations, and special Making Center, program, school or Parsons-wide initiatives as needed. Adapt to changes in the work environment; meet deadlines and anticipate facility and checkout center needs, adjusting approach and prioritizing competing demands. Ability to be proactive in addressing facility needs, including but not limited to maintaining organized workspaces, tracking inventory locations, and monitoring Technician communication networks. Properly use ladders and other hardware to support facility needs. Maintain audio and filmmaking equipment, studios, and materials. Maintain audio and filmmaking interfaces and software in labs. Perform audio and filmmaking inventory, prepare checkouts, and perform logistics and deliver to classrooms as needed. Completion of university-required staff training and necessary COF's for all Making Center Facilities within the first 6 months of employment. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by the supervisor. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor’s Degree and 3 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that include nights and weekends, in addition to schedule shifts with semester changes. Knowledge of a variety of analog and digital filmmaking processes, be familiar with practices related to current media practices - including: analog/experimental/digital audio and filmmaking processes, digital input/output, studio and on-location lighting, experience and knowledge of working with sound recording. Technical knowledge of analog and digital technologies, including but not limited to 16mm film cameras, digital video/cinema equipment, audio recording equipment, studio and on-location lighting and grip equipment, projectors, steadicams,, media players, mac mini(s), tablets and other smart devices. Full comprehensive experience in maintaining and troubleshooting equipment/tools. Understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment, with a working knowledge of large-scale hardware and software deployment, network systems, color calibration/profiling. Direct experience with Adobe Creative Cloud,, Google Docs, Audio production processes and equipment & software including Pro Tools.  Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Strong ability to assist students working in an interdisciplinary and/or experimental manner from concept to fabrication. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.  Knowledge of Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems. Knowledge of Motion Capture and Augmented/Virtual Reality practices. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-ONSITE SALARY $28.52 per hour/$51,906.40 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness  Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS For full consideration, please submit a resume and cover letter in your application. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/Technician--Sewing-and-Soft-Construction--fixed-term-_JR109308": {
      "title": "Technician, Sewing and Soft Construction (fixed term)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-18\nPosted: Posted 18 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Parsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks a Full-time Technician in Sewing and Soft Construction for the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The Technician should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. This is a fast-paced, dynamic environment that runs year-round. The Technician will interface with students and faculty daily and work with Making Center staff to support multiple processes and resources for all users in a range of making spaces including the sewing, shoemaking, tool checkout/ store, and soft construction facilities.  This is an administrative position, governed by a Local 1205 Teamsters union contract, that reports to the Assistant Manager of Sewing and Soft Construction. The hire is for a 3-year fixed term subject to end unless otherwise approved for renewal.   CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Maintain a clean and safe working environment and uphold a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Work with other Making Center staff on the troubleshooting, repair, maintenance, installation, continuous operation, and support for a variety of hardware/software and technologies. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Assist faculty and students. Lead demo sessions about various pieces of equipment. Make hardware/software purchases and process recommendations. Create web page content. Create and conduct orientations, trainings, and tours. Participate in the onboarding process for new technician staff, through peer-to-peer trainings and skills sharing. Maintain administrative systems for scheduling, record-keeping, inventory, and metric tracking. Provide support for store and tool checkout functions as related to assigned areas Oversee daily workings of the Making Center facilities as assigned. Supervise student workers, including training, tracking accountability, and assigning tasks during shifts. Oversee tool and equipment checkout for both in-shop/lab use and off-site special requests. Create clear, accurate, user-friendly technical documentation, signage, and instructions for labs and equipment. Assist with facility improvement projects and special Making Center, program, school or Parsons-wide initiatives as needed. Adapt to changes in the work environment; adjust approach and prioritize competing demands as necessary. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment with productive and positive workplace relationships. Stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by supervisor. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor’s Degree and/or 3 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that may include nights and weekends, in addition to schedule shifts with semester changes. Completion of university required staff trainings and necessary COF's for all Making Center Facilities within the first 6 months of employment. Become proficient in WebCheckout or current equipment management system within the first 6 months of employment. Knowledge of industrial sewing and soft construction techniques, finishing, and troubleshooting for traditional garment and accessory construction, as well as experimental projects. Technical knowledge of a variety of industrial sewing techniques and machinery.  Confidence in threading, safe operation and maintenance of sewing machines. Mechanical understanding, with a strong desire to learn how to troubleshoot and maintain several different types of industrial machines for repair and maintenance purposes.  Understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment. Familiarity with Adobe Creative Cloud and Google Suite. Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Strong ability to assist students working in an interdisciplinary and/or experimental manner from concept to fabrication. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.  PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with specialized industrial sewing machinery such as lockstitch, overlock, coverstitch, flatlock, post, walking foot, blind hem, chainstitch, embroidery. Experience with shoe making processes, leatherwork, and fiber art practices. Experience with fashion production processes such as patternmaking and textile manipulation. Experience with basic building and hand tools. Experience assessing and maintaining industrial sewing machines.  Knowledge of Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems.  Experience with large-scale hardware and software deployments. Experience with Juki, Consew, Brother, and Singer sewing machinery. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-ONSITE SALARY $28.52 per hour/$51,906.40 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness  Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS   Interview finalists will be asked to come in for an in-person interview in which their technical skills will be assessed. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/N---66-Fifth-Avenue/Student-Assistant--Equipment-Resource-Center---Photography-and-Media-Resources--FWS-_JR109305": {
      "title": "Student Assistant, Equipment Resource Center - Photography and Media Resources (FWS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-17\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Photography Labs and Equipment Resource Center within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.  The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  RESPONSIBILITIES: Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:  Darkroom and Alternative Process Labs - Students are responsible for setting up and maintaining equipment, mixing and preparing photographic chemicals, and keeping the space clean and organized. Assist other students with darkroom procedures, manage supplies, enforce safety protocols, troubleshoot issues, and document processes. Additionally, you might train new users and help with routine maintenance and repairs to ensure everything runs efficiently. Print and Scanning Labs - In the scanning and print lab, student workers will be responsible for setting up and operating scanning and printing equipment, managing and organizing digital files, and ensuring high-quality output. They will assist users with file preparation, troubleshoot technical issues, and maintain and calibrate equipment. Additionally, the role includes managing inventory of paper, ink, and other supplies, keeping the workspace clean and organized, and providing support and guidance to fellow students on lab procedures and equipment use. Equipment Resource Center - As a student worker assisting technicians in the daily operations of an equipment resource center, you will support the setup, maintenance, and operation of various equipment and tools. Responsibilities include effectively using Webcheckout for managing checkouts and returns, helping with routine inspections, troubleshooting technical issues, and ensuring equipment is clean and in good working order. You’ll assist in managing inventory, including tracking and restocking supplies, and provide support to users by explaining equipment functionality and usage procedures. Additionally, you’ll contribute to organizing and maintaining a safe and efficient work environment, and collaborate with technicians to streamline daily operations and address any operational challenges. Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness. Complete special projects as assigned Participate in training sessions and staff meetings MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from the supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests , assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,  and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
