    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist-Design-Workshop-Exhibition_JR109064": {
      "title": "Student Assistant Specialist - Design Workshop Exhibition",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-17\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nStudents will evaluate and document the exhibition space at 72 5th Avenue. The install will occur in August with programming following in September. Deinstall will occur in September/October in which students will manage the repurposing of materials and reset for the spring '27 class objectives. RESPONSIBILITIES:   Evaluate and document the exhibition space at 72 5th Avenue. Design in plan and in elevation the exhibition materials, based on materials produced during the spring 2026 academic year. Adjust and finalize materials from the Spring 2026 Design IV studio (Design Workshop) in conformance with the exhibition space, design, and intent. These include completing the full-scale material studies, scale models, and 2d representations, texts, graphics, and quantifiable information.  Create final presentation boards and exhibition graphics as per design. Move all materials from 25 E. 13th street for exhibition. Organize, promote, and host an opening for the exhibition at a date TBD. When completed, de-install exhibition, and re-purpose materials as per circular design guidelines developed by each team for their ADU prototypes.   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Participation in the Design IV Design Workshop studio or related graduate-level design studios in Spring 2026 which provides the framework, skills and knowledge to engage this work as outlined above. WORK MODE: On campus #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $21/hour Diversity Statement: We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Students will evaluate and document the exhibition space at 72 5th Avenue. In addition to d esign, plan, and elevation the exhibition materials, based on materials produced during the spring 2026 academic year. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/G---80-Fifth-Avenue/Health-Promotion-Specialist_JR109232-1": {
      "title": "Health Promotion Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-17\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 8,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The New School’s Student Health Services (SHS) seeks a full-time Health Promotion Specialist (HPS) to support student wellbeing through equity-centered health promotion, community engagement, and inclusive student programming. Reporting to the Director of Public Health Services, this position advances the mission of Wellness and Health Promotion (WHP) by fostering a campus culture grounded in belonging, equity, social justice, and collective wellbeing. WHP, the public health branch of SHS, promotes health as a critical component of academic success and student development. The Health Promotion Specialist will lead initiatives that support historically marginalized and underrepresented student communities while helping to embed anti-oppression, inclusion, and culturally responsive practices across student-facing programs and services. This role combines strategic organizing, student advocacy, educational programming, assessment, and cross-campus collaboration to advance equity and belonging throughout the student experience. Working under the supervision of the Director of Public Health Services, the Health Promotion Specialist will support university-wide prevention and health promotion efforts aligned with The New School’s strategic priorities and commitment to student success and social justice. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES   Student Engagement, Belonging & Advocacy Co-develop and implement student-centered initiatives that foster belonging, inclusion, and wellbeing for undergraduate and graduate students, particularly students from historically marginalized communities. Create community-building programs in response to campus, national, or global incidents impacting student wellbeing. Provide mentorship, support, and referral services to students navigating complex personal, social, or identity-related concerns. Serve as a liaison and advocate for student voices in campus planning and decision-making processes. Support retention and success initiatives for underrepresented student populations.   Health Promotion & Educational Programming Co-design and facilitate workshops, retreats, trainings, and educational campaigns focused on: Cultural competency Anti-bias education Social justice and equity Intersectionality and addressing microaggressions Self-care and collective care Community wellbeing Two Spirit, Lesbian, Gay, Bisexual, Trans, Queer, Intersex, Asexual+ (2SLGBTQIA+) health and well-being (e.g. Safe Zone) Collaborate with academic divisions and student affairs partners to integrate health and wellbeing themes into curricular and co-curricular experiences. Assist in activating The New School’s Healthy Campus Communities Framework, including: Learning and working environments Personal growth and development Services and supports Policies and procedures Physical spaces Social connection and community engagement    Assessment, Evaluation & Data Analysis Support administration and analysis of student wellness assessments, including the National College Health Assessment (NCHA). Assist in preparation of presentations and recommendations to inform strategic planning and continuous improvement efforts.   Peer Leadership & Program Coordination Help supervise and coordinate the university’s Peer Health Advocacy Program and other student leadership initiatives. Support training and professional development for peer educators, student interns, and student leaders. Assist with program operations, communications, and event logistics.   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's degree in Public Health, Health Promotion, Social Work, Psychology, Student Affairs, or a related field. Minimum of 2 years of professional experience in higher education, student affairs, public health, or community-based social justice, equity, and belonging programming. Demonstrated experience developing and facilitating educational workshops, trainings, or student engagement initiatives. Knowledge of the social determinants of health, health promotion theory and practice with intersectional frameworks. Strong understanding of diversity, equity, inclusion, anti-oppression, and student belonging practices. Excellent written, verbal, presentation, and interpersonal communication skills. Strong organizational, project management, and collaboration skills. Ability to work effectively in diverse, multidisciplinary, and fast-paced environments. Ability to manage confidential information with professionalism and discretion.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience supervising peer educators, student staff, or interns. Experience with campus climate assessment, data analysis, or program evaluation. Familiarity with restorative/healing-centered practices. Knowledge of culturally rooted practices and trauma-informed approaches. Experience working in a large, decentralized university setting. Bilingual or multilingual proficiency.   WORK MODE During the fall and spring semesters, this position is 100% in-person and requires some evening hours. During winter break and summer periods, a hybrid work schedule may be considered. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $66,300 - $70,000 per annum   WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include:   Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP)   Financial Wellness  Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan   Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable)   Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents   Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * (if applicable) Please submit two statements as part of your application package: Philosophy statement on equity and student belonging Statement on activating the social determinants of health at The New School and how this framework would inform your work within a Health Promotion specialty focused on equity and belonging. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/No-Campus-Location/NSSR-Student-Advisor_JR109200": {
      "title": "NSSR Student Advisor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-17\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The department is seeking a dedicated Student Advisor for the forthcoming 2026-2027 academic cycle. In this role, advisors support both incoming and current NSSR graduate scholars by facilitating enrollment procedures, degree requirement fulfillment, and various institutional administrative processes. This appointment carries a projected commitment of 10 to 25 hours per week for the duration of the academic year. This position is not covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Below is a list of responsibilities. The primary supervisor for the duty is noted (C=Chair; AA=Academic Affairs; AA/C=joint involvement of Academic Affairs and Chair in aspects of the project): Coordinate faculty advising for new and continuing students (C). Provide program information to continuing students (AA/C). Develop and/or update student handbooks to be available during fall registration each year (AA/C). Organize, advertise, and conduct orientation activities for new students (AA/C). Assist in gathering information about enrollment and placement (AA/C). Gather, organize, and review data for biannual academic review of students by department/committee faculty (AA/C). Maintain department records for students, including faculty advising assignments (AA/C). Report on departmental advising and dissertation committees to the Office of Academic Affairs. (AA) Participate in departmental/committee faculty meetings (C). Participate in monthly meetings with other Student Advisors and the Coordinator of Academic Affairs (AA). Post and maintain regular office hours throughout the fiscal year (July 1- June 30), as well as increased office hours during registration and add/drop periods (AA). Consult and assist students during all periods of registration(AA). Work with the Registrar’s Office to complete paperwork regarding non-course requirements, as well as complete the graduation-related tasks (AA/C). Assist with the administration of departmental/committee MA and PhD exams (C). Evaluate placement and removal of certain department registration holds (AA/C). Carry out the administrative and clerical responsibilities (typing, correspondence, telephoning, filing, etc.) associated with the above responsibilities (AA/C). QUALIFICATIONS An advanced scholar in excellent standing, possessing a cumulative GPA of at least 3.4, who has successfully fulfilled 66% of their attempted credits prior to the start of the term. Candidates must not have requested or received any timeframe extensions regarding the fulfillment of their NSSR degree requirements. Demonstrates a comprehensive understanding of the institutional policies and milestones necessary for scholarly advancement within the NSSR and their specific academic department. WORK MODE This role utilizes a hybrid model. To foster robust on-site partnership and community participation, students must maintain a physical presence on campus as required. Alternative remote or blended schedules may be reviewed individually, contingent upon departmental requirements and functional demands. Such setups require prior consultation and formal authorization from the relevant divisional leadership or an authorized representative. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $27 per hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: NSSR academic affairs seeks dedicated Student Advisors for each department and degree program for the forthcoming academic year. In this role, advisors support both incoming and current NSSR graduate scholars by facilitating enrollment procedures, degree requirement fulfillment, and various institutional administrative processes. This appointment carries a projected commitment of 10 to 25 hours per week for the duration of the academic year. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Electrician_JR109313": {
      "title": "Temporary Overhire Electrician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-17\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW   The College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking to add highly qualified Overhire Electricians to join its Lighting department. Reporting to the Master Electrician, the Overhire Electrician is directly responsible for the installation of lighting equipment and systems under the direction of the Master Electrician in venues controlled by the College of Performing Arts, assigned university space, and outside venues as needed. This includes but is not limited to Drama productions, Operas, Concerts, experimental productions, external performance partnerships, and musicals.  The Overhire Electrician is an integral and vital collaborator who thrives in a dynamic, fast-paced, and highly team-oriented environment. Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Lighting department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding.   CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Implement the lighting designs for CoPA productions under the Direction of the Master Electrician or their designee  Perform repair and maintenance of lighting equipment and systems in CoPA venues as assigned by the Master Electrician.  Perform lighting hang, focus & strikes for CoPA Productions under the direction of the Master Electrician Perform work in compliance and under permits for all Fire, Health, and Safety matters for CoPA venues and productions in coordination with University partners as it relates to lighting or effects Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS Previous experience as an entertainment electrician is required Exceptional ability to navigate challenging situations diplomatically. Interpersonal skills that allow the individual to function in a variety of settings among widely different groups of people The ability to work productively, graciously, and with an appropriate sense of humor under strict time constraints Understanding of the principles of entertainment lighting, lighting technology, low voltage LEDs, and electrical theory  Ability to read and execute light plots Flexibility, and a “team player” sensibility Knowledge of safety procedures for theatrical lighting, rigging, and safety when working at heights Flexible hours as needed, based on project assignment to include nights and weekends PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in Theatre Design and Technology  Experience in an educational or teaching environment Experience working with ETC, EOS, and Paradigm control systems  Experience working with multi-parameter lighting equipment  WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite HOURLY RATE: $27.00/hour  WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/No-Campus-Location/NSSR-Departmental-Student-Assistants_JR109294": {
      "title": "NSSR Departmental Student Assistants",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-17\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nNSSR seeks graduate students at TNS with good attention to detail, able to manage projects independently, with strong writing and organizational skills. The positions run a total 30 weeks in the Fall and Spring Semesters at 10 hours weekly. Ideal candidates have a total of 10 hours of availability during our business hours, 9am to 5pm, Monday through Friday.  CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Data collection and analysis Communications duties including emails Institutional research Events planning and community building Additional projects and duties as assigned QUALIFICATIONS Prior office or administrative experience  Strong writing skills Proficient in Google Suite Proficient / Advanced in Excel and Google Sheet with the ability to organize and manage large amounts of information Comfortable with all aspects of Zoom Ability to adhere to University COVID-19 Policy and requirements Ability to maintain to student privacy (FERPA) HOURLY RATE : $17-21/hr, depending on position, supervisor, and experience WORK MODE : Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and may work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: NSSR seeks graduate students at TNS with good attention to detail, able to manage projects independently, with strong writing and organizational skills. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant-for-Communications---Graphic-Design_JR109280": {
      "title": "Student Assistant for Communications & Graphic Design",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-14\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Student Assistant for Communications & Graphic Design will play an integral role in supporting both communications and creative design efforts in Student Success. This position combines communication strategy, content development, and student outreach with graphic design and multimedia content creation for digital and print platforms.  Applicants should anticipate working approximately 15–20 hours per week during the academic year from September 2026 through May 2027. Compensation is $17-$21/hour depending on experience. This position is primarily in-person, with some opportunities for remote work and virtual meetings. Some evening and event support hours may occasionally be required. RESPONSIBILITIES Communications & Outreach Copyedit and proofread Student Success communications Gather, organize, and develop content for the bi-weekly Student Newsletter and monthly Belonging Bulletin Board, including messaging related to student resources, university initiatives, cultural heritage months, and campus events Assist with maintaining the communication calendar, blog, and other organizational systems for divisional communications Respond to timely student inquiries in the studentquestions@newschool.edu inbox. Design & Social Media Create Canva graphics for Student Success events, programs, social media @NewSchoolStudentLife, newsletters, presentations, and campaigns. Create social media content and monitor social media trends and contribute ideas for engaging, student-centered digital content Support photography, videography, and multimedia content collection during programs and events MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Must be a current full-time New School student pursuing a degree. Ability to communicate clearly and effectively both in writing and verbally Strong editing and proofreading skills. Ability to manage multiple projects and meet deadlines. Ability to communicate effectively with diverse audiences. Proficiency in Google Workspace and Microsoft Office. Proficiency in Canva. Ability to follow The New School’s brand and style guidelines. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Full-time New School student pursuing a Graduate degree. Previous experience in communications, media, marketing, design, or a related field. Previous communications, marketing, social media, or design experience. Experience creating content for social media, newsletters, email, or other digital platforms. Ability to communicate effectively with diverse audiences. Experience with Canva or Adobe Creative Suite. Experience with email platforms such as Mailchimp or Constant Contact. Familiarity with The New School and its student community. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17-21/hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.​ Responsibilities: The Student Assistant for Communications & Graphic Design will support both communications and creative design efforts in Student Success. This position combines communication strategy, content development, and student outreach with graphic design and multimedia content creation for digital and print platforms. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/FWS---Welcome-Desk--Student-Assistant_JR109307": {
      "title": "FWS - Welcome Desk, Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-14\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe College of Performing Arts seeks highly motivated students to serve as Welcome Desk, Student Assistant. We’re looking for friendly, organized, and responsible individuals to support initiatives within the College of Performing Arts. This position reports to the Coordinator of Administration and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in non-profit, performing arts, or higher education administration. Responsibilities: Serve as a primary service point for the College of Performing Arts Reception duties and answering general phone and email inquiries Clerical duties such as filing, copying, scanning and shredding Handling assignments from department heads, staff, and administration Maintaining organization of office and supplies Running errands on campus as requested by Performing Arts staff Minimum Qualifications: Recipient of Federal Work Study award Must be a current New School student Strong organization skills with the ability to work independently and manage multiple priorities. Self-motivated, proactive, detail-oriented, and responsible. Punctual and reliable. Excellent customer service skills. Technical proficiency with computer applications including the Google Suite and MS Office Ability to commit to a regular schedule for the duration of the semester Preferred Qualifications: Experience in customer service Experience in an administrative/office environment Work Mode : On-Campus Position #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17.00/hour Hours per Week: 10 This position is open to student applicants with a Federal Work Study award.​ We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Serves as the primary service point for the College of Performing Arts by managing reception, answering phone and email inquiries, and maintaining office organization. Executes daily clerical tasks, campus errands, and administrative assignments from department staff. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist---News-Editor_JR109301": {
      "title": "Student Assistant Specialist - News Editor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education. The Dean’s Office at Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, the Adult Bachelor’s Program, and The New School for Social Research seeks a Student Assistant Specialist to update news and promotional content at our college websites. RESPONSIBILITIES Enter brief 2-3 sentence news briefs at our news blog and websites (Lang, the Adult Bachelor’s Program, and NSSR) Correspond with faculty to confirm information in drafted news posts (when necessary) Audit posted news entries and log posted items Check in weekly with a supervisor MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Matriculated New School student Applicants must have a Federal Work-Study (FWA) award since this position is paid for with Federal Work-Study funds. Organized and able to manage projects on one’s own  Attentive to details Ability to communicate in a professional manner with faculty and staff Able to work 5 hours per week PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with a content management system is a plus but not necessary. The person in this position will be trained on our cms platform. Experience with google spreadsheets. We use these to textually log the status of projects. WORK MODE Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions.  #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $18/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Student Assistant Specialist updates news and promotional content at our college websites and provide occasional academic administrative support. The person in this position works independently for the most part although a supervisor will review much of their work before it is posted. They report to the Associate Director of Academic Communication and audit their work to ensure it is posted and accurate.   We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist---Academic-Support_JR109302": {
      "title": "Student Assistant Specialist - Academic Support",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education. The Dean’s Office at Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, the Adult Bachelor’s Program, and The New School for Social Research seeks a Student Assistant Specialist to manage academic content at our college websites. RESPONSIBILITIES Research and update course samplings Manage departmental faculty pages Audit posted edits Cross-check that curricular requirements for academic offerings are consistent in all channels Check in weekly with a supervisor MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Matriculated New School student Applicants must have a Federal Work-Study (FWA) award since this position is paid for with Federal Work-Study funds. Organized and able to manage projects on one’s own  Patient and able to focus; Attentive to details Ability to communicate in a professional manner with faculty and staff Able to work 5 hours per week PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with a content management system is a plus but not necessary. The person in this position will be trained on our cms platform. WORK MODE Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $18/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: This Student Assistant Specialist manages academic content at our websites (Eugene Lang College, the Adult Bachelor’s Program, and The New School for Social Research). This work entails researching and updating course samplings, editing departmental faculty pages, and cross-checking curricular requirements for accuracy. If they complete their work in less than five hours in any given week and wish to work more to make up the difference, they will have the option to provide additional administrative support. The person in this position works independently for the most part although a supervisor will review much of their work before it is posted. They report to the Associate Director of Academic Communication. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant-for-Family-Engagement---Grad-Communications_JR109281-1": {
      "title": "Student Assistant for Family Engagement & Grad Communications",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Student Assistant for Family Engagement & Grad Communications will support communications and engagement efforts for New School families and students approaching graduation. This position will assist with developing timely, relevant communications that help families and graduating students stay informed, navigate important milestones and deadlines, and access resources and opportunities. Applicants should anticipate working approximately 10 hours per week during the academic year from September 2026 through May 2027. RESPONSIBILITIES Draft and edit emails, blog posts, and other content for New School parents and families. Help identify important university updates, deadlines, events, and resources to share with families. Provide operational support for the Family Ambassador Program. Respond to timely student inquiries in the families@newschool.edu inbox and commencement@newschool.edu Assist with parent and family programs including Family Weekend, Family Orientation, and virtual sessions. Draft and organize messaging on graduation preparation, key deadlines, university resources, events, and opportunities. Support with planning and staffing the Countdown to Commencement events. Create Canva graphics, visual assets, and branded materials for families and graduate students. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Must be a current full-time New School student pursuing a degree. Ability to communicate clearly and effectively both in writing and verbally Strong editing and proofreading skills. Ability to manage multiple projects and meet deadlines. Ability to communicate effectively with diverse audiences. Proficiency in Google Workspace and Microsoft Office. Proficiency in Canva. Ability to follow The New School’s brand and style guidelines. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Full-time New School student pursuing a Graduate degree. Previous experience in communications, media, marketing, design, or a related field. Previous communications, marketing, social media, or design experience. Experience creating content for social media, newsletters, email, or other digital platforms. Ability to communicate effectively with diverse audiences. Experience with Canva or Adobe Creative Suite. Experience with email platforms such as Mailchimp or Constant Contact. Familiarity with The New School and its student community. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17-21/hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Student Assistant for Family Engagement & Grad Communications will support communications and engagement efforts for New School families and students approaching graduation. This position will assist with developing timely, relevant communications that help families and graduating students stay informed, navigate important milestones and deadlines, and access resources and opportunities. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Cooper-Hewitt-6th-Floor-Office-Student-Assistant_JR109292": {
      "title": "Cooper Hewitt 6th Floor Office Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nResponsibilities : The student assistants will be responsible for supporting the operations of the 6th-floor classroom and office in Cooper Hewitt (CH). Additional responsibilities include: Serve as the student liaisons between Cooper Hewitt and Parsons Contributing to the HDCS newsletter Collaborating with Cooper Hewitt security on escorting faculty, students, and external visitors without CH IDs inside the facility. Supporting HDCS-related events Managing the 6th-floor workspaces: classroom, admin office, computer room, and lounge. Maintaining supplies/inventory Supporting faculty with technology Qualification : Must have a Cooper Hewitt ID and current HDCS student. No previous experience is needed. Work Mode : On-Campus (Cooper Hewitt) #LI-DNI Availability: Office Hours are subject to change, mostly Wednesdays & Thursdays. Additional hours for work performed remotely relating to HDCS operations and for events when applicable. Office hours will be split and arranged between the Student Assistant in collaboration with the Associate Director of Operations, Luke Alan Davis. Rate: $18 per hour, 10 hours max per week. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The student assistants will be responsible for supporting the operations of the 6th-floor classroom and office in Cooper Hewitt (CH). Additional responsibilities include serving as the student liaisons between Cooper Hewitt and Parsons Contributing to the HDCS newsletter. Collaborating with Cooper Hewitt security on escorting faculty, students, and external visitors without CH IDs inside the facility. Supporting HDCS-related events. Managing the 6th-floor workspaces: classroom, admin office, computer room, and lounge. Maintaining supplies/inventory. Supporting faculty with technology We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Cooper-Hewitt-Library-Student-Assistants--Non-Academic-Student-Workers-_JR109290": {
      "title": "Cooper Hewitt Library Student Assistants (Non-Academic Student Workers)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nResponsibilities:   The non-academic student worker will also be responsible for scheduling appointments for students. Appointments are required to ensure we don’t exceed the room capacity.  The student worker is responsible for pulling the books that are located onsite. Books not located onsite at CH library need to be obtained through other means through Parsons, Parsons library consortium.  Student workers will be responsible for checking books out on students' shelves. Qualifications: Must have a Cooper Hewitt ID and current HDCS student. ​ No previous experience is needed. Work Mode: On-Campus (Cooper Hewitt) #LI-DNI Rate: $18 per hour, 10 hours max per week. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The non-academic student worker will also be responsible for scheduling appointments for students. Appointments are required to ensure we don’t exceed the library capacity. The student worker is responsible for pulling the books that are located onsite. Books not located onsite at CH library need to be obtained through other means through Parsons, Parsons library consortium. Student workers will be responsible for checking books out on students' shelves. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E4-General-Student-Assistant-OCSE_JR109285": {
      "title": "E4 General Student Assistant OCSE",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the E4 Wood, Metal, Tool Checkout, and Light lab within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.  The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.    RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor  Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them  Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls / emails professionally and courteously  Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests  Help students checkout appropriate tools for their projects. Inspect tools upon return and address issues such as damage, missing parts, or cleanliness. Maintain tool organization within the shop Using ladders and assisting in the installation of lighting equipment (specific to Light Lab Student Employees) Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:  Opening and closing the shop as assigned (unlocking resources, logging into computers, etc.). Processing / recycling materials. Receiving and organizing deliveries. Tracking and moving inventory. Assist with store support, including: Procuring purchased items for students and faculty.  Processing and preparing materials (including but not limited to wood, metal, plaster, clay and alginate). Counting and tracking inventory. Moving and organizing materials (up to 25 lbs, occasionally up to 50 lbs). Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery  Replace tool parts in kits and sets. Restock consumables. Change blades and other machine components as needed. Daily maintenance and cleaning of machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, including: Dust control including vacuuming and mopping Restocking PPE Clearing surfaces and resetting machines  Complete special projects as assigned  Participate in training sessions and staff meetings  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users  Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail  Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs  Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate :  $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Research-Assistant---Eugene-Lang-College_JR109288": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - Eugene Lang College",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-12\nPosted: Posted 24 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nLang seeks graduate students to serve as Research Assistants for departments, labs, and affiliated centers with good attention to detail, independent project management skills, and strong writing and organizational skills. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Conceive new research questions and assist faculty in developing continued research questions Support faculty in literature reviews Analyze data Prepare work for presentation and publication Please be sure to discuss with your supervisor the specific expectations for your assignment QUALIFICATIONS Prior office or administrative experience  Strong writing skills Proficient in Google Suite Proficient / Advanced in Excel and Google Sheet with the ability to organize and manage large amounts of information Comfortable with all aspects of Zoom Ability to maintain to student privacy (FERPA) WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $31.37/hr This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Research Assistant will support an Lang faculty member with research activities, including developing research questions, conducting literature reviews, analyzing data, and assisting with presentations and publications. The role requires strong writing, organizational, and project management skills, proficiency with Google Workspace, Excel, and Zoom, and the ability to work independently while maintaining confidentiality. This is a hybrid graduate student position that provides hands-on experience in academic research and scholarly collaboration. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant-Printshop-FWS_JR109113": {
      "title": "Student Assistant Printshop FWS",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 25 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Printmaking and Book Arts Studios within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Responsibilities: Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor.  Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them.  Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures. Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously.  Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests.  Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:  Assist students with store purchases, fill and distribute orders. Refill and mix studio chemistry (silkscreen emulsion, cleaning mixtures, etc.). Assist students with Open Studio check in, check out and printing press reservations. Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery.  Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness.  Complete special projects as assigned.  Participate in training sessions and staff meetings.  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users.  Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes. Attention to detail.  Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds. Ability to work without close supervision or alone. Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.). Ability to lift items up to 25lbs. Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $17 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Primarily assist students and Making Center Staff with tasks in the Printshop and Book Arts studios during Open Studio Hours. Priority tasks include but are not limited to: checking in students to the space in WebCheckout, loaning out equipment, reserving printing presses and refilling chemistry. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/CESJ---Student-Assistant---Social-Media_JR109278": {
      "title": "CESJ - Student Assistant - Social Media",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Office of Civic Engagement and Social Justice (Lang CESJ) is situated within the Dean’s Office at Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts as social justice serves as the ethical cornerstone of a liberal arts education. CESJ serves as a hub, creating opportunities for students to bridge their social justice passions with their academic pursuits and build community with others dedicated to leading social justice-centered lives. CESJ programs take the form of \"learning communities,\" composed of students, faculty, staff, and community partner organizations throughout New York City. Through our civic engagement and social justice programming, the Lang community connects theoretical frameworks with the lived experience, fostering a critical space where transformative learning occurs.  We are seeking a student employee to support CESJ’s Social Media presence. RESPONSIBILITIES This position requires 10 hours per week to provide assistance in developing and maintaining the visual identity of our organization across various social media platforms. This position includes, but is not limited to, the following responsibilities: Develop and maintain a consistent visual aesthetic that aligns with CESJ's brand guidelines and target audience Assist in the creation of multimedia content, including videos, animations, and interactive graphics, to enhance social media engagement Stay updated on current design trends and social media best practices to ensure content remains fresh, relevant, and engaging Assist in creating scheduling and organizing social media content calendars, ensuring timely and consistent posting across platforms Monitor social media analytics and provide insights to optimize content performance and engagement Collaborate with other team members to brainstorm creative ideas and contribute to strategic planning for social media campaigns Work with Graphic Designer to create and post regular content Respond to social media communication and field any questions to appropriate areas within CESJ Collaborate with office staff to post regular updates related to the CESJ updates and events Report social media analytics and outcomes during staff and coworking meetings. Complete an end of semester report and evaluation to assess and improve CESJ social media engagement and outreach Attend staff meetings, 1:1 meetings, and coworking sessions Any other related duties as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Student at The New School, actively enrolled during both Fall 2025 and Spring 2026 semesters Strong interest in and/or demonstrated commitment to social justice  Creative, independent worker with ability to collaborate on a small team and adapt to shifting priorities Proficient in Adobe Suite, Google Suite, Microsoft Office Suite, and  other relevant software Detail-oriented with excellent written and verbal communication skills Ability to commit to the role from August 2026 through May 2027 Experience with event flier creation, social media posting, and general team support PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Understanding of social justice-centered programs and initiatives across The New School Proficient/familiar with Instagram, Canva  Federal Work Study WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17.00 an hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Social Media Assistant supports the Office of Civic Engagement and Social Justice (CESJ) by creating engaging digital content, maintaining a consistent visual identity across social media platforms, and helping promote CESJ programs, events, and initiatives. This role collaborates with staff to develop content, manage posting schedules, monitor social media analytics, respond to online engagement, and contribute creative ideas that strengthen CESJ's outreach and community presence while participating in regular team meetings and completing end-of-semester reporting. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/HEOP-Academic-Year-Mentor--FWS-_JR109257": {
      "title": "HEOP Academic Year Mentor (FWS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School Arthur O. Eve Higher Education Opportunity Program is a New York State Education Department–funded program that offers financial, academic, and socio-emotional support for five years of undergraduate study and related educational activities. It is designed specifically for economically and educationally disadvantaged students who might not meet traditional college admissions criteria but demonstrate promise and potential for academic achievement and success.  At The New School, the HEOP office is housed under the Student Affairs  in which they will coordinate, evaluate, and administer academic, and operational activities of the program to foster student growth and success. HEOP Mentors will report to the HEOP Manager. HEOP mentors will support the HEOP program including supporting events from prep to breakdown, assist with administrative tasks, assist HEOP students through email and in person. HEOP Mentors will also contribute to content on social media and assist with visuals for promotional material.  This is a one-academic-year position beginning Fall 26 through Spring 27,  9 hours per week. RESPONSIBILITIES Support the HEOP program with event preparation including but not limited to transporting materials, decorating & arranging spaces, and event breakdown/clean-up Perform administrative duties related to the functional operation of the HEOP Space Communicate important information regularly to first-year HEOP students via email  Contribute content on the official @NewSchoolStudentLife Instagram page in accordance with Student Success and university guidelines and policies. Assist with creating visual materials for college fairs, school visits, HEOP and New School Firsts specific events  Assist HEOP students with challenges they may be experiencing by providing support and guidance Participate in the assessment efforts of HEOP by assisting in the administration of surveys to students, as well as filling out assessment tools aimed at understanding learning outcomes MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Passionate about access/opportunity programs Be open-minded, and culturally competent Takes initiative, works independently and collaboratively, strong work ethic   Meets deadlines promptly, communicates clearly, and asks for help when needed Uses good judgment, thinks critically and thoughtfully Must have a cumulative GPA of 3.0 or higher Assist students with the development of their study skills  Knowledge of social media platforms such as but not limited to IG, Facebook, etc. Experience with researching, brainstorming, ideating, and creating programming and marketing materials Must be able to lift boxes weighing up to 35lbs  Must be available to attend in person training on September 1st WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $18 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: HEOP Mentors will report to the HEOP Manager. HEOP mentors will support the HEOP program including supporting events from prep to breakdown, assist with administrative tasks, assist HEOP students through email and in person. HEOP Mentors will also contribute to content on social media and assist with visuals for promotional material.    We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Eugene-Lang-College-Dean-s-Office---Curriculum-Graduate-Student-Assistant_JR109239": {
      "title": "Eugene Lang College Dean’s Office - Curriculum Graduate Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education. We are seeking a student employee to support administrative tasks in the Eugene Lang College Dean’s Office.This position is for a Graduate Student Assistant to provide administrative support for the College’s academic, curricular, and assessment initiatives. This position requires the ability to collect and analyze data on course-related information such as enrollments, attributes, meeting patterns, and other features of curriculum delivery and demands a high level of accuracy and strong attention to detail. The graduate assistant must have exceptional written and oral communication skills and demonstrated proficiency with Google Suite (Google Docs, Sheets, Forms, Slides) and Microsoft Excel. The position is for 10 hours per week, either remote and in person, and reports to the Associate Director of Academic Affairs. Duties vary based on College needs and the schedule is flexible.  RESPONSIBILITIES: Compile, transform, and analyze course data using Microsoft Excel and Google Suite Research and prepare materials in support of curricular projects Compile and summarize data related to Lang academic projects Proofread all curriculum-related materials, including course descriptions; crosscheck curriculum-related materials with website and university course catalog information Monitor tagging of Lang courses with Shared Capacities and track course offerings across semesters and the academic year Track progress of curricular initiatives through Lang governance and Provost Office structures  Assist with management of academic program assessment data Assist as special projects arise Some occasional on-site event support may be required.  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS :  Interest in behind-the-scenes academic affairs work at the college level and appreciation for impact of efficient academic operations on student success Exceptional written and verbal communication skills Excellent editing and organizational skills; must be able to manage multiple documents and streams of information from varying sources and in varying formats  Excellent analytical skills Superb attention to detail Demonstrated experience and proficiency with Google Suite, particularly Google Sheets Demonstrated experience and proficiency with Microsoft Excel Detail-oriented and organized, able to prioritize multiple tasks with varying deadlines Self-starter, able to work independently with limited supervision Professional and upbeat attitude; good sense of humor WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.  #LI-DNI Hourly Rate:  $19/hour, 10 hours per week We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: This position requires the ability to collect and analyze data on course-related information such as enrollments, attributes, meeting patterns, and other features of curriculum delivery and demands a high level of accuracy and strong attention to detail. Other examples of the scope of work include compiling and summarizing data related to Lang academic projects. Assisting with special projects and some occasional on-site event support may be required.  We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
