    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Assistant-Director-of-Alumni-and-Volunteer-Engagement_JR109231": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world.   POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW   The Assistant Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement creates, strengthens, and manages volunteer initiatives that cultivate dynamic, supportive alumni networks and meaningful opportunities for engagement in support of Development and Alumni Engagement efforts at The New School.   Reporting to the Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement, the Assistant Director develops opportunities for alumni at every career and life stage—from recent graduates to established alumni—to contribute their time, expertise, networks, and resources in support of students, fellow alumni, and the university.   The position leads university-wide alumni volunteer programs, with particular emphasis on alumni-to-student mentorship, career engagement, reunion host committees, admissions volunteer opportunities, regional and affinity engagement, and online alumni communities. The Assistant Director is responsible for recruiting, training, and supporting volunteers while building programs that foster lifelong connection. The role combines relationship management, communications, data analysis, and hands-on program execution. The Assistant Director works closely with both external constituents and internal colleagues across The New School.    Alumni Volunteer Programs Manage university-wide alumni volunteer programs, including annual goals, strategies, timelines, participation targets, and measures of success, that advance institutional priorities and strengthen lifelong alumni connection. Create inclusive opportunities for alumni at all career and life stages to contribute their time, expertise, and professional networks and engage with students, recent graduates, fellow alumni, and the University. Develop and maintain a comprehensive volunteer-management framework covering role development, recruitment, screening, onboarding, training, communication, support, recognition, stewardship, retention, and participation tracking. Support the annual Alumni Reunion by designing and managing reunion volunteer operations, including committee recruitment, onboarding, training, role development, outreach plans, participation goals, communications, and ongoing support. Assist the Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement with developing and managing the Alumni Council of The New School, including member recruitment, onboarding, communications, meeting preparation, and ongoing engagement. Act as lead coordinator for the Alumni and Student Engagement Committee of the Alumni Council by setting annual priorities, developing agendas, facilitating meetings, coordinating follow-up, and reporting outcomes to leadership.   Alumni and Student Engagement Design and manage alumni-to-student mentoring, career networking, and professional-development initiatives in partnership with Career Services, the schools, faculty, and campus offices. Support the Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement in developing a comprehensive regional and local engagement strategy by developing priority alumni lists, segmented engagement strategies, and industry-based communities based on expertise, affiliation, career stage, volunteer history, and engagement interests. Build and support mentoring, career, industry, regional, affinity, and interest-based communities across The New School Network and other approved digital platforms. Represent Alumni Engagement at university programs, volunteer activities, alumni events, and institutional gatherings.   Digital Engagement, Communications, and Data Manage alumni digital platforms, including LinkedIn and Graduway, and develop digital strategies that promote interaction, knowledge sharing, professional connection, and engagement. Partner with schools and campus offices to increase awareness, adoption, and effective use of alumni digital platforms; monitor participation, assess engagement, gather feedback, and recommend improvements. Write, edit, and proofread clear, compelling, inclusive, and accessible alumni volunteer communications. Enter and review alumni data in the Salesforce CRM to improve the accuracy and completeness of alumni career, employer, title, industry, volunteer, event, committee, mentoring, engagement, and contact information.   QUALIFICATIONS A minimum of three years of relevant professional experience in higher education, nonprofit advancement, alumni relations, community engagement, volunteer management, or another mission-driven environment, with a demonstrated record of directing and growing community volunteerism, engagement programs, communications, and events in an education or nonprofit setting. Exceptional organizational, verbal, written, and interpersonal communication skills, with strong attention to detail. Strong relationship-building and collaboration skills, with the ability to work effectively across departments, academic units, and teams. Demonstrated proficiency with CRM systems, event registration tools, online community platforms, or comparable technologies. Experience developing career, mentoring, professional networking, or student-to-alumni engagement programs preferred. Ability to independently and collaboratively manage multiple programs, events, communications projects, partnerships, stakeholders, and deadlines in a frequently evolving environment. Commitment to accessible, inclusive, and audience-centered engagement.. Availability to work some evenings and weekends and travel occasionally, as required. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. SALARY RANGE $70,000-$80,000 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact.   BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff . We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers   We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/SAS-Media-Studies-Film-Office-Tech-Assistant_JR109205": {
      "title": "SAS Media Studies Film Office Tech Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Media Studies Film Office within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.  The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor  Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them  Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously  Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests  Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:  Student Film Office Techs: Support Film Office managers with Film Office duties by maintaining film equipment and resources Assist  faculty and students with equipment.  Work with additional online resources including (websites, web-based platforms  Assist with on-site check ins and checkouts of camera and grip equipment Student Classroom Student Assistants: Attend all class or assigned workshops sessions- Classroom Technicians will have to arrive early to checkout equipment, and stay to the end of class to pack and return equipment. support faculty and students in use of Film Office equipment.  Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery  Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness  Complete special projects as assigned  Participate in training sessions and staff meetings  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Must be an active continuing matriculated student Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE On-Campus Position  -  Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate:  $17.00hr/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provided customer service and front-desk support in a diverse and inclusive learning environment, assisting students, faculty, and staff with equipment reservations, resource requests, and departmental services. Maintained shop, classroom, and equipment spaces through organization, upkeep, troubleshooting, and equipment management, while supporting daily operations, training sessions, and special projects. Assisted faculty and students with classroom and Film Office equipment, including check-in/check-out procedures, technical support, and maintaining equipment resources. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Office-Assistant---Office-of-Student-Engagement--OSE---FWS-_JR109167": {
      "title": "Student Office Assistant - Office of Student Engagement (OSE) (FWS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 29 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nReporting to the Assistant Director in the Office of Student Engagement, Student Office Assistants will focus on the physical and logistical support that comes from the Student Hub and Student Affairs spaces across the 1st and 2nd Floor of 39 W 13th Street. The Office of Student Engagement (OSE) at The New School supports the university's educational mission by fostering opportunities for meaningful engagement and connections beyond the classroom. OSE encourages students to actively participate in the university community by joining or starting a student organization, engaging in recreational activities, or attending events and programs organized by the Student Activities Board. OSE provides numerous opportunities to get involved on campus and enhance a student’s leadership potential. RESPONSIBILITIES Staff in-person desks across the 1st and 2nd Floor of 39 W 13th Street, providing customer service to in-person visitors, over the phone, or virtually; Must maintain a confidential work environment. Support student-led events & programs to provide event management support. Examples include supporting check-in, equipment procurement/pick up/drop-off, & decorating of spaces. Assist in the marketing, communication, & promotion of Student-Led programs through the Narwhal Nation platform & other social media platforms. Maintain, organize, inventory, and catalog the Equipment Library and Storage Spaces. Research and analyze campus-wide resources to increase student awareness and accessibility. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS This position requires a Federal Work-Study Award. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Ability to creatively problem solve, multitask, and function in a collaborative work environment. Capacity to provide and receive constructive critique. This position frequently needs to traverse multiple levels of stairs and ladders, position oneself into various levels to reach items, and move boxes weighing up to 20 pounds across offices, campus locations, and storage spaces.  WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17-$19/hr  SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS  To be considered: Through the career portal, please provide your financial aid award letter, and a resume. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Student Office Assistants assist in providing support to the Office of Student Engagement including providing customer service at reception desks, assisting with the event management, marketing, communications, and promotions of events, assist with the Equipment Library and Storage Spaces, and provide research support. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/FWS-Student-Assistant---Parson-Admission_JR109259": {
      "title": "FWS Student Assistant - Parson Admission",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 29 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons’ student assistants work collaboratively in the Parsons admission department responding to, cataloging as necessary, and fielding general inquiries, maintaining digital records, preparing recruitment materials for and participating in various virtual and in-person events as needed, and performing various administrative tasks including responding to email, and representing Parsons School of Design and its admission office from the current student-perspective. The incumbent will assist with inquiries directed to Parsons from internal and external stakeholders, including prospective students and families, applicants, and admitted and deposited students. Parsons’ student assistants report directly to the assistant and associate directors of Parsons’ admission offices.   We are looking for a dynamic, motivated individual who can work independently but is also a team player. The ideal candidates for this position are undergraduate and graduate students interested in leveraging their positive experiences at Parsons, and working on a small team, to field inquiries from prospective students, families, and other constituents.   Experience in an office setting or customer service is a plus. Applicants must be enrolled in an undergraduate or graduate program at Parsons School of Design at The New School. Students from all of Parsons’ undergraduate and graduate academic disciplines are encouraged to apply. Strong writing and speaking skills are essential. Applicants will be asked to complete a brief writing assignment during the application process. They will be assigned to undergraduate admission if enrolled in an undergraduate program and graduate admission if enrolled in a graduate program. Priority will be given to applicants who hold federal work-study awards. Responsibilities include: Monitoring Parsons admission and recruitment inbox inquiries and fielding replies Draft email response templates for commonly asked questions Provide administrative support to Parsons’ admission team Assist with admission and recruitment events as needed, including speaking about your experiences as a Parsons student, and representing both Parsons and its admissions offices Additional project-based tasks as needed including virtual and in-person meetings with prospective students and their families Desired Qualifications: Customer service experience or administrative office experience Excellent verbal and written communication skills Strong understanding of the Parsons student experience and programming Experience working independently and expediently with limited hands-on supervision Proficiency with Google Drive Ability to demonstrate discretion in handling confidential applicant information Ability to perform multiple tasks with effective time management and prioritization Availability to work 15-20 hours weekly  Hourly Rate: $17  Work Mode: Hybrid/Remote - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice . Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus, when necessary, to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI Special Instructions to Applicants : Please upload all Required Applicant Documents to be considered:  Resume/CV Cover Letter Federal Work Study Award We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Work collaboratively in the Parsons admission department responding to, cataloging as necessary, and fielding general inquiries, maintaining digital records, preparing recruitment materials for and participating in various virtual and in-person events as needed, and performing various administrative tasks including responding to email, and representing Parsons School of Design and its admission office from the current student-perspective We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/K---113-University-Place/OCSE-Student-Assistant-Specialist---ISSS-Student-Assistant-of-Communication-and-Engagement_JR109249": {
      "title": "OCSE Student Assistant Specialist - ISSS Student Assistant of Communication and Engagement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 29 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nInternational Student and Scholar Services (ISSS) assists international students and scholars to maintain their immigration records and also holds events for international students. We are currently seeking 1 Student Assistant of Communication and Engagement to assist with the operations of our office. This position is for 34 weeks and runs for the duration of the Academic Year - (start of the Fall semester until end of Spring semester) Student Assistant will not work during summer break, winter session, and spring break.  The Student Assistant for Engagement will work closely with the ISSS Director to coordinate communication and engagement efforts of the international student community at The New School.  This would include coordinating social media, Newsletter, event planning and coordinating, developing signage, and moderating webinars.  Responsibilities: ISSS Drop-in Advising moderating  Coordinating social media Instagram posting Brevo Newsletters WhatsApp Assist with coordinating and setup of ISSS events Marketing ISSS events on Narwhal Nation and social media platforms Monitor ISSS events email inbox  Generally assisting with other office tasks and special projects as necessary Minimum Qualifications: Must be available to moderate virtual drop-in advising hours Tuesday and Thursday 1:55 - 4:05 Familiar and comfortable with social media platforms Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills Ability to develop good virtual and graphic designs  Friendly and positive attitude Demonstrated excellence in interpersonal skills, including sensitivity to cultural differences and working in diverse populations Must have completed at least 1 semester at The New School Preferred Qualifications: Understand of international student processes at The New School Experience living or traveling abroad Flexible work schedule Position Hours: 10 hours per week Salary: $18.00 - $21.00 per hour Work Mode: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Application: All applicants are required to submit an application online with submission of a cover letter and resume. Please indicate in your cover letter if you are available to work on Tuesdays and Thursdays from 1:55 to 4:05pm EST weekly as part of your scheduled hours, if hired in this position. Additional Information: Please be sure to include the hours you are able to work.  This position must be able to cover virtual drop-in hours Tues, Thur 2pm - 4pm = 4 hours. The remaining 6 hours can be scheduled based on the Assistant’s availability during ISSS business hours of Mon - Fri 9am - 5pm . For questions, please contact iss@newschool.edu . We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: ISSS Drop-in Advising moderating, Coordinating social media, Instagram posting, Brevo Newsletters, WhatsApp, Assist with coordinating and setup of ISSS events, Marketing ISSS events on Narwhal Nation and social media platforms, Monitor ISSS events email inbox, Generally assisting with other office tasks and special projects as necessary We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Office-Experience-Curator--Office-of-Student-Engagement--OSE--FWS-_JR109166": {
      "title": "Student Office Experience Curator- Office of Student Engagement (OSE)(FWS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 29 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nReporting to the Assistant Director in the Office of Student Engagement, The Student Office Experience Curator will focus on the holistic experience of Student Employees and Leaders that engage and interact with The Office of Student Engagement across the 1st and 2nd Floor of 39 W 13th Street. The Office of Student Engagement (OSE) at The New School supports the university's educational mission by fostering opportunities for meaningful engagement and connections beyond the classroom. OSE encourages students to actively participate in the university community by joining or starting a student organization, engaging in recreational activities, or attending events and programs organized by the Student Activities Board. OSE provides numerous opportunities to get involved on campus and enhance a student’s leadership potential. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist in the planning, promotion, and implementation of various major office leadership events. Support the onboarding and collegiality of new student employees and support the Assistant Director in the holistic training and development of Student Office Assistants. Staff in-person desks across the 1st and 2nd Floor of 29 W 13th Street, providing customer service to in-person visitors, over the phone, or virtually; Must maintain a confidential work environment. Assist in the marketing plan and promotion of OSE Leadership programs, including review and maintenance of websites and social media accounts. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS This position requires a Federal Work-Study Award. This position requires previous employment or student leadership position within or affiliated with the Office of Student Engagement (OSE) PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Ability to creatively problem solve, multitask, and function in a collaborative work environment. Capacity to provide and receive constructive critique. This position frequently needs to traverse multiple levels of stairs and ladders, position oneself into various levels to reach items, and move boxes weighing up to 20 pounds across offices, campus locations, and storage spaces.  WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17-$21/hr  SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS  To be considered: Through the career portal, please provide your financial aid award letter, and a resume. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities:  The Student Office Experience Curator will support the Office of Student Engagement by assisting in the event planning, promotion, and implementation process, In addition, they will support with the onboarding new collogues, and provide customer service in person, over the phone and virtually. ​ We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/HEOP-Academic-Year-Mentor--OCSE-_JR109256": {
      "title": "HEOP Academic Year Mentor (OCSE)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 29 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School Arthur O. Eve Higher Education Opportunity Program is a New York State Education Department–funded program that offers financial, academic, and socio-emotional support for five years of undergraduate study and related educational activities. It is designed specifically for economically and educationally disadvantaged students who might not meet traditional college admissions criteria but demonstrate promise and potential for academic achievement and success.  At The New School, the HEOP office is housed under the Student Affairs  in which they will coordinate, evaluate, and administer academic, and operational activities of the program to foster student growth and success. HEOP Mentors will report to the HEOP Manager. HEOP mentors will support the HEOP program including supporting events from prep to breakdown, assist with administrative tasks, assist HEOP students through email and in person. HEOP Mentors will also contribute to content on social media and assist with visuals for promotional material.  This is a one-academic-year position beginning Fall 26 through Spring 27,  9 hours per week. RESPONSIBILITIES Support the HEOP program with event preparation including but not limited to transporting materials, decorating & arranging spaces, and event breakdown/clean-up Perform administrative duties related to the functional operation of the HEOP Space Communicate important information regularly to first-year HEOP students via email  Contribute content on the official @NewSchoolStudentLife Instagram page in accordance with Student Success and university guidelines and policies. Assist with creating visual materials for college fairs, school visits, HEOP and New School Firsts specific events  Assist HEOP students with challenges they may be experiencing by providing support and guidance Participate in the assessment efforts of HEOP by assisting in the administration of surveys to students, as well as filling out assessment tools aimed at understanding learning outcomes MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Passionate about access/opportunity programs Be open-minded, and culturally competent Takes initiative, works independently and collaboratively, strong work ethic   Meets deadlines promptly, communicates clearly, and asks for help when needed Uses good judgment, thinks critically and thoughtfully Must have a cumulative GPA of 3.0 or higher Assist students with the development of their study skills  Knowledge of social media platforms such as but not limited to IG, Facebook, etc. Experience with researching, brainstorming, ideating, and creating programming and marketing materials Must be able to lift boxes weighing up to 35lbs  Must be available to attend in person training on September 1st WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE $18 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: HEOP Mentors will report to the HEOP Manager. HEOP mentors will support the HEOP program including supporting events from prep to breakdown, assist with administrative tasks, assist HEOP students through email and in person. HEOP Mentors will also contribute to content on social media and assist with visuals for promotional material.  We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/FWS-Student-Assistant---Lang-Admission_JR109248": {
      "title": "FWS Student Assistant - Lang Admission",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 29 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Student Assistant - Eugene Lang College Admission plays an important role in supporting the Undergraduate Admission team by serving as a knowledgeable and welcoming resource for prospective students and families. Working collaboratively with the admission staff, this position assists with responding to inquiries, maintaining digital records, supporting recruitment initiatives, and representing the student experience through one-on-one conversations and admission events. The Student Assistant helps create a personalized and informative experience for prospective students while contributing to the daily operations of the admission office. The successful candidate is an organized, dependable, and enthusiastic communicator who enjoys working both independently and collaboratively. The ideal candidate demonstrates strong writing and interpersonal skills, exercises sound judgment when handling confidential information, and is eager to share their experience as a current Eugene Lang student. This position reports to the Assistant Director of Admission. Priority consideration will be given to current Eugene Lang College students. Students from all academic disciplines are encouraged to apply, provided they have a strong understanding of the Lang student experience and academic programs. Responsibilities: Respond to prospective student and family inquiries through the Lang Admission email inbox and other communication channels. Meet individually with prospective students and families to share the Eugene Lang student experience and answer questions about campus life and academics. Represent Eugene Lang College at undergraduate admission events, including information sessions, open houses, admitted student programs, and virtual events. Draft and maintain email templates and other communication resources for frequently asked questions. Assist with administrative projects, data entry, and digital record maintenance using university systems. Support the creation of social media and other digital content that promotes Eugene Lang College and student life. Complete additional project-based assignments and departmental duties as needed. Minimum Qualifications:  Current undergraduate student in good academic standing. Strong understanding of the Eugene Lang College student experience and academic programs. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Customer service, peer leadership, or student-facing experience. Ability to work independently while managing multiple priorities and meeting deadlines. Proficiency with Microsoft Office and Google Workspace. Ability to exercise discretion and maintain confidentiality when handling applicant information. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Preferred Qualifications:  Previous experience working in admissions, higher education, student leadership, orientation, or another customer service environment. Experience creating content for social media or digital communications. Familiarity with Slate or other CRM/database systems. Public speaking or presentation experience. Hourly Rate :  $17.00 per hour Work Mode:  Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Please upload all Required Applicant Documents to be considered:  Resume/CV Cover Letter Federal Work Study Award We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Assist the Lang Admission Office with prospective student outreach, inquiry management, event support, and administrative operations. Serve as a student ambassador by sharing firsthand experiences, creating communication materials, and supporting initiatives that strengthen recruitment and engagement. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/No-Campus-Location/Director-of-Payroll_JR109261": {
      "title": "Director of Payroll Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 29 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately  9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Human Resources’ office promotes activities which enhance the quality of work life and heighten the sense of community. The office provides guidance, administers a myriad of services to employees with continual efficiency and effectiveness, and handles employee problems with care and professionalism.  Our open-door policy welcomes all employees encouraging them to fully utilize the office’s services. Employees' matters are treated with respect, care, and confidentiality. Professionalism, capable management, collaboration, and progressive engagement are the cornerstones of office operations.  Reporting to the Assistant Vice President for HR Operations, the Director of Payroll Services leads a team of experienced payroll professionals and directs the payroll function for faculty, administrative staff, and student workers. In addition to ensuring that the university meets its financial commitment to employees by providing accurate and timely salary and wage payments, this position collaborates with Information Technology/Workday Solutions Group and other Human Resources functional areas to implement strategic projects aimed to enhance the employee experience. The successful candidate will be dedicated to providing accessible and transparent payroll processes for employees, supporting a culture of care, and fostering an equitable and inclusive workplace culture.  The Director of Payroll Services needs to be a collaborative partner that demonstrates both personal and professional integrity. They are transformative, collaborative, flexible and creative but can also be a hands-on problem solver with a track record of designing and delivering customer-focused services modeled on best practices.  CORE CAPABILITIES AND PRIMARY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Payroll Operations & Systems Strategy Direct end-to-end weekly and biweekly payroll processing across faculty, staff, and student workers, ensuring accuracy, timeliness, and total compliance within the Workday Payroll ecosystem.  Serve as the primary technical and functional Workday Payroll subject matter expert (SME), & managing backend configurations.  Manages processing in the university’s Workday Payroll system, and plans and manages the implementation of Workday system changes and updates. Oversee strategic vendor relationships with ADP for tax filing, wage garnishments, and regulatory processing, ensuring seamless integration between Workday and ADP systems.  Updates earning and deduction codes as needed, and coordinates with the university’s Finance office to update GL posting rules. Architect and maintain inbound/outbound integration files between Workday Payroll, benefit providers, financial platforms, and third-party vendors to safeguard end-to-end data integrity.  Manages payment requests for garnishments. Lead end-to-end Payroll testing strategies, including tenant updates, regression testing, semi-annual Workday feature releases, and custom integration validation.  Initiates and controls W-2 processing and reconciling. Compliance and Regulatory Oversight Develops and maintains processes to ensure best practices, internal controls, and compliance with local, state, and federal regulations and the University’s policies and procedures. Oversees Non-Resident Alien processing for faculty, staff, and student workers to ensure appropriate W-2/1042-S reporting and tax withholding. Remains current with United States and New York City and State payroll-related regulations and tax laws. Issue Resolution and Data Integrity Proactively identifies, researches, analyzes, and resolves payroll-related issues and discrepancies. Collaborates with University departments to ensure system and data integrity in the university’s Workday system and all related systems. Leadership and Staff Development Lead, mentor, and empower the payroll team, cultivating a collaborative, continuous-learning environment that promotes professional growth, high performance, and operational excellence  Drive large-scale strategic payroll projects, managing cross-functional initiatives from initial scoping and business process re-engineering through implementation and post-go-live optimization.  Streamline end-to-end year-end processing, directing W-2, 1042-S, and multi-state/local tax reconciliation processes with high precision and error-free execution. ​ Audit and Financial Collaboration Works collaboratively with the university’s Finance office in leading preparation of payroll information for the university’s annual audit and other audits as needed. Time and Attendance Management Maintains non-union and union time off and time tracking processes, including system process improvements. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum Qualifications: Seven years of payroll management experience in a mid-sized or large organization Strong Excel and other Microsoft Office application skills Workday Payroll  Demonstrated effective communication and reporting skills Bachelor’s Degree required Preferred Qualifications: Familiarity with Sprintax Calculus software Five or more years of experience with Workday payroll processing Experience managing Payroll in a university environment Experience working with ADP for payroll and tax processing Certified Payroll Professional certification WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. SALARY RANGE:   $120,000 - $130,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness  Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/OCSE-Senior-Student-Assistant-Specialist---Welcome-Center_JR109244-1": {
      "title": "OCSE Senior Student Assistant Specialist - Welcome Center",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-06\nPosted: Posted 30 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School is seeking enthusiastic, outgoing, and dependable graduate students to serve as Senior Student Assistant Specialists in the University Welcome Center . Senior Student Assistant Specialists play an important role in welcoming prospective students, families, and other visitors to The New School and helping them learn more about our academic programs, student experience, and admission process. They lead in-person campus tours and support virtual and on-campus recruitment programs and events, serving as knowledgeable and authentic representatives of the New School student experience. In addition to these visitor-facing responsibilities, Senior Student Assistant Specialists provide operational and peer leadership within the Welcome Center . They assist with student staff onboarding, training, and scheduling; visitor communications; recruitment logistics; and special projects. They may also help develop student-focused content for Welcome Center social media, online admitted student communities, and the New School Parent and Family Hub. This position is ideal for a graduate student who enjoys working with people, demonstrates strong organizational and communication skills, and is comfortable balancing public-facing responsibilities with administrative and operational work. Senior Student Assistant Specialists are responsible for: Serving as a welcoming, knowledgeable, and professional representative of The New School and creating a positive first impression for prospective students, families, and other campus visitors. Leading engaging in-person campus tours and supporting virtual and on-campus admission programs and events. Maintaining current knowledge of The New School's academic programs, student services and resources, campus facilities, and admission processes and requirements. Assisting with the scheduling and coordination of student tour guides, private group visits, and other Welcome Center activities. Supporting student staff onboarding, peer training, and ongoing professional development. Providing on-site operational and administrative leadership during select weekday and Saturday visit programs. Assisting with visitor communications and maintaining accurate campus visit and participation data. Coordinating recruitment material shipments and maintaining Welcome Center materials and inventory. Contributing content and student perspectives to Welcome Center social media, online admitted student communities, and parent and family engagement platforms. Supporting special projects and other Admission and Welcome Center initiatives as assigned. ​ Qualifications: Must be a currently enrolled New School graduate student. Previous administrative or office experience required. Strong interpersonal, communication, and organizational skills. Comfortable speaking to and engaging with individuals and groups. Ability to provide accurate information while communicating in a warm, approachable, and professional manner. Demonstrated reliability, attention to detail, and ability to manage multiple responsibilities. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively as part of a team. Interest in mentoring and supporting fellow student employees. Familiarity with The New School's academic programs, resources, and student experience preferred. Pay Rate: $21/hour Work Mode: Hybrid/Split: Employees hired for this position have a regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (e.g., 1–4 times per week) and also work some of the time remotely. Because of the visitor-facing nature of this position, the majority of scheduled hours will take place in person at the University Welcome Center, 2 West 13th Street. #LI-DNI The New School is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Senior Student Assistant Specialists play an important role in welcoming prospective students, families, and other visitors to The New School and helping them learn more about our academic programs, student experience, and admission process. They lead in-person campus tours and support virtual and on-campus recruitment programs and events, serving as knowledgeable and authentic representatives of the New School student experience. In addition to these visitor-facing responsibilities, Senior Student Assistant Specialists provide operational and peer leadership within the Welcome Center. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/D---79-Fifth-Avenue/Admissions-Counselor_JR109233": {
      "title": "Admissions Counselor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-06\nPosted: Posted 30 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Admission Counselor reports to an Associate Director of Admission within the Office of Enrollment Management. This role is responsible for the recruitment and enrollment of students for both undergraduate and graduate programs across all colleges at The New School, including Parsons School of Design, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, the College of Performing Arts, and The New School for Social Research. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES   Key Responsibilities Comprehensive Recruitment & Travel : Traveling extensively across the U.S. and internationally to represent all university programs at high schools, college fairs, and professional conferences. Admissions & Holistic Review : Evaluating a wide range of applications and academic credentials to determine eligibility for the university’s diverse degree offerings. Interdisciplinary Collaboration : Serving as a liaison between the Office of Admission and faculty across various departments—from design and social sciences to humanities and the arts—to coordinate application reviews and student yield efforts. University-Wide Event Planning : Assisting with the coordination of large-scale on-campus recruitment events, open houses, and orientation sessions for the entire university community. Strategic Communication : Managing outreach to prospective students and families across all disciplines via phone, email, and social media to communicate the unique value of a New School education. Specialty and Project Teams : Lead/serve on department wide speciality and project teams, focused on admission events, communications, recruitment planning and marketing, and other key enrollment services, as assigned.   QUALIFICATIONS Minimum Qualifications : A Bachelor’s degree, excellent public speaking and interpersonal skills, a valid driver’s license, and a passport for international travel. Preferred Qualifications : Experience working in a multi-disciplinary higher education environment and proficiency with CRM databases (e.g., Slate). For this position we are seeking a candidate with experience in visual art and design, having either achieved a BFA or worked as an admission counselor in art and design admission ​ WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. SALARY RANGE $66,300 - $70,500 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include:   Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP)   Financial Wellness  Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan   Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable)   Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents   Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Director-of-Alumni-and-Volunteer-Engagement_JR109230": {
      "title": "Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-06\nPosted: Posted 30 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world.   POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement provides strategic and operational leadership for The New School’s university-wide alumni engagement program. The Director develops and advances a comprehensive strategy that strengthens alumni connection, supports institutional priorities, and creates meaningful opportunities for alumni to engage with students, faculty, fellow alumni, and the university.   Reporting to the Assistant Vice President for Alumni & Community Engagement, the Director sets goals and priorities and supervises staff responsible for school-based and university-wide engagement initiatives. The Director directly leads alumni engagement for Parsons and Performing Arts while ensuring that alumni across all New School schools and programs receive coordinated, relevant, and inclusive opportunities for lifelong connection.   The Director oversees regional and affinity engagement, reunions, events, communications, digital communities, volunteer leadership, and data-informed outreach. The role establishes shared standards, manages resources, assesses performance, and collaborates closely with Development and Alumni Engagement colleagues, academic leadership, faculty, and other university partners.   Alumni Engagement Develop and implement a comprehensive university-wide alumni engagement strategy aligned with institutional and Development and Alumni Engagement priorities while recognizing the distinct identities of The New School’s schools and programs  Establish annual goals, priorities, work plans, timelines, and measures of success for the alumni engagement team. Oversee the strategic development and execution of an annual slate of in-person and virtual alumni initiatives, including regional, industry, affinity, reunion, volunteer, and community-building opportunities. Expand alumni participation across career and life stages, geographic regions, industries, affinities, interests, and levels of prior engagement. Manage alumni engagement resources and align investments with strategic priorities. Direct Reunion strategy, planning, communications, and execution in partnership with Development and Alumni Engagement and school-based colleagues. Lead the development and management of the Executive Alumni Council of The New School and provide strategic direction for Alumni Council committees. Manage relationships with alumni leaders and volunteers, supporting their involvement and deepening their connection to The New School. Develop alumni leaders and volunteers who serve as informed ambassadors and advocates for The New School. Partner with student-facing offices to strengthen alumni-to-student engagement and create pathways that support the transition from student to active alum. Build relationships with industry leaders, employers, and external partners that expand engagement opportunities for alumni and students. Represent Alumni Engagement on university-wide committees and special initiatives and build partnerships that strengthen alumni and student engagement.   Staff Leadership and University Partnerships Supervise alumni engagement staff, including setting team priorities, coordinating workflows, establishing expectations, and ensuring accountability for program execution and results. Promote collaboration and information sharing across the Development and Alumni Engagement division. Partner with deans, academic leaders, faculty, and school-based colleagues to identify priorities and advance alumni engagement plans. Build and maintain productive relationships across the university to support coordinated alumni engagement.   Communications, Data, and Assessment Guide segmented outreach strategies based on alumni interests, industries, affiliations, geographic locations, career stages, and engagement preferences. Direct the strategy, messaging, and development of alumni communications in partnership with the Associate Director of Alumni and Community Engagement Communications Establish standards for the accurate and timely entry, review, and maintenance of alumni engagement information in Salesforce and lead efforts to improve data quality and completeness. Establish performance measures for volunteer engagement, mentoring, programming, digital activity, communications, data quality, and program outcomes. Use performance data and alumni feedback to assess effectiveness, identify engagement gaps, refine audience strategies, allocate resources, and guide future planning.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Five to seven years of progressively responsible professional experience in higher education, advancement, alumni relations, or constituent engagement. Strong presentation and public-speaking skills, with the ability to represent the university effectively in varied settings.  Demonstrated record of designing, growing, and evaluating programs, communications, and events, including large-scale initiatives. Exceptional organizational, verbal, written, interpersonal, and relationship-management skills, with the ability to engage effectively with varied audiences. Strong leadership and collaboration skills, with the ability to build trust, establish credibility, and work effectively across departments, academic units, and leadership teams. Sound judgment, discretion, diplomacy, and the ability to navigate complex organizational relationships.  Entrepreneurial and solutions-oriented approach, with the ability to anticipate needs, adapt to changing priorities, and creatively address challenges.  Experience using data and performance measures to evaluate engagement, improve programs, and guide strategic decisions. Demonstrated proficiency with CRM systems, online community platforms, event registration tools, email marketing systems, or comparable technology. Experience developing and managing segmented communications and digital engagement strategies. Strong commitment to The New School’s mission and to accessible, inclusive, and audience-centered engagement. Availability to work some evenings and weekends and travel occasionally, as required. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-Onsite SALARY RANGE: $108,000- $112,000 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact.   BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers   We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant---Making-Center_JR108986": {
      "title": "Student Assistant - Making Center",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-06\nPosted: Posted 30 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School of Design Parsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in various shops within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open work spaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines, and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. Duties for each space vary slightly, but entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment and/or monitoring making spaces and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  RESPONSIBILITIES Report for shift on time and primarily assist Making Center Staff with reviewing priority of tasks in the shop including: upkeep, organization, and maintenance. Work in WebCheckout providing students appropriate tools for their projects as well as process materials for the store. Proper opening and closing of the shops, as assigned by Making Center Staff, including opening the space and unlocking resources and turning on / logging into computers. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent customer service skills.  Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users.  Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes. Attention to detail.  Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds. Ability to work without close supervision or alone. Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.). Physical ability to lift items.  Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.  WORK MODE On-campus position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work that is not feasible, or not in the organization's best interest, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE   $17.00 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Report for shift on time and primarily assist Making Center Staff with reviewing priority of tasks in the shop including: upkeep, organization, and maintenance. Work in WebCheckout providing students appropriate tools for their projects as well as process materials for the store. Proper opening and closing of the shops, as assigned by Making Center Staff, including opening the space and unlocking resources and turning on / logging into computers. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Operations-Hub-Student-Assistant_JR109209": {
      "title": "Operations Hub Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-04\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Lang/NSSR operations hub is seeking a student employee to support administrative tasks in the Lang/NSSR operations hub.  RESPONSIBILITIES Processing honorarium requests for guest speakers  Maintain and update expense tracking spreadsheets Update and prepare office signage and other materials as needed Providing general administrative and operational support for the Lang/NSSR joint operations hub Assisting with additional office tasks and special projects as assigned by team MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Registered student Ability to work 10 -15 hours per week onsite/hybrid Experience with google suite WORK MODE: Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17 - $19   We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.   Responsibilities: Student Assistant will support the Lang/NSSR Joint Operations Hub with a variety of administrative and operational tasks. Responsibilities include preparing honorarium requests for guest lecturers, inputting and maintaining expense tracking information, creating signage and materials as needed, and assisting with other administrative duties that arise to support daily office operations. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Student-Assistant--FWS----Dean-s-Office-Eugene-Lang_JR109210": {
      "title": "Student Assistant (FWS) - Dean's Office Eugene Lang",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-03\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThere are currently several short-term (1-year) Federal Work Study employment positions open in the Dean's Office at Eugene Lang College. Ideal applicants must possess a positive attitude; an ability to work well with staff, faculty, and students; attention to detail; schedule flexibility (especially early mornings); and punctuality. Student workers will be expected to maintain a professional attitude and treat all materials and information they encounter with the utmost discretion. Student workers will assist with department events, marketing, and logistics as needed. Candidates will be paid through the federal work study program. As such, only students actively enrolled in the work-study program are eligible for this role.  RESPONSIBILITIES General Administrative Position Duties and Responsibilities:  Greet visitors and answer phones: direct callers and visitors to appropriate sources or information  Assist Lang College staff in short-term and long-term projects  Maintain Dean's office files and assist in office communication  Sort mail, make copies/PDFs, file and shred documents  Email staff/faculty and check ELC Dean e-mail address as needed  Deliver mail and packages to other offices as needed (on and off campus)  Set up and organize for office events  Stock and maintain supply rooms, copy rooms, basement printers and faculty resource room Maintain general neatness and organization of Dean's office, including the kitchen pantry and conference room  Ensure the Millimeter Reading Room is organized and tidy Design promotional materials (posters, flyers, PDFs, JPEG, graphics) geared to students for our department events as needed Distribute posters/flyers Greet and register event guests  Proof copy and branding for marketing materials and digital event promo Transcribe audio or edit transcriptions as needed Assist Senior Secretaries with other general office duties as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Adaptable and enthusiastic attitude, strong work ethic, proactive stance  Self-starter, able to work independently Able to take direction and responsibility for assignments, follows through on jobs until completion Professional demeanor and strong customer service skills Good communication skills Strong organization skills and interest in office administration  Proficiency in Google Apps, Microsoft Office Suite, and internet research  Basic understanding of design principles PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with data entry and working in Excel databases  Understanding of University divisions and programs  Strong written and verbal communication skills   Experience with flyer design (Canva or comparable) a plus Experience with Adobe Suite (Photoshop/Illustrator/Acrobat/Publisher) a plus WORK MODE : On-Campus Position ,Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $18 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.   Responsibilities: The student assistant greets visitors to the 3rd floor of Eugene Lang College and provides assistance as needed. The position also supports Dean’s Office staff and faculty as needed and monitors the copy room/mailboxes/supply closet. Additional duties include posting flyers/notices around the 11th and 12th Street buildings as well as other TNS buildings. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/Student-Assistant---Lang-Economics_JR109223": {
      "title": "Student Assistant - Lang/Economics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-03\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education. We are seeking 1 student to help with administrative tasks as described below. RESPONSIBILITIES Greet students, faculty, and visitors at the department front desk, providing courteous and professional assistance.  Replenish paper and toner maintain department copiers to ensure uninterrupted operation.  Assist with department-sponsored events by setting up rooms, welcoming guests, and managing check-in sheets.  Run campus errands, including posting flyers, delivering materials and supplies, and collecting departmental mail.  Perform light administrative and clerical duties as needed to support daily department operations, such as photocopying, scanning books for professors, etc. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Must be eligible for Federal Work Study- please provide letter Reliable, punctual, and organized Comfortable using standard office equipment (copiers, scanners, etc.) Professional communication and a collaborative attitude Ability to maintain confidentiality and follow instructions carefully Ability to help with events that start at 6pm (on either M, W or R). WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI .  Hourly Rate : $19 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.   Instructions: Make sure to include your resume, cover letter and FWS letter confirming your funding to be a FWS Student assistant with your application. Incomplete applications won’t be considered.   Responsibilities: Lang Economics is seeking a dependable, friendly, and organized Student Assistant to support the daily operations of the department office. The Student Assistant will greet students, faculty, staff, and visitors at the front desk, providing courteous and professional assistance while serving as the first point of contact for the department. Responsibilities include replenishing paper and toner and helping maintain department copiers to ensure uninterrupted operation, assisting with department-sponsored events by setting up rooms, welcoming guests, and managing check-in sheets, and running campus errands such as posting flyers, delivering materials and supplies, and collecting departmental mail. The Student Assistant will also perform light administrative and clerical duties as needed to support daily office operations, including photocopying, scanning books and documents for faculty, filing, and completing other office tasks as assigned. The ideal candidate is reliable, organized, customer service-oriented, and able to work independently while maintaining a professional and welcoming environment. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/University-Center-Library-Student-Assistant_JR109169": {
      "title": "University Center Library Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-03\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nFWS Student Worker Library Assistant University Center Library  Located in the heart of New York City, The New School has been a pioneering force in education, creativity, and innovation since its founding in 1919. With a progressive, globally recognized interdisciplinary model, the university is home to a diverse community of thousands of students from more than 100 countries. We offer over 135 degree and diploma programs across our esteemed colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The New School Libraries are seeking 60+ Student Worker Library Assistants who will support the university's mission of preparing students to understand, contribute to, and succeed in a rapidly changing society, thereby making the world a better and more just place. Our work contributes to this mission by offering consistent and reliable service to all students, faculty, and staff of The New School. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with supervisor Monitor patrons entering and exiting the library and be ready to assist them at the circulation desk Check-in and check-out library materials to patrons at the desk using library software Stacks maintenance including shelving, in-housing books, shelf reading, etc. Answer the telephone Help patrons find items in the stacks and in the library catalog Assist patrons with library printing Other library-related tasks such as scanning books, paging books, preparing reserve books, and other duties assigned by staff Assist with the opening or closing procedures in the library depending on shift time QUALIFICATIONS Excellent customer service skills  Ability to understand and follow verbal and written instructions and communicate clearly with library users  Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail  Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Physical ability to lift items and push loaded book carts. Accommodations can be made if needed. WORK MODE: This position is in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE: $17.00-$18.00 per hour  WHY JOIN US? The New School Library plays a central role in supporting the university's mission of preparing students to understand, contribute to, and succeed in a rapidly changing society, thus making the world a better and more just place. Our work contributes to this mission by offering consistent and reliable service to all students, faculty, and staff of The New School.  We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Students provide excellent customer service by assisting patrons with circulation services, locating library resources, and answering general inquiries. Support daily library operations through collection maintenance, opening and closing procedures, and other assigned tasks to ensure a safe, organized, and welcoming environment. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Student-Lab-Assistant---Lang_JR109208": {
      "title": "Student Lab Assistant - Lang",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-03\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe Student Assistant will provide essential support to the computer lab by ensuring the lab's smooth operation and readiness for student, faculty, and staff use. This role requires a proactive and responsible individual who can perform tasks independently while effectively communicating with lab users and the administration team. Key Responsibilities: Ensure all lab equipment is functional, including computers, printers, and other devices. Monitor and restock supplies such as paper, toner, and other materials as needed. Assist lab users with technical issues or general inquiries. Communicate equipment malfunctions, supply shortages, or other concerns to the Associate Director of Administration at Lang and IT support. Complete tasks independently, ensuring the lab is well-maintained and operational at all times. Qualifications: Ability to work independently and manage time efficiently. Strong communication skills to relay issues and requests to management and IT support. Basic understanding of technical equipment and willingness to learn troubleshooting processes. Punctual and reliable, with a proactive approach to resolving issues in the lab. Workmode :   On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly rate: $19 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Student Assistant supports the day-to-day operation of the computer lab by ensuring all equipment is functional and the space is ready for student, faculty, and staff use. They assist users with basic technical issues, answer general questions, and monitor supplies to keep the lab fully stocked. The role requires communicating equipment problems and supply needs to the Associate Director of Administration and IT support in a timely manner. Successful candidates are dependable, able to work independently, possess strong communication skills, and have a willingness to learn basic technical troubleshooting. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Part-Time-Teaching-Staff_JR109164": {
      "title": "Part-Time Teaching Staff",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-24\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The School of Jazz & Contemporary Music at The New School is seeking highly qualified candidates to join its roster of Part-Time Teaching Staff. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Provide dedicated mentorship and instruction to students within the School of Jazz & Contemporary Music Serve as a substitute instructor within the School of Jazz & Contemporary Music Assess student learning outcomes and aid student success and individual artistic growth Submit grades in a timely manner MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Demonstrated success as a performing artist, with significant professional accomplishments and accolades 2-3 years of teaching experience with a variety of teaching methods and curricular perspectives, preferably in a College, University, and/or Conservatory Ability to work with diverse students and colleagues, fostering inclusive classroom environments Proven track record with achieving learning outcomes as defined by curriculum and course syllabi PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor’s, advanced, or terminal degree in the field of specialty or equivalent experience (BM, MM, DMA, PH.D or equivalent) WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE Jazz Private Lessons - $156.00/hour Part-Time Teaching Staff Substitute - $120.00/hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Part-Time-Faculty--Local-802_JR109165-1": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty, Local 802",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-24\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The School of Jazz & Contemporary Music at the College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking highly qualified candidates to join its roster of Part-Time Faculty. This is a Local 802 - American Federation of Musicians unit position as per the collective bargaining agreement between AFM Local 802 and the University. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teaching graduate and/or undergraduate students within a defined field of expertise Developing and managing course syllabi and ensuring that syllabi meet Program and School standards Planning and creating lectures, discussions, and course assignments Assessing student learning outcomes and aiding student success Submitting grades in a timely manner MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree or equivalent experience  Demonstrated success as a creative, teaching, and/or performing artist, with significant professional accomplishments and accolades 2-3 years of teaching experience with a variety of teaching methods and curricular perspectives, preferably in a College, University, and/or Conservatory Ability to work with diverse students and colleagues, fostering inclusive classroom settings Proven track record with achieving learning outcomes PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS An advanced, or terminal degree in the field of specialty (MM, MA, DMA, PH.D or equivalent) WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE Local 802 Part-Time Faculty are compensated at the following minimum hourly rates for the duration of the 2026-27 Academic Year: Jazz Private Lessons - $156.00/hour Jazz Classroom - $156.00/hour Rates are subject to increase per contractual obligations as outlined in the Collective Bargaining Agreement between Local 802 and The New School. WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position We look forward to receiving your application!"
