    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E3-Light-Lab-Student-Assistant_JR109161": {
      "title": "E3 Light Lab Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-24\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the E4 Wood, Metal, Tool Checkout, and Light lab within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.  The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.    RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor  Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them  Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls / emails professionally and courteously  Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests  Help students checkout appropriate tools for their projects. Inspect tools upon return and address issues such as damage, missing parts, or cleanliness. Maintain tool organization within the shop Using ladders and assisting in the installation of lighting equipment (specific to Light Lab Student Employees) Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:  Opening and closing the shop as assigned (unlocking resources, logging into computers, etc.). Processing / recycling materials. Receiving and organizing deliveries. Tracking and moving inventory. Assist with store support, including: Procuring purchased items for students and faculty.  Processing and preparing materials (including but not limited to wood, metal, plaster, clay and alginate). Counting and tracking inventory. Moving and organizing materials (up to 25 lbs, occasionally up to 50 lbs). Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery  Replace tool parts in kits and sets. Restock consumables. Change blades and other machine components as needed. Daily maintenance and cleaning of machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, including: Dust control including vacuuming and mopping Restocking PPE Clearing surfaces and resetting machines  Complete special projects as assigned  Participate in training sessions and staff meetings  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users  Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail  Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs  Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests , assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,  and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Sign-Language--Tutor_JR109140": {
      "title": "Sign Language  Tutor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-24\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe language tutoring position is responsible to assist other students in one-on-one sessions of thirty minutes out-of-class. Tutors will answer questions about course content taught in class including grammar explanations, pronunciation of vowels sounds, diphthongs and consonant combinations that change according to the language. Tutors can review student’s homework and guide them through it assisting in a couple of sentences, help in student’s presentations preparation and written and oral assignments. Tutors will promote language courses after they accomplish the tutoring administrative, pedagogical and technological training sessions, they need to participate in cultural events organize in class according to their time availability. Tutors report to the faculty tutoring coordinator.   Pursuant to the CBA, Academic Student Workers (ASWs) shall not be required to perform a service outside of the listing of required duties as noted above. ASWs are encouraged to notify their supervisors and contact person as soon as practicable during the academic term if they anticipate their total work assignment will exceed the terms of the assignment. RESPONSIBILITIES Attend the administrative, pedagogical and technological tutoring training sessions for this objective they need to review all digital material sent previously for the training.  Perform class visits of no more than fifteen minutes in every language class offered and introduce themselves. Explain students how to sign in STARFISH for a tutoring session and the services provided including the extra-credit benefit when performing four tutoring sessions (some language classes require only three sessions, it varies). Review, explain and guide students about material taught in class such as grammar structure, pronunciation of vowels, diphthongs and consonant combination sounds. Guide students by reviewing a couple of homework exercises. Tutors can assist students in their written and oral assignments, mini-projects and exam material review. Assist students in the oral and written language used for class presentations providing corrections if needed and constructive feedback. Facilitate interactive speaking and listening exercises to build a real-world conversation skill. Contact language professors to participate in cultural class events allowing students to integrate in foreign language culture customs and social conventions. Set up their STARFISH office hours according to the university academic calendar and remove it during holidays. Tutoring is only offer at the university. Submit their online timesheets for payment through My-Day application.  Communicate immediately when a tutoring session has been cancelled. Contact the student and the program coordinator to inform about it. Inform the tutoring coordinator if students are not at tending sessions. Tutoring is a free service and if a student does not attend the session their tutoring privilege can be removed. Register all sessions performed in the tutoring excel track sheet provided every semester, indicating the day and time the session was performed and the student name too. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Native or near native fluency in the target language is desired, alongside strong English communication skills to clearly explain complex language rules. Experience as a prior tutor in high school or other institutions in the country or abroad is a plus. Strong and interpersonal skills such as patience, empathy, respect and tolerance to students diverse opinions. The tutoring job position is opened to students that are currently enrolled at the New School University only. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Applicants with advanced knowledge of literature, grammar, writing and speaking in the target language is a plus.  WORK MODE: Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary, in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : U$29.20 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Tutors are responsible for setting up their office hours- calendar in STARFISH and perform class visits in the language class for no more than fifteen minutes. They need to attend the tutoring appointments and cancel them when a problem/emergency occurs. Cancelation needs to be done forty-eight hours ahead letting the student and department know about it. Tutors need to submit their timesheets for payment every week (Fridays at noon). We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Mannes-Sounds-Festival-Assistant_JR109067": {
      "title": "Temporary Mannes Sounds Festival Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-24\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The College of Performing Arts at The New School seeks a Temporary Mannes Sounds Festival Assistant to support the Mannes Sounds Festival. The Temporary Mannes Sounds Festival Assistant will support the administration, operations, and production coordination for all concert presentations. The position will report to the Artistic Director of the Mannes Sounds Festival. In 1999, Pavlina Dokovska, chair of the Mannes School of Music’s Piano Department, founded the Mannes Sounds Festival and serves today as its artistic director. The festival presents hundreds of Mannes students each year in more than 20 concerts at iconic venues across New York City and on the campus of The New School. The Mannes Sounds Festival also features collaborations between the Mannes School of Music, The School of Drama, The School of Jazz and Contemporary Music and Parsons School of Design. In addition, the festival presents master classes and lectures by distinguished faculty members and guest artists. The Mannes Sounds Festival provides unparalleled opportunities for students of the College of Performing Arts to be presented at a variety of notable venues and professional settings. The festival is also a key fundraising vehicle for Mannes School of Music. This is an FLSA nonexempt hourly (on average, up to 19 hours per week) position with a desired start date of 8/10/2026 and end date of 6/30/2027. Working hours will vary depending upon the Mannes Sounds Festival’s season calendar and departmental need. The position may be renewed pending available funding and continued need.  CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Event Production  Support festival season programming and logistics, communicating with internal teams and off-site venue contacts to identify and address all production needs Coordinate artistic planning and with the Director, event curators, student performers, guest artists, and others Advance all Mannes Sounds productions. This includes but is not limited to creation of show documentation, contact sheets, rehearsals schedules, runs of show, cast and crew calls, rehearsal reports, and stage plots  Serve as on-site producer for festival events Lead and/or attend production planning meetings as needed Support pre-event season planning, and post-event season closeout as needed Event Promotion & Donor Communications Generate festival programs including creating and/or reviewing program copy, and overseeing printing and distribution Assist the event promotions team at the College of Performing Arts to promote the festival through social media, mailchimp, flyers, posters, etc. Collate mailing lists to prepare targeted communications and personalized invitations to donors Generate document to track and secure donor RSVPs for all scheduled events on a semesterly basis Miscellaneous Additional duties as assigned Provide administrative support to the Artistic Director Support additional projects related to the Mannes Sounds Festival MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience in arts administration or event production Ability to leverage University platforms including Splash, 25Live, Tableau, Google Suite, and MyDay to complete duties  Demonstrated commitment to Equity, Inclusion, Social Justice, and the transformative power of the arts and education Excellent oral and written communication skills; can present complex information to various audiences Capability to manage competing priorities, navigate sensitive situations, and communicate with professionalism and tact. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience supporting student-facing initiatives that foster artistic and creative growth in a higher education environment WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Frequent nights and weekends for planning and event management will be required. This position will work primarily throughout the duration of the academic year (August through May) – additional opportunities for work during the summer months will be determined and confirmed with advance notice if needed.  Hybrid work arrangements will be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. SALARY RANGE $25.00-28.00/hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit both a resume and cover letter outlining their interest, skills, and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-New-York-String-Orchestra-Seminar-Intern_JR109068": {
      "title": "Temporary New York String Orchestra Seminar Intern",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-24\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The New School’s College of Performing Arts is seeking three to four highly qualified Temporary New York String Orchestra Seminar Interns to support The New School Concerts Department’s 2026 New York String Orchestra Seminar (NYSOS). Reporting to the Director of New School Concerts, during the eleven days of the NYSOS, and two paid advanced training sessions to be scheduled around intern availability, interns will learn administrative and operations skills related to arts administration through hands-on experiences and small group discussions during the nationally acclaimed pre-professional orchestral training program. For more than twenty years, the New York String Orchestra Seminar has offered internships for students interested in learning about operations, programming, and finance in the non-profit live music performance field. This internship is an ideal experiential learning opportunity for those pursuing studies in arts management; student musicians considering transitioning to music administration; and those who intend to pursue entrepreneurial-hybrid careers combining roles as creative artists and administrators. NYSOS interns stay in the same accommodations, and share meals with the members of the Orchestra; local transportation costs are included. Interns must pay for and arrange their own transportation to and from New York City. Visit https://www.newschool.edu/new-york-string-orchestra-seminar for more information about the New York String Orchestra Seminar. This is an FLSA nonexempt hourly (up to 35hrs/week) with a desired start date of November 30, 2026, and an end date of December 31, 2026.  Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Support the program director in administrative and operational duties Serve as on-site liaison with hotel staff Attend all learning sessions to which assigned Represent the NYSOS professionally Additional responsibilities as assigned to advance internship learning goals for participants QUALIFICATIONS Demonstrated interest in live performing arts Professional, educational, or volunteer experience in live performing arts Currently enrolled undergraduate or graduate student in an accredited institution or equivalent Availability all day each day December 18 - 28, 2026 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Orchestral and chamber music background Experience in live performing arts administration, production, or operations WORK MODE This position is in-person and is based on and off-campus to support the rehearsals and performances of the New York String Orchestra Seminar participants. #LI-Onsite SALARY RANGE $17.00/hour   WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume and cover letter outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/FWS-Student-Assistant---Content-Creator--Admission-Communications-_JR109123": {
      "title": "FWS Student Assistant - Content Creator (Admission Communications)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-24\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School is looking for an enthusiastic, responsible, creative individual with a Federal Work Study Award to join our Admission Communications team as a Content Creator. This position is hybrid, with required on-campus content creation, weekly in-person office time, mandatory staff meetings, and mandatory event coverage. This position is an important, integral part of Admission Communications and Welcome Center work, and essential to the success of Enrollment Management’s mission. Our Content Team members connect with and create content for prospective students, applicants, parents, and families on Instagram, ZeeMee, CampusESP, and other communications platforms. They also engage with our audiences at in-person and online events. Importantly, individual Content Creators work independently and collaboratively, contributing to a positive, enjoyable work environment. Applications without work samples will not be considered. Click the “Add” button on the “Websites” part of the “My Experience” section of the application to share a link to work samples in a portfolio of your relevant work. Your work sample portfolio can include website or email design, social media strategy and design, graphic design, media editing, and/or writing samples. Your portfolio can be a website, or a collection of work on a Google Doc or Google Slides. Candidates considered for this role will be asked to complete a creative assessment/video editing exercise. Finalists may be asked to provide references who can evaluate qualifications and overall fit for the position. Candidates will be contacted at their New School email addresses after a review of all applications submitted for the position. Successful candidates will: Meet all content deadlines, attend all content team meetings, and represent Admission Communications at in-person and online events Check in daily with Content Managers and Welcome Center professional administrators on our Slack workspace Be comfortable in front of the camera Create dynamic, authentic, approachable multi-media content for our communications platforms that provides an honest view and personal insight into student life Learn and understand our different audiences and platforms, including prospective students, applicants, parents/families, Instagram, ZeeMee, and CampusESP Enjoy connecting with and assisting our audiences, answering questions about student life and admission processes, and providing support Enjoy collaborating with professional staff and other student team members Contribute to a friendly, fun working environment Understand this is a position within a team where all team members are responsible to each other and rely on each other in different ways Be a positive, dependable, responsible resource for our team Complete other content creation and duties as assigned Manage their own time Commit to working 5-8 hours per week Understand this is job position is an important, integral part of Admission Communications and Welcome Center work, and essential to the success of Enrollment Management’s mission Minimum Qualifications:  Applicants must be currently enrolled in a degree program at The New School. Additional qualifications include: Federal Work Study Award Familiarity with and enthusiasm for social network sites, social media tools, and associated apps and technology Excellent writing and editing skills Strong video editing skills with desire to learn and troubleshoot challenges Impeccable attention to detail and aesthetics Sound judgment Positive attitude Excellent communicator Highly organized--enjoys organization Self-motivated  Enjoys assisting others and providing support Able to focus without being distracted by cell phones, personal social media, and competing responsibilities Familiarity with Instagram, Zeemee, and CampusESP Familiarity with Google applications including Sheets, Docs, Forms, and Slides Extensive experience with Canva video, photo, and design capabilities Hourly Rate:  $17 per hour  Work Mode:  Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Please upload all Required Applicant Documents to be considered:  Resume/CV Cover Letter ​Work Sample Federal Work Study Award We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Please upload your Work Sample and Federal Work Study Award letter if you have one to the “Cover Letter and Resume/CV” part of the “My Experience” section of the application. Responsibilities: Our Content Team members connect with and create content for prospective students, applicants, parents, and families on Instagram, ZeeMee, CampusESP, and other communications platforms. They also engage with our audiences at in-person and online events. Importantly, individual Content Creators work independently and collaboratively, contributing to a positive, enjoyable work environment. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/FWS-Front-Desk-Student-Assistant--Creative-Writing_JR109131": {
      "title": "FWS Front Desk Student Assistant, Creative Writing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-21\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe New School for Social Research (NSSR) is seeking a Front Office Student Assistant to work in the Creative Writing Program. The Creative Writing Program supports New School students, faculty, administration, and the New York City community. We are a busy office composed of staff members, faculty, graduate and undergraduate students.  The ideal candidate should be reliable and motivated to help with day-to-day operations in a busy, fast-paced office. Selected candidates will be familiarized with Creative Writing’s academic programs, course offerings, events, and curriculum. This position is expected to work on-site Mondays - Thursdays, up to 20 hours per week. Responsibilities include: Assist with in-person, telephone, and email inquiries  Direct calls/emails/requests for faculty, administrators, or other departments/programs  Assist with clerical support for Creative Writing staff and faculty as assigned, including data entry, filing, and other projects Other duties as assigned  Minimum Qualifications: Professional with excellent interpersonal, customer service and communication (written and verbal) skills Self-motivated, proactive and able to work independently and use good judgement Punctual and reliable Tech savvy with command of Google Drive, Zoom Ability to proofread and a keen eye for details   Friendly demeanor and a positive attitude Federal work study funding Preferred Qualifications: Students whose concentration is writing and literature are particularly encouraged to apply Proficiency in Adobe Suite a plus Prior experience in an administrative support position Workmode : This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17 - $19/hr   To apply, submit a cover letter and resume. Please include a scan of your FWS authorization. Be sure to include the days and times you’re available to work. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The student employee will serve as the front desk office assistant and assist with in-person, telephone and emial inquiries. Additionally, they will also suppor the Creative Writing staff and faculty with clerical and administrative funcitons such as date entry, filing and other projects assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Digital-Tools---Student-Assistant_JR109093": {
      "title": "Digital Tools - Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-20\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Digital Tools Shops (3D Lab, CNC Shops, Laser Lab, D12 Prototyping Shop) within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.  The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor  Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them  Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously  Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests  Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:  3D Lab Checking in and assisting with 3D printing consultations Operating 3D Printers Managing print submission waitlist Laser Lab Check in students for laser cutting consultations and orientations Review files for laser cutting and engraving Troubleshoot problems with student cuts D12 Prototyping All of the above CNC Shops Assisting with CNC consultations and orientations Assisting staff with loading and unloading materials Operations room cleaning Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery  Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness  Complete special projects as assigned  Participate in training sessions and staff meetings    MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users  Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail  Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends Work Mode : On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Korean-Language-Tutor_JR109116": {
      "title": "Korean Language Tutor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-20\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe language tutoring position is responsible to assist other students in one-on-one sessions of thirty minutes out-of-class. Tutors will answer questions about course content taught in class including grammar explanations, pronunciation of vowels sounds, diphthongs and consonant combinations that change according to the language. Tutors can review student’s homework and guide them through it assisting in a couple of sentences, help in student’s presentations preparation and written and oral assignments. Tutors will promote language courses after they accomplish the tutoring administrative, pedagogical and technological training sessions, they need to participate in cultural events organize in class according to their time availability. Tutors report to the faculty tutoring coordinator.   Pursuant to the CBA, Academic Student Workers (ASWs) shall not be required to perform a service outside of the listing of required duties as noted above. ASWs are encouraged to notify their supervisors and contact person as soon as practicable during the academic term if they anticipate their total work assignment will exceed the terms of the assignment. RESPONSIBILITIES Attend the administrative, pedagogical and technological tutoring training sessions for this objective they need to review all digital material sent previously for the training.  Perform class visits of no more than fifteen minutes in every language class offered and introduce themselves. Explain students how to sign in STARFISH for a tutoring session and the services provided including the extra-credit benefit when performing four tutoring sessions (some language classes require only three sessions, it varies). Review, explain and guide students about material taught in class such as grammar structure, pronunciation of vowels, diphthongs and consonant combination sounds. Guide students by reviewing a couple of homework exercises. Tutors can assist students in their written and oral assignments, mini-projects and exam material review. Assist students in the oral and written language used for class presentations providing corrections if needed and constructive feedback. Facilitate interactive speaking and listening exercises to build a real-world conversation skill. Contact language professors to participate in cultural class events allowing students to integrate in foreign language culture customs and social conventions. Set up their STARFISH office hours according to the university academic calendar and remove it during holidays. Tutoring is only offer at the university. Submit their online timesheets for payment through My-Day application.  Communicate immediately when a tutoring session has been cancelled. Contact the student and the program coordinator to inform about it. Inform the tutoring coordinator if students are not at tending sessions. Tutoring is a free service and if a student does not attend the session their tutoring privilege can be removed. Register all sessions performed in the tutoring excel track sheet provided every semester, indicating the day and time the session was performed and the student name too. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Native or near native fluency in the target language is desired, alongside strong English communication skills to clearly explain complex language rules. Experience as a prior tutor in high school or other institutions in the country or abroad is a plus. Strong and interpersonal skills such as patience, empathy, respect and tolerance to students diverse opinions. The tutoring job position is opened to students that are currently enrolled at the New School University only. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Applicants with advanced knowledge of literature, grammar, writing and speaking in the target language is a plus.  WORK MODE Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary, in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions.  #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : U$29.20 Diversity Statement:  We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Tutors are responsible for setting up their office hours- calendar in STARFISH and perform class visits in the language class for no more than fifteen minutes. They need to attend the tutoring appointments and cancel them when a problem/emergency occurs. Cancelation needs to be done forty-eight hours ahead letting the student and department know about it. Tutors need to submit their timesheets for payment every week (Fridays at noon).   We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/German-Language-Tutor_JR109117": {
      "title": "German Language Tutor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-20\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe language tutoring position is responsible to assist other students in one-on-one sessions of thirty minutes out-of-class. Tutors will answer questions about course content taught in class including grammar explanations, pronunciation of vowels sounds, diphthongs and consonant combinations that change according to the language. Tutors can review student’s homework and guide them through it assisting in a couple of sentences, help in student’s presentations preparation and written and oral assignments. Tutors will promote language courses after they accomplish the tutoring administrative, pedagogical and technological training sessions, they need to participate in cultural events organize in class according to their time availability. Tutors report to the faculty tutoring coordinator.   Pursuant to the CBA, Academic Student Workers (ASWs) shall not be required to perform a service outside of the listing of required duties as noted above. ASWs are encouraged to notify their supervisors and contact person as soon as practicable during the academic term if they anticipate their total work assignment will exceed the terms of the assignment. RESPONSIBILITIES Attend the administrative, pedagogical and technological tutoring training sessions for this objective they need to review all digital material sent previously for the training.  Perform class visits of no more than fifteen minutes in every language class offered and introduce themselves. Explain students how to sign in STARFISH for a tutoring session and the services provided including the extra-credit benefit when performing four tutoring sessions (some language classes require only three sessions, it varies). Review, explain and guide students about material taught in class such as grammar structure, pronunciation of vowels, diphthongs and consonant combination sounds. Guide students by reviewing a couple of homework exercise