 notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary, in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : U$29.20 Diversity Statement:  We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University   Responsibilities: Tutors are responsible for setting up their office hours- calendar in STARFISH and perform class visits in the language class for no more than fifteen minutes. They need to attend the tutoring appointments and cancel them when a problem/emergency occurs. Cancelation needs to be done forty-eight hours ahead letting the student and department know about it. Tutors need to submit their timesheets for payment every week (Fridays at noon). We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/ULC-Student-Assistant_JR109121": {
      "title": "ULC Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-20\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe University Learning Center (ULC) is dedicated to providing academic support to The New School’s diverse student population through one-on-one tutoring sessions, workshops, and more. Student assistants provide crucial customer service and admin support to ensure students can easily access our services. Responsibilities include , but are not limited to, assisting students, faculty and staff with requests (in-person, via email, online chat), including: Monitor the ULC inbox and respond to student, faculty and staff inquiries including: Scheduling assistance ULC policy clarification Troubleshoot minor tech issues, such as connectivity/audio issues, etc. Direct students to the appropriate New School office(s) Schedule appointments for students, including using Google calendar and ULC online scheduler (WCONLINE) Communicate with ULC staff and tutors via Slack and Gchat, including notifying tutors of timely student issues–lateness, tech problems, etc. and vice-versa Update spreadsheets to track confidential information, including: Student attendance Scheduling data Session summaries 6-8 hours a week, depending on availability Minimum Qualifications: Strong communication, attention to detail, and problem-solving skills Comfortable working in a fast-paced, collaborative environment Friendly demeanor and a positive attitude Experience working with Google Drive, especially Google Sheets (or Excel) Prior office experience preferred Experience working with ESL students a plus Preferred Qualifications: Federal Work Study Preferred Work Mode : Hybrid #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: ULC student assistants help students, faculty and staff to access tutoring services, including answering emails, scheduling appointments, communicating with staff and tutors via Gchat and Slack, and updating spreadsheets to track confidential information. ​ This role is hybrid, with both online and in-person shifts. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/ULC-Writing-Tutor_JR109122": {
      "title": "ULC Writing Tutor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-20\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe University Learning Center (ULC) is dedicated to providing academic support to The New School’s diverse student population through one-on-one tutoring sessions, workshops, and more. Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals.  Job Responsibilities: Tutor graduate and undergraduate students in a variety of writing projects and assist with general academic skill building such as oral presentations and time management (training provided). Writing assistance includes brainstorming topics, identifying the questions they hope to answer, developing outlines, formulating/strengthening arguments, helping revise drafts in various stages of development, helping students correctly cite sources and other plagiarism avoidance (including AI), cover letter and resume help, personal statements, as well as more in-depth writing projects such as theses, dissertations and research proposals.  Help students develop their independent writing and revision skills (and presentation and time management skills in those cases) and increase their awareness of additional university resources.  Create a professional and welcoming environment by modeling respect for students’ diverse cultures, language skills, and experiences. Complete administrative tasks, such as writing brief descriptions of each session and emailing a brief summary of daily attendance. Other opportunities include leading in-class workshops and/or specialized ULC workshops. Minimum Qualifications: Tutors must be currently enrolled in a relevant program at The New School and have at least one year of tutoring experience or teaching writing/English in a formal academic setting. Must have a 3.0 GPA or higher Excellent communication skills Demonstrated reliability and punctuality Able to commit to 6 (or more) tutoring hours per week Experience working with students with ADHD a plus Preferred Qualifications: Federal Work Study preferred Experience working with ESL and graduate-level students strongly preferred ​ Work Mode : Hybrid #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $29.20/hr through August 31 (new pay rate will be determined by next collective bargaining agreement) Special Instuction : Please send at least one faculty recommendation to learningcenter@newschool.edu . We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals. Tutors work one-on-one with undergraduate and graduate students of varying skill levels on general and graduate-level writing. Support during sessions can entail demonstrating the basics to assisting with complex problems and a range of issues in between. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/ULC-Math-Tutor_JR109125": {
      "title": "ULC Math Tutor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-20\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe University Learning Center (ULC) is dedicated to providing academic support to The New School’s diverse student population through one-on-one tutoring sessions, workshops, and more. Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals.  Job Responsibilities: Tutor undergraduate and graduate students in a variety of math and economics subjects, including Quantitative Reasoning, Statistics, R, Tableau, SPSS, Economics, Accounting, Excel, Math Merchandising, Math Tools, and Calculus.  Work with students of varying skill levels from demonstrating the basics to assisting with complex problems and a range of issues in between.  Create a professional and welcoming environment by modeling respect for students’ diverse cultures, language skills, and experiences. Complete administrative tasks, such as writing brief descriptions of each session and emailing a brief summary of daily attendance. Other opportunities include leading in-class workshops and/or specialized ULC workshops. Minimum Qualifications: Tutors must be currently enrolled in a graduate program at the university and have at least one year of tutoring experience or teaching math/economics in a formal academic setting.  Candidates must have taken Algebra, Pre-Calculus and Calculus, and Statistics. Potential tutors must have earned a grade of B+ or higher in the above mentioned mathematics courses relevant to the subject they will be tutoring.  Prior tutoring or teaching experience is preferred. Please send at least one faculty recommendation indicating your proficiency with specific software programs to learningcenter@newschool.edu . Must have a 3.0 GPA or higher Excellent communication skills Demonstrated reliability and punctuality Able to commit to 6 (or more) tutoring hours per week Experience working with students with ADHD a plus Preferred Qualification : Experience working with ESL and graduate-level students strongly preferred Federal Work Study preferred ​ Work Mode : Hybrid #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $29.20/hr through August 31 (new pay rate will be determined by next collective bargaining agreement) We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals. Tutors work one-on-one with undergraduate and graduate students of varying skill levels on math and economics. Support during sessions can entail demonstrating the basics to assisting with complex problems and a range of issues in between. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/ULC-Software-Computer-Programming-Tutor_JR109124": {
      "title": "ULC Software/Computer Programming Tutor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-17\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe University Learning Center (ULC) is dedicated to providing academic support to The New School’s diverse student population through one-on-one tutoring sessions, workshops, and more. Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals.  Job Responsibilities: Tutor undergraduate and graduate students in various software, including Photoshop, InDesign, Illustrator, Rhino, Figma, Blender, Premiere, After Effects, AutoCAD, Fusion, V-Ray, Revit, SketchUp, etc and/or in various coding languages, such as HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Python, Unity, Arduino and other languages you are fluent in.  Work with students of varying skill levels from demonstrating the basics to assisting with complex problems and a range of issues in between.  Create a professional and welcoming environment by modeling respect for students’ diverse cultures, language skills, and experiences. Complete administrative tasks, such as writing brief descriptions of each session and emailing a brief summary of daily attendance. Other opportunities include leading in-class workshops and/or specialized ULC workshops. Minimum Qualifications: Must have a high level of comfort and expertise in the Adobe CS and other software programs and/or must have strong knowledge of computer programming and a high level of expertise in HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Knowledge of additional programming languages is highly preferred, especially Python.  Must be currently enrolled in an undergraduate or graduate program at The New School.  Prior tutoring or teaching experience is preferred. Please send at least one faculty recommendation indicating your proficiency with specific software programs to learningcenter@newschool.edu . Must have a 3.0 GPA or higher Excellent communication skills Demonstrated reliability and punctuality Able to commit to 6 (or more) tutoring hours per week Experience working with ESL and graduate-level students strongly preferred Experience working with students with ADHD a plus Federal Work Study preferred Work Mode : Hybrid #LI-DNI Pay Rate : $29.20/hr through August 31 (new pay rate will be determined by next collective bargaining agreement) We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals. Tutors work one-on-one with undergraduate and graduate students of varying skill levels on software or computer programming. Support during sessions can entail demonstrating the basics to assisting with complex problems and a range of issues in between. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Event-Assistant---Office-of-Student-Engagement--OSE-_JR109004": {
      "title": "JR109004 Student Event Assistant - Office of Student Engagement (FWS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-17\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nReporting to the Assistant Director in the Office of Student Engagement (OSE), Student Event Assistants will support the goals of the office and the division of Student Success within The New School.  This position primarily focuses on the logistical support for OSE related Events/Initiatives as well as other Student-Led Events/Programs with occasional collaboration from Student Activities Board (SAB). The Office of Student Engagement (OSE) at The New School supports the university's educational mission by fostering opportunities for meaningful engagement and connections beyond the classroom. SLI encourages students to actively participate in the university community by joining or starting a student organization, engaging in recreational activities, or attending events and programs organized by the Student Activities Board.  RESPONSIBILITIES Provide onsite event management support during SLI and student-led events and programs. Participate in recurring student staff meetings and retreats. Assist in the marketing plan, communication,  and promotion of Student-Led programs through the Narwhal Nation and across major social media and communications platforms.  Maintain, organize, inventory, and catalog office supplies and Storage Spaces of the Office of Student Leadership and Involvement across Fanton Hall and the University Center. The  Student Event Assistant will also cross-train and work collaboratively across SLI Student workers and volunteers where needed. Photography: If applicable, provide documentation and photography support at campus events and programs. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS This Position requires a Federal Work-Study Award PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Ability to creatively problem solve, multi-task, and function in a collaborative work environment. Capacity to provide and receive constructive critique. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.  #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17-$19/hr  We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS  To be considered: Through the career portal, please provide your financial aid award letter, and a resume. You may contact Jose Gomez, Assistant Director, via email at gomezj2@newschool.edu for more information. Responsibilities: The Office of Student Engagement (OSE), Student Event Assistants will support the goals of the office and the division of Student Success within The New School.  This position primarily focuses on the logistical support for OSE related Events/Initiatives as well as other Student-Led Events/Programs with occasional collaboration from Student Activities Board (SAB).   We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Activity-Group-Leader--Office-of-Student-Engagement--OSE-_JR109003": {
      "title": "JR109003 Student Activity Group Leader- Office of Student Engagement (FWS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-17\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nReporting to the Assistant Director in the Office of Student Engagement (OSE), Student Activity Group Leaders will support the goals of the office and the division of Student Success within The New School.  This position primarily focuses on the Student-Led Events and Programs for the Student Activities Board (SAB) with collaborative support from Student Event Assistants. The Office of Student Engagement (OSE) at The New School supports the university's educational mission by fostering opportunities for meaningful engagement and connections beyond the classroom. OSE encourages students to actively participate in the university community by joining or starting a student organization, engaging in recreational activities, or attending events and programs organized by the Student Activities Board, which is the New School's programming board, responsible for developing and implementing small/large scale events such as open mic nights, silent disco, and mixers to bring the New School's community together.  RESPONSIBILITIES Serve as a student officer on the Student Activities Board (SAB), which hosts student-led events throughout the Academic Year (Fall/Spring)for on and off campus. Reply to student inquiries related to student-led programming. Staff SLI-TE events (when needed), meetings, and office hours for SAB in person. Prepare for events and hold virtual office hours remotely. Photography: If applicable, provide documentation and photography support at campus events and programs. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled New School student who qualifies for Federal Work Study. Demonstrated commitment to community building and teamwork. Reliable, organized, and communicative. Able to manage a hybrid schedule and confirm weekly changes for event staffing. Able to commit to weekly meetings on Thursdays from 4-5 (in person) OR Fridays 4-5 (on zoom) PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience working in an office or community building environment is preferred. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Houry Rate: $17-$19/hr  We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. ​ SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS  To be considered: Through the career portal, please provide your financial aid award letter, and a resume. You may contact Jose Gomez, Assistant Director, via email at gomezj2@newschool.edu for more information. Responsibilities: The Office of Student Engagement (OSE), Student Activity Group Leaders will support the goals of the office and the division of Student Success within The New School.  This position primarily focuses on the Student-Led Events and Programs for the Student Activities Board (SAB) with collaborative support from Student Event Assistants. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Digital-Tools---Student-Assistant-FWS_JR109097": {
      "title": "Digital Tools - Student Assistant FWS",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-17\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Digital Tools Shops (3D Lab, CNC Shops, Laser Lab, D12 Prototyping Shop) within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.  The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.  Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.  Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor  Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them  Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously  Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests  Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:  3D Lab Checking in and assisting with 3D printing consultations Operating 3D Printers Managing print submission waitlist Laser Lab Check in students for laser cutting consultations and orientations Review files for laser cutting and engraving Troubleshoot problems with student cuts D12 Prototyping All of the above CNC Shops Assisting with CNC consultations and orientations Assisting staff with loading and unloading materials Operations room cleaning Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery  Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness  Complete special projects as assigned  Participate in training sessions and staff meetings  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users  Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail  Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends HOURLY RATE : $17.00/hour WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.  We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Tutor_JR109092": {
      "title": "Tutor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-14\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nThe language tutoring position is responsible to assist other students in one-on-one sessions of thirty minutes out-of-class. Tutors will answer questions about course content taught in class including grammar explanations, pronunciation of vowels sounds, diphthongs and consonant combinations that change according to the language. Tutors can review student’s homework and guide them through it assisting in a couple of sentences, help in student’s presentations preparation and written and oral assignments. Tutors will promote language courses after they accomplish the tutoring administrative, pedagogical and technological training sessions, they need to participate in cultural events organize in class according to their time availability. Tutors report to the faculty tutoring coordinator. Pursuant to the CBA, Academic Student Workers (ASWs) shall not be required to perform a service outside of the listing of required duties as noted above. ASWs are encouraged to notify their supervisors and contact person as soon as practicable during the academic term if they anticipate their total work assignment will exceed the terms of the assignment. RESPONSIBILITIES Attend the administrative, pedagogical and technological tutoring training sessions for this objective they need to review all digital material sent previously for the training. Perform class visits of no more than fifteen minutes in every language class offered and introduce themselves. Explain students how to sign in STARFISH for a tutoring session and the services provided including the extra-credit benefit when performing four tutoring sessions (some language classes require only three sessions, it varies). Review, explain and guide students about material taught in class such as grammar structure, pronunciation of vowels, diphthongs and consonant combination sounds. Guide students by reviewing a couple of homework exercises. Tutors can assist students in their written and oral assignments, mini-projects and exam material review. Assist students in the oral and written language used for class presentations providing corrections if needed and constructive feedback. Facilitate interactive speaking and listening exercises to build a real-world conversation skill. Contact language professors to participate in cultural class events allowing students to integrate in foreign language culture customs and social conventions. Set up their STARFISH office hours according to the university academic calendar and remove it during holidays. Tutoring is only offer at the university. Submit their online timesheets for payment through My-Day application. Communicate immediately when a tutoring session has been cancelled. Contact the student and the program coordinator to inform about it. Inform the tutoring coordinator if students are not at tending sessions. Tutoring is a free service and if a student does not attend the session their tutoring privilege can be removed. Register all sessions performed in the tutoring excel track sheet provided every semester, indicating the day and time the session was performed and the student name too MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Native or near native fluency in the target language is desired, alongside strong English communication skills to clearly explain complex language rules. Experience as a prior tutor in high school or other institutions in the country or abroad is a plus. Strong and interpersonal skills such as patience, empathy, respect and tolerance to students diverse opinions. The tutoring job position is opened to students that are currently enrolled at the New School University only. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Applicants with advanced knowledge of literature, grammar, writing and speaking in the target language is a plus. WORK MODE: Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary, in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $29.20 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Tutors are responsible for setting up their office hours- calendar in STARFISH and perform class visits in the language class for no more than fifteen minutes. They need to attend the tutoring appointments and cancel them when a problem/emergency occurs. Cancelation needs to be done forty-eight hours ahead letting the student and department know about it. Tutors need to submit their timesheets for payment every week (Fridays at noon). We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
    "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Stitcher_JR109073": {
      "title": "Temporary Overhire Stitcher",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-10\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School  At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Stitcher to join the Performance Operations – Costume Department Staff. The Costume Department is responsible for the creation and delivery of garments as designated and discussed by the Costume Designer, Design Team, and Production Staff.  The Stitcher is a well-organized, self-disciplined individual who takes direction well and is self-sufficient when immediate direction is unavailable. The Stitcher reports to the Costume Shop Manager. The Stitcher coordinates with the design team(s) and shop management to ensure the timely and safe production of any garments requiring construction and/or alterations. Currently, the department covers the productions for the School of Drama, the Mannes Operas, and Concerts and Events for the School of Jazz.  This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Costumes department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Provide support during garment fittings for upcoming productions Attend all shifts in person as scheduled by shop management Hand stitch and machine stitch garments, utilizing various machinery as needed Follow all fitting notes documents, paying close attention to shop management’s instructions  Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS 1 or more years of experience in patternmaking 3 years of professional experience in costume construction, stitching, and alterations Knowledge of current techniques, materials, and repertoire relevant for theatrical costuming Ability to follow guidance from leaders throughout the production process PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor of Arts or higher, or equivalent professional experience. Professional experience in one or more of the following areas: tailoring, historical costuming, couture stitching, draping Experience in an academic environment/working with students or young professionals. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Some nights and weekends may be required. Costume Shop hours are 10:00am-5:00pm Monday through Friday. Schedule adjustments may be made due to external venue time constraints when offsite. SALARY RANGE $27.00/hour  WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that’s reshaping the future of higher education. You’ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"
    },
